nferences cannot be made. It therefore does not support the claim that larger spatial scales directly cause higher BMI via reduced walking/greater transport reliance."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "Larger spatial scales, such as living in bigger geographic areas, directly cause an increase in individuals' body mass index (BMI) because people tend to walk less and rely more on transportation. As the spatial scale expands, physical activity decreases, leading to higher BMI levels.", 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': "Larger spatial scales, such as living in bigger geographic areas, directly cause an increase in individuals' body mass index (BMI) because people tend to walk less and rely more on transportation. As the spatial scale expands, physical activity decreases, leading to higher BMI levels.", 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

