   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Spatial Scale does not cause Sleep disturbance (PSQI), nor does Sleep disturbance (PSQI) cause Spatial Scale. There is no direct or plausible causal mechanism linking the concept of spatial scale to sleep disturbance. No evidence or theoretical basis supports a direct causal relationship between these variables. Therefore, no causal link should be included in the CLD.', 'session_id': 'bed1b631-a5f2-4763-b6fe-7861a08a2e02', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7268445/', 'https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-1-4419-9893-4_67', 'https://www.sleep.pitt.edu/psqi', 'https://www.med.upenn.edu/cbti/assets/user-content/documents/Pittsburgh Sleep Quality Index (PSQI).pdf', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11973415/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7268445/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review summarizes observational evidence that less-dense/neighborhoods with more natural/open space (green space, water, lower urban density) are associated with longer/ better sleep and fewer sleep problems, and proposes stress-reduction as a plausible mechanism. However, the evidence is correlational, sometimes inconsistent, and the paper cautions against causal claims; it does not specifically and consistently tie “larger spatial scale” (e.g., bigger indoor living/sleeping area) to reductions in PSQI-measured sleep disturbances nor demonstrate a direct causal effect. Thus the general idea (more open/less-dense environments relate to better sleep) is supported, but the stronger claim of a direct causal effect on PSQI sleep disturbances is not fully supported by the citations alone."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11973415/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited articl