edict or causally produce changes in PSS (nor that reducing hassles reduces PSS), so it does not provide direct evidence for the claimed causal relationship. Thus the claim of a direct, well‑established causal effect of daily hassles on PSS is not supported by this citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10025564/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review frames stressors (including everyday/daily stressors) as stimuli that elicit a stress response and reports numerous studies linking self-reported daily stressors and perceived stress (PSS) to biological and cognitive outcomes. Thus it supports a positive association and the conceptual view that exposure to stressors raises perceived stress. However, the review also emphasizes heterogeneity, individual appraisal, resilience, moderators (age, sex, genetics) and methodological limitations (many correlational/group-level studies), and does not provide unambiguous evidence of a simple direct causal pathway nor that the effect is \\"not fully mediated\\" by other variables. It therefore supports the positive association but not the stronger claim of an unmediated direct causal relationship or clear evidence that reducing hassles will necessarily reduce PSS in all cases."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.researchgate.net/publication/232512600_Contrasting_the_Hassles_Scale_and_the_Perceived_Stress_Scale", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The only cited source is inaccessible (ResearchGate page returning an access/connection error), so no evidence from the provided citation supports the claimed causal relationship between daily hassles and perceived stress. Because the citation content cannot be examined, the explanation is not corroborated by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.researchgate.net/publication/262197133_Stress_From_Daily_Hassles_in_Couples_Its_Effects_on_Intradyadic_Stress_Relationship_Satisfaction_and_Physical_and_Psychological_Well-Being", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The only cited source is inaccessible (ResearchGate page returning an access/connection error) and contains no usable content or evidence. Because the provided citation does not present any findings or data, it cannot support the claimed causal relationship between daily hassles and perceived stress."}], "aggregate_score": 0.2, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Daily hassles (HS) cause perceived stress (PSS) to increase in a direct causal relationship. Experiencing more frequent or intense daily hassles directly raises perceived stress, while reducing daily hassles decreases perceived stress. This relationship is well-established in stress research and does not appear to be fully mediated by other variables in the provided list.', 'aggregate_score': 0.2, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.3436458753589369, 'mean_token_prob': 0.822747149287169, 'min_prob': 0.06636626719679288, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0037225967805226854, 'prob_variance': 0.06725290543279293, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.092921761897603, 'avg_nll': 0.2953867217865004}

