   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Perceived stress (PSS) does not cause daily hassles (HS) to change. Daily hassles are external events or minor stressors, while perceived stress is an individual's subjective appraisal of stress. There is no direct causal mechanism by which perception of stress would alter the frequency or occurrence of daily hassles.", 'session_id': 'bed1b631-a5f2-4763-b6fe-7861a08a2e02', 'citations': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Psychological_stress', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6345505/', 'https://openbooks.library.baylor.edu/psychscience/chapter/stress-health-wellness/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Psychological_stress", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Wikipedia article describes daily hassles as a category of stressors and explains that stress influences perception, behavior, and social interactions (e.g., irritability, communication difficulties) that could plausibly increase minor problems noticed or created. However, the source does not provide direct evidence or explicit claims that higher perceived stress causally increases the number of daily hassles or that stress actively generates new hassles. It more strongly treats hassles as stressors and emphasizes perception/modulation of impact rather than a demonstrated causal direction from perceived stress → more hassles."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6345505/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review presents a conceptual model and empirical examples showing that chronic stress and higher perceived stress are associated with more frequent or more severe daily stressors (e.g., caregiver samples showing higher PSS and more daily “stressor days”), and it discusses how habitual appraisals/mental filters can make people appraise and notice more daily hassles. However, the paper primarily offers correlational evidence and theoretical mechanisms (appraisal, context, bidirectional influences) rather than direct causal evidence that higher perceived stress (PSS) actively generates new daily hassles. It supports an association and plausible pathways, but not the strong unidirectional causal claim in the explanation.\\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://openbooks.library.baylor.edu/psychscience/chapter/stress-health-wellness/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The chapter clearly links daily hassles and perceived stress (daily hassles are described as common stressors and are strongly associated with health and mood) and emphasizes that appraisal/perception shapes whether events are experienced as stressful. However, the text treats hassles mainly as causes of stress (and discusses how perception determines whether events are appraised as stressors) rather than presenting evidence that higher perceived stress directly produces or increases the objective number of daily hassles. It implies perceptual/ appraisal effects (stressed people may appraise events as more stressful) but does not provide direct empirical support for the claim that increasing perceived stress actively generates more daily hassles.\\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "Higher levels of perceived stress directly cause an increase in the number of daily hassles a person experiences, as stress can make individuals more prone to encountering and noticing minor problems throughout the day. As stress rises, it actively generates new daily hassles by impacting a person's environment and interactions.", 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': "Higher levels of perceived stress directly cause an increase in the number of daily hassles a person experiences, as stress can make individuals more prone to encountering and noticing minor problems throughout the day. As stress rises, it actively generates new daily hassles by impacting a person's environment and interactions.", 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

