33. Perceived stress (PSS)-[NEGATIVE]->Smoking (SMQ)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Perceived stress (PSS) causes smoking (SMQ) in a direct causal relationship. Higher levels of perceived stress can lead individuals to increase smoking as a coping mechanism, while lower stress tends to reduce the urge to smoke. This effect is widely observed in behavioral health research and does not require mediation by other variables in the provided list.', 'session_id': 'bed1b631-a5f2-4763-b6fe-7861a08a2e02', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9859085/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5531612/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4558262/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3776130/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4876877/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9859085/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study reports that higher perceived stress is positively correlated with stronger smoking urges and, via those urges, is indirectly associated with lower abstinence (i.e., greater likelihood of continued smoking). The mediation results therefore indicate stress is linked to more — not less — smoking behavior. The paper does not provide evidence that increased stress leads people to avoid smoking for health or control reasons."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5531612/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article reports that higher perceived stress is associated with stronger negative-reinforcement smoking expectancies and with greater smoking-related barriers/problems and experiential avoidance — i.e., links to continued or more problematic smoking and poorer cessation outcomes. It provides no evidence that higher perceived stress leads people to avoid smoking or become more health-conscious and reduce smoking. The direction claimed (stress → decreased smoking) contradicts the study\'s findings (stress → factors that maintain/increase smoking likelihood)."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4558262/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article does not support the claim that higher perceived stress leads people to avoid smoking or become more health-conscious and reduce smoking. Rather, the paper and its literature review describe stress as a risk factor for smoking and smokers often use cigarettes to relieve stress; its primary findings show a positive association between perceived stress and nicotine withdrawal, and only a mixed/limited inverse association with cigarettes-per-day (observed in men and discussed as potentially due to reverse causation). It does not provide evidence that increased stress causes reduced smoking for health reasons."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3776130/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited MIDUS prospective study finds that higher psychosocial stress is generally associated with greater odds of persistent smoking and failure to quit; the authors conclude chronically high stress increases risk of continued smoking. While a few stress domains showed mixed results (e.g., some associations between financial/work stress and quitting), the overall pattern contradicts the proposed negative relationship (higher stress → less smoking). The citation does not support the explanation that increased perceived stress leads people to avoid or reduce smoking to cope or become more health-conscious.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4876877/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article reports that perceived stress is positively associated with smoking-related outcomes (higher stress linked to greater nicotine dependence, more barriers to cessation, and more problems during quit attempts) and that smoking-specific experiential avoidance helps explain why stressed smokers may use smoking to cope. It does not provide evidence that higher perceived stress leads people to avoid smoking or reduce smoking; in fact it supports the opposite direction. Therefore the explanation is not supported by the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Higher perceived stress might lead individuals to avoid smoking as a coping mechanism, believing that abstaining will help them feel calmer and more in control. As stress increases, people could become more health-conscious and thus reduce their smoking behavior to prevent additional stress-related health issues.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Higher perceived stress might lead individuals to avoid smoking as a coping mechanism, believing that abstaining will help them feel calmer and more in control. As stress increases, people could become more health-conscious and thus reduce their smoking behavior to prevent additional stress-related health issues.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

