   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Rumination (CERQ-R) causes proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6) to increase through sustained psychological stress, which activates physiological stress pathways and elevates inflammatory biomarkers. Increased rumination prolongs stress responses, leading to higher levels of TNF-alpha and IL-6. Conversely, reducing rumination would decrease these proinflammatory processes. This causal link is supported by psychoneuroimmunology research on stress and inflammation.', 'session_id': 'bed1b631-a5f2-4763-b6fe-7861a08a2e02', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8957598/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10425218/', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-025-85514-0', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0149763422000094', 'https://www.researchgate.net/publication/38306184_The_Cognitive_Emotion_Regulation_Questionnaire'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8957598/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited scoping review summarizes 14 studies and finds mixed but overall more evidence linking rumination with increased or unchanged inflammation—especially in experimental manipulations (increases in CRP or salivary IL-1β and some positive associations with IL-6/CRP). Negative associations are rare, often non‑significant, conditional (e.g., gender interactions), or pertain to \\"reflection\\" (a non‑ruminative subtype) rather than to rumination broadly. The review also highlights heterogeneous measures (not specifically CERQ‑R) and inconsistent findings. It does not support a general claim that rumination reduces TNF‑α and IL‑6.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10425218/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited systematic review summarizes 38 studies and reports that perseverative cognition/rumination is generally associated with higher or unchanged inflammatory markers—not lower. Acute experimental studies in the review found rumination produced steeper increases in IL-6 and CRP (and IL-1β in some paradigms); observational studies mostly showed positive or null associations, with only a small number of exceptions (one study found an unexpected inverse relation for repetitive thought and IL-6; another found \\"reflection\\" associated with lower IL-6, which is conceptually distinct from maladaptive rumination). Overall the evidence in the cited source contradicts, rather than supports, the claim that rumination (CERQ-R) reduces TNF-α and IL-6."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-025-85514-0", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article examines associations between peripheral IL-6 and TNF-α and resting-state network connectivity in depression versus controls; it does not test rumination measured by the CERQ-R or show that rumination predicts lower IL-6 or TNF-α. The paper actually links DMN connectivity to depressive symptoms (including literature on rumination) and reports higher IL-6 in depressed participants, but provides no evidence that repeated cognitive processing/rumination lowers proinflammatory markers. Therefore the provided source does not support the claimed negative relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0149763422000094", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The sole cited source returns an error message and contains no accessible content or evidence about rumination (CERQ-R) and inflammatory markers (TNF-α, IL-6). Therefore there is nothing in the provided citation to support the claim that rumination reduces proinflammatory processes."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.researchgate.net/publication/38306184_The_Cognitive_Emotion_Regulation_Questionnaire", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The only citation is an inaccessible ResearchGate page that provides no content or empirical data linking CERQ-R rumination scores to TNF‑alpha or IL‑6. It does not report results or evidence that rumination reduces proinflammatory markers; therefore the claim is not supported by the provided source.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Rumination, as measured by the CERQ-R, is actually believed to reduce proinflammatory processes like TNF-alpha and IL-6 because repeated cognitive processing of emotions helps individuals to mentally resolve stressors, thereby dampening the physiological stress response. This mental processing acts as a buffer, leading to lower levels of inflammatory biomarkers rather than increasing them.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Rumination, as measured by the CERQ-R, is actually believed to reduce proinflammatory processes like TNF-alpha and IL-6 because repeated cognitive processing of emotions helps individuals to mentally resolve stressors, thereby dampening the physiological stress response. This mental processing acts as a buffer, leading to lower levels of inflammatory biomarkers rather than increasing them.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

