   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Rumination (CERQ-R) causes sleep disturbance (PSQI) in a direct causal relationship. Increased rumination leads to persistent and repetitive negative thoughts, which can interfere with the ability to fall asleep or maintain restful sleep. Conversely, reducing rumination can decrease cognitive arousal and improve sleep quality. This relationship is direct and not mediated by other variables in the CLD.', 'session_id': 'bed1b631-a5f2-4763-b6fe-7861a08a2e02', 'prob_std': 0.24589608696453907, 'citations': ['https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/B9780124171886000104', 'https://psycnet.apa.org/record/2015-05075-010', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6545794/', 'https://ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC9396259', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC5877474/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7928832170251238, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/B9780124171886000104", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source is an error message (ScienceDirect Cloudflare error) and contains no usable content or evidence relating rumination (CERQ-R) to sleep disturbance (PSQI). Therefore there is nothing in the provided citation to support the causal explanation or association described."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6545794/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article and referenced studies report a positive association between rumination (RRS/CERQ-R–type measures) and worse sleep (PSQI) and even model rumination as a mediator predicting poorer sleep; experimental work cited (e.g., Zoccola et al.) shows rumination predicting longer sleep-onset latency. However, the provided explanation argues the causal path runs from sleep disturbance → more nighttime wakefulness → increased rumination as a time‑filler. The citations do not provide evidence for that reverse causal mechanism (and the main study is cross-sectional and cautions against inferring causal direction). Therefore the positive association is supported but the specific causal explanation is not. \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC9396259", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article provides evidence that (especially) negative rumination and dampening are positively associated with greater insomnia symptoms (measured with the ISI), supporting a positive link between ruminative thinking and sleep disturbance. However, the paper uses different instruments (Ruminative Response Scale and RPA; sleep measured by ISI) rather than the exact measures named in the claim (CERQ-R and PSQI). Crucially, the specific causal mechanism asserted in the explanation — that disrupted sleep gives people more awake time at night which they use to ruminate (i.e., wakefulness → rumination → reinforced poor sleep perception) — is not demonstrated or described in the cited study; the article emphasizes rumination increasing arousal and interfering with sleep and only notes the possibility of reciprocal relationships, but does not provide evidence for the “passing time during wakefulness” mechanism. Therefore the claim is supported in part (there is a positive association) but not for the precise measures or the specific proposed mechanism."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC5877474/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study (Slavish et al., 2018) does not support the proposed causal pathway. In their daily-diary multilevel analyses, daily rumination did not independently predict that night’s sleep quality or difficulty falling asleep (whereas daily negative affect did). The paper also notes the data are correlational and does not provide evidence that disrupted sleep increases rumination by giving people more awake time, nor that rumination during wakefulness subsequently reinforces perceived poor sleep. Thus the specific directional mechanism in the explanation is not supported by the provided citation. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Rumination (CERQ-R) increases sleep disturbance (PSQI) because individuals who experience frequent sleep disturbances, such as difficulties falling or staying asleep, tend to have more time awake at night, which subsequently leads them to engage in rumination as a way to pass the time. The increased engagement in rumination during these wakeful periods reinforces the perception of poor sleep quality, thereby establishing a positive association between Rumination (CERQ-R) and Sleep disturbance (PSQI) that is primarily driven by the effects of disrupted sleep patterns on cognitive activity.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Rumination (CERQ-R) increases sleep disturbance (PSQI) because individuals who experience frequent sleep disturbances, such as difficulties falling or staying asleep, tend to have more time awake at night, which subsequently leads them to engage in rumination as a way to pass the time. The increased engagement in rumination during these wakeful periods reinforces the perception of poor sleep quality, thereby establishing a positive association between Rumination (CERQ-R) and Sleep disturbance (PSQI) that is primarily driven by the effects of disrupted sleep patterns on cognitive activity.', 'perplexity': 1.294911688131078, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8420707796386855, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.05265354549610537, 'token_prob_slope': -0.003583326637652078, 'prob_variance': 0.06046