60. Mindfulness (FFMQ)-[POSITIVE]->Rumination (CERQ-R)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Mindfulness (FFMQ) causes Rumination (CERQ-R) to decrease in a direct causal relationship. Increased mindfulness promotes present-moment awareness and acceptance, which directly reduces the tendency to engage in repetitive, negative thought patterns characteristic of rumination. Conversely, decreased mindfulness leads to greater rumination. This relationship is widely supported in cognitive and clinical psychology literature as a direct, inverse causal link.', 'session_id': 'bed1b631-a5f2-4763-b6fe-7861a08a2e02', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3843486/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3679190/', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31414836/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3843486/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article (Feldman et al., 2013) does not provide evidence that overall mindfulness (FFMQ) increases rumination. It uses RSQ-derived rumination facets (brooding, reflection), not the CERQ-R, and its results do not report a general positive association between FFMQ mindfulness and rumination. Instead, the paper distinguishes adaptive reflection from maladaptive brooding and finds that a mindfulness facet (acting with awareness) and reflection predicted greater distress-tolerance; it does not show that mindfulness increases repetitive, maladaptive rumination. Because the measure of rumination differs and the findings do not support the claimed positive relationship, the explanation is not supported by the citation. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3679190/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review and the studies it summarizes report that higher mindfulness (including increases on the FFMQ) is associated with lower rumination and that mindfulness interventions reduce rumination; several trials and mediation analyses are cited showing mindfulness decreases, not increases, in repetitive negative thinking. The provided sources do not present evidence that mindfulness practice increases rumination or that trait mindfulness is positively associated with rumination; they in fact report the opposite."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31414836/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article reports that the mindfulness facet nonjudgmental acceptance was associated with less concurrent rumination, and present-moment attention was linked to more reflection (not rumination). It also found mindfulness reduced the impact of rumination on affect. These findings contradict the claim that mindfulness (FFMQ) increases rumination; the source does not provide evidence of a positive association between mindfulness and rumination."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Recent theories suggest that practicing mindfulness, as measured by the FFMQ, can actually increase rumination because heightened self-awareness leads individuals to dwell more on their thoughts and feelings. This increased focus on internal experiences may inadvertently encourage repetitive thinking patterns, resulting in a positive association between mindfulness and rumination.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Recent theories suggest that practicing mindfulness, as measured by the FFMQ, can actually increase rumination because heightened self-awareness leads individuals to dwell more on their thoughts and feelings. This increased focus on internal experiences may inadvertently encourage repetitive thinking patterns, resulting in a positive association between mindfulness and rumination.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

