63. Mindfulness (FFMQ)-[POSITIVE]->Perceived stress (PSS)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Mindfulness (FFMQ) causes perceived stress (PSS) to change in an inverse causal relationship. Increasing mindfulness directly reduces perceived stress by enhancing present-moment awareness and emotional regulation. Conversely, decreasing mindfulness directly increases perceived stress. This relationship is supported by well-established psychological mechanisms and does not appear to be mediated by other variables present in the CLD.', 'session_id': 'bed1b631-a5f2-4763-b6fe-7861a08a2e02', 'citations': ['https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2024.1272720/full', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3679190/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2024.1498458/full'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2024.1272720/full", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study reports small but significant associations in the direction the explanation claims for some FFMQ facets: higher Actaware use predicted slightly higher next-day perceived stress, and the trait-like Describe facet correlated positively with average daily stress. The authors explicitly note mindfulness can sometimes be associated with increased stress (and discuss awareness-related explanations). However the effects are facet-specific (Nonreact predicted lower stress), small in magnitude, and the paper emphasizes measurement and temporal limitations. The citation therefore supports the notion that some aspects of self-reported mindfulness may heighten perceived stress, but does not support a general, uniform effect of overall mindfulness increasing PSS over time."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2024.1498458/full", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study overall reports that mindfulness facets (FFMQ) are negatively associated with perceived stress and that mindfulness-acceptance predicts lower PSS scores; quantitatively there is no evidence that greater mindfulness increases perceived stress. However, the qualitative data mention one nuance — some participants said that attention alone (without acceptance) could heighten awareness of stressors and sometimes increase stress. Thus the claim that mindfulness in general increases perceived stress is not supported by the quantitative evidence, though a limited qualitative caveat about attention-only effects partially aligns with the explanation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3679190/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review overwhelmingly reports that higher mindfulness (including FFMQ-assessed changes) and mindfulness training reduce perceived stress and psychological distress. Multiple RCTs and mediation analyses cited (e.g., MBSR trials, Bränström et al., Nyklíček & Kuijpers, Shapiro et al., Carmody & Baer) found decreases in perceived stress and that increases in FFMQ/trait mindfulness mediated reductions in perceived stress. The review does not present evidence that increased mindfulness heightens perceived stress; rather it documents the opposite and notes some measurement caveats but no empirical support for the proposed \\"awareness -> amplified perceived stress\\" effect.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.3333333333333333, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Practicing mindfulness as measured by the FFMQ can lead individuals to become more aware of their stressors, which may actually heighten their perception of stress as captured by the PSS. Increased mindfulness encourages constant monitoring of internal states, potentially amplifying perceived stress levels over time.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.3333333333333333, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Practicing mindfulness as measured by the FFMQ can lead individuals to become more aware of their stressors, which may actually heighten their perception of stress as captured by the PSS. Increased mindfulness encourages constant monitoring of internal states, potentially amplifying perceived stress levels over time.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

