on regulation, body awareness, and working mindfully with cravings. It does not provide evidence that higher trait mindfulness (e.g., FFMQ scores) increases sensitivity to cigarette taste or smell, nor that such heightened sensory perception reduces smoking because smoking becomes less pleasurable. Moreover, the review found no clear, consistent benefit of mindfulness interventions on quit rates (low/very low certainty). Thus the specific sensory‑awareness → reduced pleasure → lower smoking causal pathway in the explanation is not supported by the provided citation(s)."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3909670/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited paper provides evidence of a negative association between dispositional mindfulness (FFMQ) and substance dependence severity in a clinical sample and discusses mechanisms like reduced craving and decoupling of urges from behavior. However, it is cross-sectional (so causality is not established), focuses on general substance dependence rather than smoking specifically, and does not provide any evidence that heightened sensory awareness increases sensitivity to the taste or smell of cigarettes or that such sensory changes make smoking less pleasurable and thus reduce smoking. The specific sensory mechanism claimed is not supported by the citations."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://link.springer.com/rwe/10.1007/978-3-031-47219-0_15", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source describes the FFMQ instrument and its psychometric properties; it does not provide evidence that higher FFMQ scores cause reduced smoking, nor does it support the proposed sensory-perception mechanism (reduced pleasure from taste/smell). No empirical data or causal link to smoking behavior is presented in the provided citation excerpt.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 