 The cited chapter and referenced studies review evidence that mindfulness and related meditation trainings tend to increase compassion and prosocial behavior (including economic game measures and real-world helping), and report correlations between dispositional mindfulness and empathic/prosocial outcomes. While the chapter raises theoretical concerns that secular mindfulness without ethical framing might in principle encourage self-focused motives, it does not provide empirical evidence that higher mindfulness (e.g., as measured by FFMQ) reduces prosocial behavior in Public Goods Game (PGG) or causes withdrawal/self-absorption leading to less helping. Thus the citations do not support the asserted negative relationship. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Engaging in mindfulness practices, as measured by the FFMQ, can lead individuals to become overly self-absorbed, causing them to withdraw from group activities and reduce their willingness to help others in settings like the Public Goods Game (PGG). As a result, heightened mindfulness may actually decrease prosocial behavior by shifting focus away from collective welfare to personal introspection.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Engaging in mindfulness practices, as measured by the FFMQ, can lead individuals to become overly self-absorbed, causing them to withdraw from group activities and reduce their willingness to help others in settings like the Public Goods Game (PGG). As a result, heightened mindfulness may actually decrease prosocial behavior by shifting focus away from collective welfare to personal introspection.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

