   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Perceived social support (MSPSS) causes rumination (CERQ-R) to change in an inverse relationship. Increasing perceived social support directly reduces the tendency for rumination by providing emotional resources and alternative coping strategies. Conversely, decreased social support can increase rumination as individuals lack external support to buffer negative thoughts. This relationship is direct and not fully mediated by other variables in the system.', 'session_id': 'bed1b631-a5f2-4763-b6fe-7861a08a2e02', 'prob_std': 0.2801728457533546, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10915202/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12076526/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2024.1330720/full', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3219878/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7488358030961512, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10915202/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article examines MSPSS, perceived stress (PSS), and outcomes like affect, anxiety, and depression, finding that greater perceived social support is associated with lower perceived stress and better mental-health outcomes via appraisal/coping mechanisms. It does not measure rumination (CERQ-R) or repetitive negative thinking, nor does it claim that social support reduces exposure to stressful events; in fact the paper frames support as affecting stress appraisal/coping rather than preventing stressors. Therefore the specific claim that perceived social support causally reduces rumination by preventing exposure to stressors (and not via cognitive/emotional processes) is not supported by the citation.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12076526/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Jud