   Properties: {'session_id': 'bed1b631-a5f2-4763-b6fe-7861a08a2e02', 'prob_std': 0.26334814940578516, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4751868/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6991989/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK99236/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8358441/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7803001573867167, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4751868/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper (Burns et al., 2014) reviews mixed findings but provides empirical evidence that partner-related perceived support (PIQ factors) predicted smoking abstinence at 21 days and, to a lesser extent, 3 months. While the authors discuss models in which social support may operate via stress buffering and note inconsistent/mediated pathways, they do not demonstrate that perceived social support has no direct effect; in fact some PIQ subscales showed direct predictive associations with short-term cessation. The citation therefore does not support asserting a complete absence of a direct relationship (or that any effect is always mediated by stress/depression).  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6991989/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Waring et al. EMA study found perceived support/pressure to quit was not related to daily smoking in their low-SES sample, which supports the idea that social support did not have a direct effect in that study. However, the same paper cites prior studies (e.g., Lawhon, Mermelstein, Lüscher, Scholz) reporting direct associations between social support and reduced smoking, so the literature in the cited source is mixed. The provided citation does not present direct evidence that any effect of social support is mediated specifically by stress or depressive symptoms, so the mediation claim is not supported by these citations. Overall, the evidence in the cited material partially supports omitting a direct link in some contexts but does not conclusively support the general claim that perceived social support never directly affects smoking or that its influence is mediated by stress/depression."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8358441/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited MSPSS psychometric paper reports reliability/validity and correlations of perceived social support with stress, coping, family functioning and caregiving feelings, but it contains no data or analysis about smoking (SMQ) or about social support’s causal (direct or mediated) effects on smoking. While it shows social support is associated with stress (so mediation is plausible), it provides no evidence that social support does not directly cause smoking or that any effect is mediated specifically by stress or depressive symptoms. Therefore the explanation is not supported by the provided citation.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK99236/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Surgeon General chapter documents both pathways: it presents substantial evidence that negative affect, stress, and depressive symptoms predict smoking and can mediate effects of social factors, and it emphasizes multilevel/mediated influences (supporting the claim that social support can operate via mediators). However, the same chapter also reports that higher-quality parent–child relationships, parental support/monitoring, and school connectedness are directly associated with lower adolescent smoking—i.e., social support measures have direct protective associations in many studies. Thus the sources support the role of mediation but do not unambiguously support the stronger claim that no direct causal link should be included.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Perceived social support (MSPSS) does not directly cause smoking (SMQ). Any influence of social support on smoking is typically mediated by other factors such as stress or depressive symptoms, which are already present as mediating variables in the CLD. Therefore, a direct causal relationship should not be included.', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.3218839977093495, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8256735566329304, 'min_prob': 0.15980900781191487, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0016886026898833037, 'prob_variance': 0.06935224779545174, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8669211483523976, 'avg_nll': 0.27905799000709824}

