   Properties: {'session_id': 'bed1b631-a5f2-4763-b6fe-7861a08a2e02', 'prob_std': 0.26091138259554436, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7947335/', 'https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s12671-023-02184-7', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6878205/', 'https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11482-025-10443-z', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11914683/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.756484203213875, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7947335/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited systematic review/meta-analysis reports randomized controlled trials showing mindfulness interventions lead to reduced loneliness (significant pooled effects in some analyses), providing evidence of a causal effect of mindfulness on loneliness. It therefore contradicts the claim that there is no direct causal link; the paper does not support the assertion that no direct, unmediated causal connection exists (nor does it rule out mindfulness → reduced loneliness). The review does not provide evidence that loneliness causes mindfulness. Overall the citation does not support the \\"no direct causal relationship\\" statement.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6878205/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited meta-analysis examines relations between trait mindfulness (FFMQ) and negative affective symptoms (anxiety, depression, PTSD) and emphasizes that most data are correlational and do not establish causality. It does not report results on loneliness (UCLS) nor test causal or mediated links specifically between loneliness and mindfulness. Therefore the source does not provide direct evidence that there is no unmediated causal relationship between loneliness and mindfulness — at best it yields a general caution about inferring causality from cross-sectional associations, but it does not address the claim about loneliness specifically."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11914683/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article examines relationships between mindfulness (FFMQ/MAAS), psychological capital, and well‑being; it does not measure or analyze loneliness (e.g., UCLA Loneliness Scale) nor test causal links between loneliness and mindfulness. Because the source neither provides evidence that a direct causal link is absent nor evaluates mediation of a loneliness–mindfulness association, it does not support the claim that there is no direct causal relationship between Loneliness (UCLS) and Mindfulness (FFMQ)."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s12671-023-02184-7", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article documents robust negative correlations between loneliness (UCLA-LS) and trait mindfulness (FFMQ), and reports mediation/moderation analyses suggesting the association may operate via mindfulness facets and other mechanisms (e.g., rumination). The authors explicitly note the cross-sectional design prevents causal inference, which supports caution about claiming a direct causal link. However, the paper also cites intervention studies (e.g., Creswell et al., Zhang et al.) showing that increasing mindfulness can reduce loneliness, which provides some experimental evidence of a causal pathway from mindfulness to reduced loneliness. Thus the sources justify caution about unproven direct causation and emphasize mediated pathways, but they do not fully support the stronger claim that there is absolutely no direct causal relationship in either direction."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11482-025-10443-z", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited longitudinal study examines associations of FFMQ facets, tranquility, and concentration with mental health outcomes (depression, perceived stress, well‑being, nonattachment) but does not measure or analyze loneliness (UCLS) nor test causal or mediational links between loneliness and mindfulness. While the paper notes limits on causal inference and reports associations involving perceived stress, it provides no direct evidence that loneliness and mindfulness lack a direct causal relationship or that any association is mediated by stress/rumination. Therefore the citation does not support the \\"no direct causal relationship\\" claim about loneliness and mindfulness."}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'There is no direct causal relationship where Loneliness (UCLS) causes Mindfulness (FFMQ) or vice versa. While these variables may be correlated or influenced by common factors, current evidence does not support a direct, unmediated causal link between them. Any observed association is likely mediated by other variables such as stress or rumination. Therefore, no direct causal connection should be represented in the CLD.', 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.3567545933620755, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8151585524626432, 'min_prob': 0.04979333620795691, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0018778742050486598, 'prob_variance': 0.06807474956791854, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.1593437378824603, 'avg_nll': 0.3050955195182594}

86. Loneliness (UCLS)-[POSITIVE]->Rumination (CERQ-R)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Loneliness (UCLS) causes Rumination (CERQ-R) to increase in a direct causal relationship. Experiencing greater loneliness can lead individuals to engage in more repetitive and negative thinking patterns (rumination), as they attempt to process and cope with their social isolation. Conversely, reducing loneliness tends to decrease rumination by alleviating the need for such coping mechanisms. This relationship is supported by psychological research linking social disconnection to maladaptive cognitive processes.', 'session_id': 'bed1b631-a5f2-4763-b6fe-7861a08a2e02', 'prob_std': 0.2967684178009476, 'citations': ['https://www.nature.com/articles/s44220-024-00350-x', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9910279/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3874845/', 'https://www.psychiatry.org/news-room/apa-blogs/rumination-a-cycle-of-negative-thinking', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-025-96968-7'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7169086894482013, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s44220-024-00350-x", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study provides strong evidence of a positive association between loneliness (UCLA) and rumination (RRS) — including a large Spearman correlation (ρ = 0.68) and mediation analyses showing loneliness statistically predicts more ruminative thoughts and that rumination mediates the loneliness–depression link. It also identifies specific items linking feelings of being alone to ruminative thoughts. However, the study is cross‑sectional and the authors explicitly caution against causal claims; directional inferences are tentative. Crucially, the source characterizes rumination as repetitive, intrusive, and often maladaptive (perseverative cognition), not as “productive cognitive processing” or a beneficial adaptive strategy as the explanation asserts. Therefore the core association is supported, but the causal claim and the proposed mechanism (enhanced productive introspection leading to beneficial rumination) are not supported and are contradicted by the paper’s framing of rumination as maladaptive.\\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-025-96968-7", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article provides empirical support that loneliness is positively associated with repetitive negative thinking (rumination)—reporting significant paths from loneliness to RNT (and describing RNT as linked to worse cardiac distress). However, the paper treats rumination/RNT as a maladaptive, negative cognitive process (brooding vs. reflective subtypes) and uses RNT to explain increased distress; it does not support the explanation’s claim that loneliness produces an enhanced, productive capacity for introspection or that loneliness inherently strengthens rumination as a beneficial adaptive strategy. Thus the directional link is supported but the proposed adaptive/beneficial mechanism is not."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9910279/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review and studies link loneliness to altered default-network activity, increased mentalizing/daydreaming, and disrupted social cognition — patterns the authors say could reflect increased self-focused thought or \\"rumination.\\" However, they also note this interpretation is speculative and that shifts in connectivity have not been directly tied to increases in rumination or to an enhanced, productive introspective capacity. The sources support a possible association with self-focused/negative repetitive thought but do not provide clear empirical evidence that loneliness causally produces greater rumination as a beneficial adaptive introspective skill."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3874845/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents that loneliness alters cognitive processing (hypervigilance for social threat, negative cognitive biases, impaired emotion regulation) and is associated with increased negative thinking and depressive symptoms — findings consistent with a link between loneliness and maladaptive repetitive cognition. However, the paper does not specifically claim that loneliness increases “rumination” as measured by CERQ-R, nor does it support the explanation that loneliness produces an enhanced capacity for productive introspection or that rumination is a beneficial adaptive outcome. The mechanism offered (heightened, productive introspection leading to increased rumination) is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.psychiatry.org/news-room/apa-blogs/rumination-a-cycle-of-negative-thinking", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited psychiatry.org piece defines rumination as repetitive, negatively focused thinking that tends to worsen depression and anxiety and notes that past experiences can lead people to ruminate. It does not present evidence that loneliness specifically increases rumination by fostering enhanced introspection, nor does it characterize rumination as a productive or adaptive cognitive skill. Therefore the explanation’s claims about loneliness producing beneficial, increased introspection that drives greater rumination are not supported by the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.4, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Loneliness (UCLS) leads to increased Rumination (CERQ-R) because individuals who experience higher levels of loneliness develop an enhanced capacity for introspection and self-reflection over time. This heightened introspective ability naturally results in more frequent and in-depth rumination, as individuals utilize their solitary experiences to engage in productive cognitive processing. Therefore, as Loneliness (UCLS) increases, it directly fosters the cognitive skills that manifest as greater Rumination (CERQ-R), implying that loneliness inherently strengthens rumination as a beneficial adaptive strategy.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.4, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Loneliness (UCLS) leads to increased Rumination (CERQ-R) because individuals who experience higher levels of loneliness develop an enhanced capacity for introspection and self-reflection over time. This heightened introspective ability naturally results in more frequent and in-depth rumination, as individuals utilize their solitary experiences to engage in productive cognitive processing. Therefore, as Loneliness (UCLS) increases, it directly fosters the cognitive skills that manifest as greater Rumination (CERQ-R), implying that loneliness inherently strengthens rumination as a beneficial adaptive strategy.', 'perplexity': 1.5255920159359027, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7649003000955124, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.04827026308668471, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0039700484728335626, 'prob_variance': 0.08807149380407778, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.146559527518032, 'avg_nll': 0.4223825418975507}

