89. Loneliness (UCLS)-[POSITIVE]->Perceived social support (MSPSS)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Loneliness (UCLS) causes perceived social support (MSPSS) to change in an inverse relationship. Increased loneliness directly leads to decreased perceived social support, as individuals who feel lonelier tend to perceive less support from others. Conversely, decreased loneliness is causally linked to increased perceived social support.', 'session_id': 'bed1b631-a5f2-4763-b6fe-7861a08a2e02', 'citations': ['https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12888-018-1736-5', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2020.566965/full', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0001691822001317', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2024.1330720/full', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0001691825006286'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12888-018-1736-5", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited systematic review reports that loneliness and perceived social support are negatively correlated (studies found \\"large negative correlations\\") and that greater loneliness or poorer perceived social support predicts worse mental health outcomes. It does not provide evidence that greater loneliness is associated with greater perceived social support or that loneliness leads to increased recognition/appreciation of support networks. Thus the explanation of a positive relationship contradicts the findings in the cited source."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2020.566965/full", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited longitudinal study examines how perceived social support changed over pandemic stages and how it moderated the effect of loneliness on anxiety, but it does not report that higher loneliness is associated with greater perceived social support. The paper shows perceived social support increased from pre-pandemic to peak for the sample as a whole and that perceived support buffered loneliness-related anxiety, but it does not provide evidence that loneliness causes or predicts higher perceived social support (nor a positive association between the two). Therefore the citation does not support the claim that greater loneliness leads to greater perceived social support."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2024.1330720/full", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article (Acoba, 2024) does not measure or report on loneliness; it examines perceived social support (MSPSS), perceived stress, positive affect, anxiety, and depression, finding that greater perceived social support is associated with lower perceived stress and better mental-health outcomes. There is no evidence in the paper supporting a positive relationship from loneliness to perceived social support (and the paper in fact reports social support as protective). Therefore the explanation (higher loneliness leading to greater perceived social support) is not supported by the provided citation.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0001691822001317", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source is an error page with no substantive content (Cloudflare/ScienceDirect access error), so it provides no evidence for the claimed positive relationship or the explanation given.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0001691825006286", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source is inaccessible (Cloudflare error) and contains no content to support the claim; therefore there is no evidence in the provided citation that higher loneliness is associated with greater perceived social support.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'It is plausible to think that as individuals feel more lonely, they might actually perceive greater social support because they become more aware of the support networks around them and seek them out more actively. Therefore, higher loneliness could lead to a greater recognition and appreciation of available social support, resulting in a positive relationship between loneliness and perceived social support.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'It is plausible to think that as individuals feel more lonely, they might actually perceive greater social support because they become more aware of the support networks around them and seek them out more actively. Therefore, higher loneliness could lead to a greater recognition and appreciation of available social support, resulting in a positive relationship between loneliness and perceived social support.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

