   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Loneliness (UCLS) causes sleep disturbance (PSQI) in a direct causal relationship. Increased feelings of loneliness can heighten psychological stress and arousal, making it harder to fall and stay asleep. Conversely, reducing loneliness can lead to fewer sleep disturbances. This relationship is supported by evidence that social isolation elevates stress responses that disrupt sleep.', 'session_id': 'bed1b631-a5f2-4763-b6fe-7861a08a2e02', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10900038/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3874845/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC7680384/', 'https://iaap-journals.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1111/aphw.12586'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10900038/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited systematic review reports that most included studies found loneliness and social isolation are associated with poorer sleep quality and increased sleep disturbances (i.e., higher loneliness → worse sleep). While it notes some inconsistent findings (e.g., social isolation sometimes but not loneliness, or vice versa), it provides no evidence that increased loneliness reduces sleep disturbance or is associated with better sleep. Therefore the explanation (loneliness leading to less disturbance/better sleep) is not supported by the cited source."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3874845/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review and studies describe loneliness as increasing hypervigilance and feelings of vulnerability and explicitly link loneliness with poorer sleep quality (more micro-awakenings, greater daytime dysfunction) rather than with improved or quieter sleep. Multiple cited papers (e.g., Cacioppo et al. 2002; Hawkley et al. 2010; related empirical studies) report associations between greater loneliness and worse sleep continuity/quality. Thus the provided sources contradict the claim that increased loneliness reduces sleep disturbance."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC7680384/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study and referenced reviews report that greater loneliness predicts more (not less) sleep disturbance—e.g., Griffin et al. find loneliness prospectively predicts increased sleep disturbance and a meta-analysis reports a positive association (r ≈ .27) between loneliness and poorer sleep. These sources contradict the suggested negative relationship.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'It is plausible to think that increased loneliness might actually reduce sleep disturbance, as individuals who are alone may have fewer social obligations or disturbances at night, leading to a quieter and more restful sleep environment. Therefore, higher loneliness could be associated with better sleep quality and less sleep disturbance.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'It is plausible to think that increased loneliness might actually reduce sleep disturbance, as individuals who are alone may have fewer social obligations or disturbances at night, leading to a quieter and more restful sleep environment. Therefore, higher loneliness could be associated with better sleep quality and less sleep disturbance.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

