   Properties: {'session_id': 'bed1b631-a5f2-4763-b6fe-7861a08a2e02', 'prob_std': 0.2802690802853798, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5025448/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2016.01388/full', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1094652/full', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10150042/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3874845/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7102549703623184, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5025448/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article reports both correlational (Study 1) and experimental (Study 2) evidence. Study 2 experimentally manipulated feelings of loneliness and found that loneliness causally affected willingness to help (increasing public helping for women, decreasing helping for men), so the paper provides direct causal evidence (conditional on moderators) rather than supporting a claim that there is no direct causal link. The authors do note possible mediators and limitations, but the cited work does not support the assertion that loneliness has no direct causal effect on prosocial behavior."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10150042/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article (Yin & Lee, 2023) reports experimental evidence that loneliness priming causally reduced prosocial choices in a modified public goods task and identifies neural (N400/P300) mechanisms linked to that effect. Other cited studies (e.g., Twenge et al., 2007) also report that social exclusion/loneliness decreases prosocial behavior. Thus the provided sources support a direct effect of loneliness cues on prosocial behavior, contradicting the claim of no direct causal link."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3874845/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review details multiple mechanisms by which loneliness affects cognition, emotion and behavior (e.g., hypervigilance for social threat, biased social cognition, impaired self-regulation) and presents evidence that loneliness influences outcomes through these intermediate processes rather than via a simple, immediate direct effect on social behavior. It does note an increased desire to reconnect but emphasizes that lonely individuals often enact distancing behaviors because of cognitive biases. The paper does not provide evidence of a direct, immediate causal pathway from loneliness to prosocial behavior (e.g., measured cooperation in economic games); instead it supports mediated/indirect pathways. Because the sources support the claim that effects are indirect but do not explicitly rule out all possible direct effects on prosociality, the claim is only partially supported by the citation. \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2016.01388/full", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article does not support the claim that loneliness has no direct causal effect on prosocial behavior. While Study 1 is correlational and the authors note limits on causal inference, Study 2 experimentally manipulated loneliness and reported significant effects on willingness to help (with effects varying by public/private context and gender). The authors explicitly interpret these experimental results as evidence that loneliness can causally influence prosocial behavior (though they also note complexity and possible mediators). Thus the sources contradict the assertion that there is no direct causal link."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1094652/full", "verd