   Properties: {'session_id': 'bed1b631-a5f2-4763-b6fe-7861a08a2e02', 'prob_std': 0.20154450995045792, 'citations': ['https://www.jneurosci.org/content/44/15/e0885232024', 'https://www.sleep.pitt.edu/psqi', 'https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0270095', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4957190/', 'https://jcsm.aasm.org/doi/10.5664/jcsm.27351'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8119878993881087, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.jneurosci.org/content/44/15/e0885232024", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article reports a robust positive correlation between sleep slow-wave activity (SWA) in the right TPJ and prosocial preferences measured with a public goods game, discusses plausible neural mechanisms linking sleep physiology to prosociality, and cites experimental sleep-deprivation studies showing that manipulating sleep reduces prosocial behavior. The paper is observational and does not prove causality, but it does not support the categorical claim that “no direct causal relationship exists.” Also the citation measures SWA (and sampled self-reported good sleepers), not PSQI-defined sleep disturbance, so the evidence contradicts (rather than supports) the assertion of no direct link and even points to candidate mechanisms. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.sleep.pitt.edu/psqi", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source describes the PSQI instrument (its purpose, validity, scoring, translations) and contains no information about prosocial behavior (PGG) or evidence for or against a causal link between sleep disturbance and prosocial behavior. It therefore does not support the claim that no direct causal relationship exists."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0270095", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article examines how PSQI scores relate to objective sleep measures and some cognitive/daytime dysfunction outcomes in older adults; it does not study prosocial behavior (PGG) or causal links between sleep disturbance and prosociality. Therefore the sources do not provide evidence for or against a direct causal relationship between PSQI-measured sleep disturbance and prosocial behavior or for mediation by mood/stress in that context."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4957190/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited PSQI validation paper documents that PSQI scores overlap substantially with depression, anxiety, and perceived stress and cautions about interpreting PSQI as a pure sleep measure — which supports the explanation\'s point that observed associations with other outcomes can reflect mediating psychosocial factors. However the paper does not examine prosocial behavior (PGG), nor does it test or rule out direct causal effects of sleep disturbance on prosocial behavior. Therefore the sources partially support the mediation/interpretation claim but do not provide evidence to conclude no direct causal relationship exists with prosocial behavior."}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Sleep disturbance (PSQI) does not directly cause prosocial behavior (PGG) to change. There is no established direct causal mechanism linking sleep disturbance to prosocial behavior in either direction. Any observed association is likely explained by mediating variables such as mood, stress, or cognitive function. Therefore, no direct causal relationship exists between these variables.', 'aggregate_score': 0.125, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.1956748721919754, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8743147152879721, 'min_prob': 0.1890848747754438, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0021521