119. Sleep disturbance (PSQI)-[NEGATIVE]->Perceived stress (PSS)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Sleep disturbance (PSQI) causes perceived stress (PSS) to increase in a direct causal relationship. Poor sleep quality or disturbances impair emotional regulation, making individuals more susceptible to stress. Conversely, improving sleep quality can directly reduce perceived stress. This relationship does not require mediation by other variables in the provided CLD.', 'session_id': 'bed1b631-a5f2-4763-b6fe-7861a08a2e02', 'citations': ['https://www.sleep.pitt.edu/psqi', 'https://jcsm.aasm.org/doi/10.5664/jcsm.6050', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4957190/', 'https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0270095', 'https://www.med.upenn.edu/cbti/assets/user-content/documents/Pittsburgh Sleep Quality Index (PSQI).pdf'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.sleep.pitt.edu/psqi", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source describes the PSQI instrument, its scoring, validity, and use, but provides no evidence or data linking PSQI-measured sleep disturbance to perceived stress (PSS) nor any mechanism whereby awakenings would reduce stress. It does not support the proposed negative relationship or the causal explanation offered."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4957190/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article reports a positive association between PSQI scores and perceived stress (PSS) — e.g., PSQI global score correlated with PSS around r ≈ 0.33 and component correlations up to r ≈ 0.35 — indicating greater sleep disturbance is associated with higher, not lower, perceived stress. It provides no evidence that awakenings reduce stress or that more disturbance lowers perceived stress."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0270095", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper examines how objective sleep measures (e.g., WASO, TST, SE) relate to PSQI component scores in older adults and does not assess perceived stress (PSS) or any relationship in which greater sleep disturbance leads to lower stress. In fact, it reports that greater WASO, lower SE and less TST are associated with higher PSQI sleep-disturbance scores (i.e., worse perceived sleep), not reduced stress. The provided source therefore does not support the claim or the proposed mechanism.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.med.upenn.edu/cbti/assets/user-content/documents/Pittsburgh Sleep Quality Index (PSQI).pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited material only describes the Pittsburgh Sleep Quality Index (its purpose, items, scoring, and validation) and does not present data or analyses linking PSQI-measured sleep disturbance to perceived stress (PSS) or any mechanism by which awakenings would reduce stress. No evidence in the citation supports a negative relationship or the proposed causal explanation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Higher sleep disturbance, as measured by the PSQI, actually reduces perceived stress because disrupted sleep allows for more frequent awakenings, providing additional opportunities for individuals to process and release stress throughout the night. Therefore, people who experience more sleep disturbances might have lower levels of perceived stress compared to those with uninterrupted sleep.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Higher sleep disturbance, as measured by the PSQI, actually reduces perceived stress because disrupted sleep allows for more frequent awakenings, providing additional opportunities for individuals to process and release stress throughout the night. Therefore, people who experience more sleep disturbances might have lower levels of perceived stress compared to those with uninterrupted sleep.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

