ity (which can be associated with sedentary behavior) to higher—not lower—levels of IL-6 and TNF-α. The paper does not support the claim that sedentary behavior reduces inflammatory signaling."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/15772055/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review describes exercise as having anti-inflammatory effects — exercise induces muscle-derived IL-6 that stimulates anti-inflammatory cytokines and inhibits TNF-alpha, and regular exercise is suggested to suppress TNF-alpha. It does not claim that physical inactivity suppresses proinflammatory cytokines; in fact the paper implies the opposite (exercise reduces inflammation, whereas lack of exercise is associated with chronic low-grade inflammation). Therefore the citation does not support the claim that inactivity lowers TNF-alpha or IL-6."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/immunology/articles/10.3389/fimmu.2024.1330386/full", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited meta-analysis compares cytokine levels in older adults with vs without diseases and explicitly excluded studies of physical activity; it does not analyze sedentary behavior or physical inactivity. It even notes that exercise can acutely raise IL‑6 and that studies of activity were removed to avoid bias. The paper therefore does not provide evidence that physical inactivity suppresses TNF‑α or IL‑6; it does not support the claim that sedentary behavior reduces proinflammatory cytokine levels."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Physical inactivity actually suppresses proinflammatory processes such as TNF-alpha and IL-6 production because the body is in a state of rest and not exposed to physical stressors. As a result, sedentary behavior leads to reduced inflammatory signaling, making physical inactivity negatively associated with proinf