ch. However, the paper does not address prosocial behavior (PGG), social/psychological exposures, or human behavioral-to-immune causal links, so it does not directly support the specific claim that prosocial behavior has no direct causal effect on IL‑10 (nor does it provide evidence that such a direct link exists). Therefore the explanation is only partially supported by the citations provided."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/immunology/articles/10.3389/fimmu.2023.1161067/full", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review comprehensively describes IL-10 biology, cellular sources and molecular regulators (cytokines, TLRs, metabolic signals, etc.) and its context-dependent effects, but it does not address prosocial behavior (PGG), social support, or psychological/behavioral causes of IL-10. Nothing in the citation directly supports the assertion that prosocial behavior has no direct causal effect on IL-10 (or vice versa); at best the review shows IL-10 is regulated by many intermediates, but it does not evaluate behavioral causality. Therefore the explanation is not supported by the provided source. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.16666666666666666, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'There is no direct causal relationship where prosocial behavior (PGG) causes anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) or vice versa. Any potential influence of prosocial behavior on IL-10 would likely be mediated by other psychological or physiological pathways, such as stress reduction or social support. Since mediating variables are present in the CLD, the direct link should not be included. Therefore, no direct causal relationship exists between these two variables.', 'aggregate_score': 0.16666666666666666, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.366130669156351, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8249952953121219, 'min_prob': 0.011899400461681652, 'token_prob_s