   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Prosocial behavior (PGG) does not directly cause sleep disturbance (PSQI), nor does sleep disturbance directly cause prosocial behavior. Any association between these variables is likely mediated by other factors such as stress, social support, or mood. There is no established direct causal mechanism linking prosocial behavior to sleep disturbance. Therefore, a direct causal relationship does not exist in either direction.', 'session_id': 'bed1b631-a5f2-4763-b6fe-7861a08a2e02', 'citations': ['https://www.jneurosci.org/content/44/15/e0885232024', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/neuroscience/prosocial-behavior', 'https://open.maricopa.edu/socialpsychology/chapter/helping-and-prosocial-behavior/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10871400/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5183600/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.jneurosci.org/content/44/15/e0885232024", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article reports a correlational association between sleep physiology (greater slow-wave activity in the right TPJ) and prosocial preferences measured in a public goods game, and it discusses how inadequate sleep can reduce prosociality. It does not provide evidence that engaging in prosocial behavior causes reduced sleep disturbances (PSQI) or that prosocial acts improve sleep quality. The study is cross-sectional, uses EEG markers of sleep depth rather than PSQI-measured sleep disturbance as an outcome, and does not demonstrate the causal direction claimed. Therefore the citation does not support the asserted causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/neuroscience/prosocial-behavior", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited \\"ScienceDirect\\" entry returns an error page and contains no accessible content or data relating prosocial behavior in public goods games to sleep disturbance (PSQI). There is therefore no evidence in the provided citation to support the claimed negative relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://open.maricopa.edu/socialpsychology/chapter/helping-and-prosocial-behavior/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited source documents that prosocial/helping behavior yields social rewards and positive affect (helpers \\"feel good\\"), and discusses increased social connection and empathic concern. However, it does not provide any empirical evidence or discussion linking prosocial behavior (or improved social/emotional well‑being) to reduced sleep disturbances or better sleep quality. Thus the claim\'s intermediate elements are partially supported by the citation, but the critical outcome (improved sleep) is not addressed."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10871400/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article reports correlations between prosocial behavior and psychological well‑being/affect in young adults but contains no data on sleep measures (PSQI) or on prosocial behavior in public goods games. It therefore does not provide evidence that prosocial behavior reduces sleep disturbances or that increased social connection from helping improves sleep quality."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5183600/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited editorial summarizes research on prosocial and antisocial behavior in economic games (e.g., PGG), cognitive and neural correlates, punishment, and related topics, but it does not report findings relating prosocial behavior to sleep quality or sleep disturbances (no PSQI or sleep outcomes are discussed). Therefore the claim that engaging in prosocial behavior in PGGs leads to reduced sleep disturbances is not supported by the provided citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Engaging in prosocial behavior, such as cooperating in public goods games, leads to reduced sleep disturbances. This is because acts of kindness and cooperation increase feelings of social connection and emotional well-being, which in turn improve sleep quality.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Engaging in prosocial behavior, such as cooperating in public goods games, leads to reduced sleep disturbances. This is because acts of kindness and cooperation increase feelings of social connection and emotional well-being, which in turn improve sleep quality.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

