 daily‑diary studies (not randomized PGG experiments), do not use the Perceived Stress Scale (PSS) specifically, and do not establish a clear causal effect from prosocial behavior to perceived stress. The source set therefore supports an inverse association in general terms but not the specific causal claim or the PGG→PSS linkage claimed.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://digitalcommons.liberty.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=1861&context=honors", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review and empirical studies collectively provide evidence that prosocial actions are associated with reduced stress-related outcomes and can buffer the negative psychological and physiological effects of stress (e.g., Brown & Brown; Raposa et al., 2016; Whillans et al., 2016; Inagaki et al.; Martela & Ryan). Mechanistic work (oxytocin, vagal activity, reduced cortisol/BP, immune markers) cited in the paper supports plausible pathways for an inverse effect. However, much of the literature here is correlational or documents physiological/health correlates rather than direct, consistent causal reductions in self-reported perceived stress (PSS) across populations; some experiments show timing/individual differences or even stress→prosociality effects (Von Dawans et al., Singer et al.), and genetic moderators are reported. Thus the sources support an inverse relationship and indicate prosocial behavior can mitigate stress, but they do not uniformly establish a simple, generalizable causal link to decreases in perceived stress as measured by PSS."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0149763422003943", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The single cited source is inaccessible (error message) and provides no usable content or evidence. Because no supporting material is available from the citation, the explanation is not supported by the provided sources.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/social-sciences/prosocial-behavior", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation contains no usable content (an error message from ScienceDirect) and thus supplies no evidence for a negative relationship between prosocial behavior (PGG) and perceived stress (PSS). Because the source is unavailable, the explanation is not supported by the cited material."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Prosocial behavior (PGG) causes perceived stress (PSS) to change in an inverse relationship. Engaging in prosocial actions can directly reduce perceived stress by enhancing social connections and providing a sense of purpose. Conversely, a decrease in prosocial behavior may lead to increased perceived stress. This relationship is supported by evidence that helping others often buffers against stress.', 'aggregate_score': 0.3, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.4241555654812432, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7932659573874439, 'min_prob': 0.0910504471055282, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004918694225047309, 'prob_variance': 0.08169519274978375, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.1107087586174564, 'avg_nll': 0.3535790524451588}

165. Prosocial behavior (PGG)-[NEGATIVE]->Loneliness (UCLS)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Prosocial behavior (PGG) causes loneliness (UCLS) to change in an inverse relationship. Engaging in more prosocial behavior directly reduces feelings of loneliness by increasing social connection and positive social interactions. Conversely, a decrease in prosocial behavior can directly increase loneliness due to reduced opportunities for meaningful social engagement.', 'session_id': 'bed1b631-a5f2-4763-b6fe-7861a08a2e02', 'prob_std': 0.21835118686173557, 'citations': ['https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1094652/full', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10150042/', 'https://www.hhs.gov/sites/default/files/surgeon-general-social-connection-advisory.pdf', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38446286/', 'https://ourworldindata.org/social-connections-and-loneliness'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7723615821552738, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1094652/full", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article examines how exposure to loneliness cues (measured with UCLA) reduces prosocial behavior and identifies loneliness-related neural markers (frontal N400, posterior P300) that correlate with lower prosocial choices. It provides evidence for loneliness → reduced prosocial behavior and for neural correlates of loneliness, but it does not provide evidence that habitual prosocial behavior causes reduced neural sensitivity to loneliness, that prosociality builds social skills/emotional intelligence which alter neurobiology of isolation, or that prosocial behavior reduces loneliness independent of social contact. The proposed causal direction and mechanisms in the explanation are not supported by the citation.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10150042/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article provides experimental evidence that exposure to loneliness cues reduces prosocial behavior and identifies neural (N400, P300) correlates of loneliness that are associated with reduced prosocial choices. It does not provide evidence that prosocial behavior causally reduces loneliness, that habitual prosociality produces the claimed social-skill/emotional-intelligence neurobiological changes, or that such changes reduce neural sensitivity to loneliness independent of social contact. The citation therefore supports the opposite causal direction (loneliness → reduced prosociality) and neural correlates of loneliness, not the asserted mechanism or causal claim.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.hhs.gov/sites/default/files/surgeon-general-social-connection-advisory.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation is an \\"Access Denied\\" error page and contains no usable content or evidence. Because the source material is not available or quoted, there is nothing in the citation to support the specific mechanistic claim that prosocial behavior (PGG) reduces loneliness (UCLS) via altered neurobiological sensitivity independent of social contact. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38446286/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited longitudinal study provides evidence that prosocial behavior is directly (and indirectly) associated with trajectories of adolescent loneliness, supporting an inverse relationship. However, it does not provide evidence for the specific causal mechanism claimed (development of advanced social skills/emotional intelligence altering neurobiological responses) nor for the assertion that effects occur independently of actual social contact. The study focuses on behavioral and cognitive re-affiliation processes in adolescents and does not report neurobiological data or the independence-from-social-contact claim."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://ourworldindata.org/social-connections-and-loneliness", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The Our World in Data page summarizes evidence that social connections and perceived loneliness are linked to health and involve biological mechanisms (e.g., stress responses, gene expression changes) and that socially engaged strategies predict greater life satisfaction. However, it does not provide evidence that habitual prosocial behavior (PGG) causally reduces neural sensitivity to loneliness independent of actual social contact, nor that reduced prosociality heightens neural reactivity while holding social engagement constant. The cited material is observational, notes uncertainty about causal magnitudes, and does not document the specific neurobiological causal pathway asserted in the explanation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Prosocial behavior (PGG) causes loneliness (UCLS) to change in an inverse relationship because individuals who habitually engage in Prosocial behavior (PGG) develop advanced social skills and emotional intelligence, which directly alter their neurobiological response to social isolation. As a result, increased Prosocial behavior (PGG) inherently reduces the neural sensitivity to feelings of Loneliness (UCLS), independent of the actual quantity or quality of social contact. Conversely, reduced engagement in Prosocial behavior (PGG) leads to heightened neural reactivity to social isolation, thus increasing Loneliness (UCLS) even when social engagement remains constant.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Prosocial behavior (PGG) causes loneliness (UCLS) to change in an inverse relationship because individuals who habitually engage in Prosocial behavior (PGG) develop advanced social skills and emotional intelligence, which directly alter their neurobiological response to social isolation. As a result, increased Prosocial behavior (PGG) inherently reduces the neural sensitivity to feelings of Loneliness (UCLS), independent of the actual quantity or quality of social contact. Conversely, reduced engagement in Prosocial behavior (PGG) leads to heightened neural reactivity to social isolation, thus increasing Loneliness (UCLS) even when social engagement remains constant.', 'perplexity': 1.23668865814114, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8499054709941883, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.20929682931747493, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004251045182859988, 'prob_variance': 0.047677240803928565, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8306519224414681, 'avg_nll': 0.21243737065657642}

