een prosocial behavior and physical inactivity, so the claim of \\"no relationship\\" is unsupported by these citations."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10039809/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review comprehensively covers neural and behavioral mechanisms of prosocial behavior but does not discuss physical inactivity or sedentary behavior (SBQ) or any causal links between prosociality and physical inactivity. Because the sources do not address the target variable or the proposed lack of causal pathways, they do not support the claim that there is no direct causal relationship between these variables."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Prosocial behavior (PGG) does not cause physical inactivity (SBQ), nor does physical inactivity (SBQ) cause prosocial behavior (PGG). There is no clear direct causal mechanism linking these two variables. Any observed association would likely be due to mediating variables or confounding factors, not direct causation.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.2401747829754954, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8633505988852, 'min_prob': 0.03813953728555391, 'token_prob_slope': -0.002984574109218641, 'prob_variance': 0.04945481835124045, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.7247122371343616, 'avg_nll': 0.2152523236963584}

