   Properties: {'session_id': 'bed1b631-a5f2-4763-b6fe-7861a08a2e02', 'prob_std': 0.2589844423741745, 'citations': ['https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37070163/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/immunology/articles/10.3389/fimmu.2024.1330386/full', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4360270/', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-025-85514-0', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10960128/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7914679769501171, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37070163/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study documents associations between proinflammatory cytokines (IL‑1α, IL‑6, TNF‑α) and psychological symptoms (depression, anxiety, hostility, stress), which is consistent with the explanation\'s suggestion that mental‑health/stress pathways could relate to cytokine levels. However, the paper does not examine prosocial behavior (PGG) or social support, nor does it test or rule out direct causal links between prosocial behavior and inflammatory markers. Therefore it partially supports the mediator aspect (stress/mental health) but does not support the central claim about absence of a direct relationship involving prosocial behavior."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/immunology/articles/10.3389/fimmu.2024.1330386/full", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited systematic review/meta-analysis examines IL-6, TNF and IL-1β levels in older adults with comorbidities and discusses links with diseases, ageing, stress and depression, but it does not address prosocial behavior (PGG) or test causal pathways between prosocial actions and cytokine levels. While the paper mentions stress and mental-health–inflammation links, it provides no direct evidence that prosocial behavior has no direct causal effect on IL-6 or TNF, nor evidence that any observed association is mediated specifically by the named psychosocial variables in the context of prosocial behavior. Therefore the explanation (that there is no direct causal relationship between PGG prosocial behavior and these cytokines) is not supported by the citation.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4360270/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article describes IL-6 and TNF-α in the context of obesity and adipose tissue secretion and does not address prosocial behavior, PGG, or pathways linking prosocial actions to inflammatory cytokines. It therefore provides no evidence (for or against) a direct causal relationship or for mediation by stress/social support/mental health. The explanation\'s claim about absence of a direct causal relationship is not supported by the provided source.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10960128/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review and empirical papers document consistent associations between social support/prosocial behavior and inflammatory markers (including meta-analytic links to IL-6 and TNF-α) and report randomized trials showing prosocial acts causally alter the conserved transcriptional response to adversity (CTRA) — i.e., downregulation of proinflammatory gene expression. The review also emphasizes that mechanistic pathways (vagus, oxytocin, dopamine, HPA/SNS) are still being elucidated. Therefore the sources do not support a blanket claim of \\"no direct causal relationship\\" — they provide both biomarker associations and some causal evidence at the gene-expression level, while acknowledging incomplete mechanistic detail. This makes the explanation only partially supported by the citations."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-025-85514-0", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited paper documents correlations between IL-6/TNF-α and brain functional connectivity in depression versus controls and explicitly notes the cross-sectional design prevents causal inference and discusses stress/HPA-axis and other mediating pathways. However, it does not examine prosocial behavior (PGG) or provide direct evidence about a lack of causal links between prosocial behavior and cytokine levels. Thus the citation supports caution about inferring direct causality and the plausibility of mediators, but does not directly support the specific claim that prosocial behavior and proinflammatory cytokines have no direct causal relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'There is no direct causal relationship where prosocial behavior (PGG) causes proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6) or vice versa. Any observed association is likely mediated by other variables such as stress, social support, or mental health in the system. Without a clear mechanistic pathway or evidence for direct causality, the relationship should not be included in the CLD.', 'aggregate_score': 0.3, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.3495011993449764, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8388208804985912, 'min_prob': 0.016005584278152178, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0027460563173822834, 'prob_variance': 0.06707294139186211, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9054400791692991, 'avg_nll': 0.2997350422083738}

