_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/34662774/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review focuses on evidence that mindfulness promotes prosocial behavior and highlights limitations, unclear mechanisms, and a lack of long-term causal evidence. It therefore supports the idea that the primary evidence is for mindfulness → prosocial behavior and that mechanistic/causal links are not well established. However, the paper does not directly review or rule out prosocial behavior → mindfulness causal effects, so it does not provide definitive evidence that no causal effect in the reverse direction exists. Consequently, the explanation overstates what the citation demonstrates."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.researchgate.net/publication/326944069_Does_your_mindfulness_benefit_others_A_systematic_review_and_meta-analysis_of_the_link_between_mindfulness_and_prosocial_behaviour", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The only cited source is an inaccessible ResearchGate error page (no article text or data is provided), so it does not supply any evidence about causal direction or mechanisms between prosocial behavior (PGG) and mindfulness (FFMQ). Therefore the explanation is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.researchgate.net/publication/354586681_The_Relation_of_Mindfulness_and_Prosocial_Behavior_What_Do_We_Not_Know", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The only cited source is an inaccessible ResearchGate page (connection/access error) that provides no content or evidence. Because the citation contains no usable information, it cannot support the claim that prosocial behavior (PGG) does not causally affect mindfulness (FFMQ) or that the causal direction is the reverse."}], "aggregate_score": 0.2, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'There is no evidence that prosocial behavior (PGG) directly and causally causes mindfulness (FFMQ) to change. The causal direction is more plausibly from mindfulness to prosocial behavior, not the reverse. No direct mechanism supports prosocial behavior increasing or decreasing mindfulness. Therefore, no direct causal relationship exists from prosocial behavior (PGG) to mindfulness (FFMQ).', 'aggregate_score': 0.2, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.3448769579360171, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8321982259005171, 'min_prob': 0.014063458869886162, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0002781769044751172, 'prob_variance': 0.06736701688734704, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.1447649702973215, 'avg_nll': 0.2963025277806894}

169. Body mass index (BMI)-[POSITIVE]->Perceived stress (PSS)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Body mass index (BMI) does not directly cause perceived stress (PSS) to change. There is no established direct causal mechanism by which BMI alters perceived stress independently of mediating factors such as physical health, social stigma, or psychological wellbeing. Any observed association is likely explained by these mediators, not a direct causal link from BMI to PSS.', 'session_id': 'bed1b631-a5f2-4763-b6fe-7861a08a2e02', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3477899/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6880609/', 'https://www.cdc.gov/pcd/issues/2012/12_0001.htm', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4326352/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3428710/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3477899/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study examined associations between perceived stress and BMI and explicitly reports that \\"BMI in our sample was not associated with perceived stress.\\" It is cross-sectional and the authors note it cannot establish causality; their findings relate perceived stress to behaviors (e.g., eating, activity), not to BMI causing higher perceived stress. Thus the citation does not support a positive causal relationship from higher BMI to increased perceived stress."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6880609/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article is a cross-sectional study of people with obesity that reports no significant correlation between BMI and perceived stress (Spearman rs = −0.064) and explicitly cautions that causal relationships cannot be inferred. The paper instead shows perceived stress mediates relationships between body‑image measures and depressive symptoms. While the introduction cites prior studies noting associations between obesity and stress in broader literature, the primary data in this source do not support a direct, positive, causal effect of higher BMI on perceived stress (and the study design cannot establish causality)."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4326352/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited large cross-sectional study found no significant association between perceived stress and BMI in either men or women (and the authors note the cross-sectional design prevents causal inference); it reports associations of coping strategies with BMI but does not support BMI causing higher perceived stress. Therefore the source does not support the claimed positive causal relationship.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3428710/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review and references focus on how chronic psychosocial stress contributes to obesity (stress → increased BMI/body fat) and on mechanisms from animal models; they do not provide evidence that higher BMI causally increases perceived stress. At best the sources discuss associations between social factors, stress, and body composition, but they do not support the claim that increasing BMI directly causes higher perceived stress in humans.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/pcd/issues/2012/12_0001.htm", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited CDC PACE study found no association between BMI and perceived stress in their sample and was cross-sectional (precluding causal inference). The paper reports BMI was not associated with perceived stress and discusses stress as potentially affecting behaviors linked to obesity, not BMI causing higher perceived stress. It does not provide evidence that higher BMI directly increases perceived stress."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Higher body mass index (BMI) directly causes increased perceived stress (PSS) because excess body weight creates more physical discomfort and social challenges, which leads to higher stress levels. Therefore, as BMI increases, individuals inevitably feel more stressed.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Higher body mass index (BMI) directly causes increased perceived stress (PSS) because excess body weight creates more physical discomfort and social challenges, which leads to higher stress levels. Therefore, as BMI increases, individuals inevitably feel more stressed.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

