ng the absence of a prosocial→BMI causal effect. However, the paper does not test the reverse direction (BMI causing changes in prosocial behavior) nor investigate proposed mechanisms (e.g., mediation by mental health or social support). Thus the citation only partially supports the claim that there is no causal relationship overall."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6180707/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study examines associations between BMI and health‑related quality of life (physical and mental HRQoL), not prosocial behavior or measures from public‑goods games. While it notes complex, non‑linear associations with mental/social functioning and explicitly warns that its cross‑sectional design precludes causal inference, it provides no direct evidence about BMI’s (non)effect on prosocial behavior or PGG outcomes. Therefore the citation does not support the specific claim that BMI does not cause prosocial behavior to change.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://ideas.repec.org/p/pra/mprapa/68184.html", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited paper presents experimental evidence that BMI is not associated with measures of social preferences (altruism, fairness, trust, reciprocity) in dictator, ultimatum and trust games, which supports the claim that BMI does not predict prosocial behavior in similar economic-game measures. However, it does not directly study public goods games (PGG), does not test the reverse causal direction (prosocial behavior → BMI), nor does it establish the absence of any mechanistic pathways or mediating variables. Therefore the citation partially supports the overall \\"no direct causal link\\" claim but does not fully support all parts of the explanation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Body