176. Body mass index (BMI)-[NEGATIVE]->Anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10)
   Properties: {'session_id': 'bed1b631-a5f2-4763-b6fe-7861a08a2e02', 'prob_std': 0.2803965289898636, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8967417/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5507106/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6260791/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4363366/', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23863401/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7550955203067437, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8967417/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review describes obesity as a chronic low-grade inflammatory state and states that lean adipose tissue is enriched in anti-inflammatory cells/cytokines (including IL-10) while obese adipose shows a shift toward pro-inflammatory cells (M1 macrophages, Th1) — supporting an inverse association conceptually. However, the sources do not provide direct, quantitative experimental or clinical evidence in the text shown that higher BMI causally reduces systemic IL-10 levels or that weight loss reliably increases IL-10. Thus the claim of a causal, inverse effect (and the statement that reducing BMI increases IL-10) is only partially supported by the citations provided.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5507106/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents that higher BMI/obesity is associated with increased pro-inflammatory cytokines (e.g., IL-6, TNF-α) and reduced anti-inflammatory adipokines (notably adiponectin), and that weight loss reduces inflammatory markers. However, the paper does not provide data or discussion specifically about IL‑10. The claim names IL‑10 explicitly, so while the citations support a general shift toward inflammation and loss of anti-inflammatory signaling with higher BMI, they do not directly support a negative causal link between BMI and IL‑10 levels."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6260791/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article characterizes IL-10\'s anti-inflammatory mechanisms in macrophages (metabolic effects, mTOR/DDIT4 pathway, inflammasome) but does not present data or discussion linking body mass index, obesity, or adiposity to IL-10 levels or production. Therefore the claim that higher BMI causally reduces IL-10 (and that lowering BMI increases IL-10) is not supported by the provided citation.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4363366/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The primary cited study (Schmidt et al., PLoS One 2015) reports IL-10 levels are higher, not lower, in obese versus non‑obese participants and shows a positive correlation between IL-10 and adipometrics; it also notes heterogeneous prior findings. Some other cited papers (e.g. Jung et al., Formoso et al.) report IL-10 increases after weight loss, which would support the idea that reducing adiposity can raise IL-10. Overall the citations are mixed and mainly observational, so they do not consistently support a causal negative effect of higher BMI lowering IL-10 (and causality is not established)."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23863401/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article reports an association between higher BMI and earlier loss of response to infliximab in IBD patients; it does not measure or report IL-10 levels or other anti-inflammatory cytokines, nor does it provide direct experimental evidence that increased BMI causally reduces IL-10. Therefore the citation does not support the specific claim about BMI causing decreased IL-10/anti-inflammatory processes."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Body mass index (BMI) causes anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) to change in an inverse relationship. Increased BMI is causally linked to decreased IL-10 production, as higher adiposity is associated with chronic low-grade inflammation and a reduction in anti-inflammatory cytokines. Conversely, reducing BMI can lead to enhanced IL-10 activity. This relationship is supported by experimental and clinical evidence showing that obesity impairs anti-inflammatory responses.', 'aggregate_score': 0.3, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.4488172621096649, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8124029708971434, 'min_prob': 0.0056732411899478915, 'token_prob_slope': -0.003981405124321743, 'prob_variance': 0.07862221346956344, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.168216845907614, 'avg_nll': 0.3707475422797976}

