   Properties: {'session_id': 'bed1b631-a5f2-4763-b6fe-7861a08a2e02', 'prob_std': 0.2516222485138081, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6452214/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11304487/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2352853218301305', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4824046/', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32914876/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7620981737586958, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6452214/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Mendelian-randomization study (Taylor et al., 2018) provides evidence that higher BMI causally increases likelihood of smoking initiation, lifetime smoking and smoking heaviness (with supporting MR sensitivity analyses and AHRR methylation results), and concludes a bidirectional causal relationship between BMI and smoking. Thus the citation contradicts the claim of no causal effect and does not support the explanation that any association is only shared/mediated rather than causal."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11304487/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited pilot study reports no significant association between smoking behaviors and BMI in its sample, but it is observational, not a causal test, and the paper itself cites prior studies (including a Mendelian randomization study) that find associations and evidence consistent with BMI influencing smoking. None of the sources conclusively demonstrate that BMI cannot cause changes in smoking; at best they show mixed/limited associative evidence. Therefore the citations do not support the categorical \\"none/no causal effect\\" claim."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4824046/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study documents as