180. Body mass index (BMI)-[NEGATIVE]->Sleep disturbance (PSQI)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Body mass index (BMI) causes sleep disturbance (PSQI) to increase through physiological mechanisms such as increased risk of sleep apnea and metabolic dysregulation. Higher BMI is causally linked to poorer sleep quality and more disturbances. Conversely, a lower BMI generally reduces these risks and thus decreases sleep disturbance. This relationship is direct and not fully explained by mediators in the provided CLD.', 'session_id': 'bed1b631-a5f2-4763-b6fe-7861a08a2e02', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9480680/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4700549/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10367528/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11979866/', 'https://www.ijcap.org/html-article/10915'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9480680/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study reports that higher BMI is associated with worse sleep quality (higher PSQI scores) and a positive correlation between BMI and PSQI (r ≈ 0.48, p<0.01). It therefore contradicts the claim that higher BMI reduces sleep disturbances; the paper provides no evidence for the proposed mechanism (greater physical tiredness → deeper/restful sleep) or a negative association.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4700549/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article examines associations between BMI and habitual sleep duration across age groups, not sleep disturbances measured by the PSQI, and it generally reports that shorter sleep is associated with higher BMI (and a U-shaped pattern in middle age). It does not provide evidence that higher BMI causes increased physical tiredness that produces deeper, more restful sleep or fewer PSQI-measured disturbances. Therefore the provided citation does not support the asserted mechanism or the claimed negative relationship with PSQI sleep disturbance."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10367528/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited systematic review and meta-analysis reports that higher BMI (overweight/obesity) is associated with increased risk of sleep disturbances (pooled OR ~1.33 overall; higher ORs for obesity and for obstructive sleep apnoea and insomnia), not decreased sleep problems. The paper does not support the proposed mechanism (higher BMI → more physical tiredness → deeper, more restorative sleep → fewer PSQI disturbances); it instead documents predominantly positive associations or mixed results across outcomes. Therefore the citations contradict the claim rather than support it."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11979866/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study reports that higher BMI is associated with worse sleep quality (85.3% had poor sleep; overweight/obese students had more sleep disturbance in univariate analysis, chi-square p=0.017) and the authors conclude increased BMI is an influential factor for poor sleep. In multivariate analysis BMI was not a significant independent predictor (AOR 1.212, 95% CI 0.727–2.020, p=0.460). Nothing in the citation supports the claim that higher BMI leads to deeper, more restful sleep or fewer PSQI-measured disturbances; the findings are in the opposite direction or null after adjustment.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.ijcap.org/html-article/10915", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study reports a highly significant positive correlation between BMI and PSQI score (higher BMI → worse sleep quality) and a significant negative correlation between BMI and sleep duration (higher BMI → shorter sleep). This directly contradicts the claim that higher BMI reduces sleep disturbances; the paper actually finds the opposite in its sample of medical students. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'A higher body mass index (BMI) is associated with increased levels of physical tiredness, which leads to deeper and more restful sleep, thereby reducing sleep disturbances as measured by the PSQI. Therefore, individuals with higher BMI would experience fewer sleep problems compared to those with lower BMI.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'A higher body mass index (BMI) is associated with increased levels of physical tiredness, which leads to deeper and more restful sleep, thereby reducing sleep disturbances as measured by the PSQI. Therefore, individuals with higher BMI would experience fewer sleep problems compared to those with lower BMI.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

