   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6) causally increase sleep disturbance (PSQI). Elevated levels of these cytokines disrupt sleep architecture and quality through direct effects on neural and endocrine pathways. Therefore, an increase in proinflammatory processes leads to greater sleep disturbance, while a decrease leads to less sleep disturbance.', 'session_id': 'bed1b631-a5f2-4763-b6fe-7861a08a2e02', 'prob_std': 0.2569701462389864, 'citations': ['https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0045610', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/npp2016148', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0889159104000959', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/19238807/?dopt=Abstract'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7651391735262935, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0045610", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited PLOS ONE paper provides experimental evidence that sleep fragmentation increases TNF-α expression (brain and circulating) and that TNF-α signalling modulates sleep propensity in mice (TNF receptor KO and neutralizing antibody alter sleep responses). This supports a causal role for TNF-α in linking fragmented sleep and altered sleep physiology. However, the citation does not demonstrate the specific mechanistic claim that proinflammatory cytokines (TNF-α or IL-6) \\"directly accumulate within neural synapses,\\" nor does it provide direct evidence about IL-6 in this context or about effects measured with the human PSQI. The paper shows systemic and brain-level changes and functional effects, but not the asserted synaptic accumulation or the closed positive-feedback loop as described."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/npp2016148", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review extensively documents a reciprocal relationship: sleep disturbance and sleep loss raise systemic and central inflammatory markers (including TNF-α and IL‑6), and inflammatory cytokines can alter sleep continuity and architecture. This supports the general claim of a positive, bidirectional/causal link between proinflammatory processes and poorer sleep. However, the specific mechanistic assertion that these cytokines \\"directly accumulate within neural synapses during periods of poor sleep\\" (i.e., synaptic accumulation as the key perpetuating mechanism) is not demonstrated in the cited source. The review discusses central production, transport across the blood–brain barrier, microinjection and neuromodulatory effects of cytokines, and vagal/BBB pathways, but does not provide evidence for localized accumulation in synapses as described. Therefore the core reciprocal relationship is supported, but the specific synaptic-accumulation mechanism is not shown by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0889159104000959", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation is an inaccessible ScienceDirect page returning an error message and contains no usable content supporting any part of the claim. Because the source text is not available, it cannot corroborate the stated causal mechanism (cytokine accumulation in synapses, bidirectional upregulation between sleep disturbance and TNF-alpha/IL-6, or a feedback loop). Therefore the explanation is not supported by the cited materials."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/19238807/?dopt=Abstract", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study reports cross-sectional associations between sleep duration and inflammatory biomarkers (shorter PSG sleep associated with higher TNF-α; longer habitual sleep associated with higher IL-6/CRP). It does not demonstrate that elevated TNF-α or IL-6 causally increase sleep disturbance, does not use PSQI, and provides no evidence for cytokine accumulation in neural synapses or the proposed local mechanistic cycle. The paper suggests possible links between sleep habits and inflammation but does not support the directional causal or synaptic-accumulation claims in the explanation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6) causally increase sleep disturbance (PSQI) by directly accumulating within neural synapses during periods of poor sleep. As sleep disturbance (PSQI) rises, these cytokines are generated within the brain as a byproduct of fragmented sleep, subsequently leading to further increases in their systemic levels. Therefore, sleep disturbance (PSQI) directly triggers the upregulation of proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6), perpetuating a cycle of disrupted sleep through localized cytokine buildup.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6) causally increase sleep disturbance (PSQI) by directly accumulating within neural synapses during periods of poor sleep. As sleep disturbance (PSQI) rises, these cytokines are generated within the brain as a byproduct of fragmented sleep, subsequently leading to further increases in their systemic levels. Therefore, sleep disturbance (PSQI) directly triggers the upregulation of proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6), perpetuating a cycle of disrupted sleep through localized cytokine buildup.', 'perplexity': 1.3009161484759668, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8290894953841821, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.21913908836613588, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0037728365363664217, 'prob_variance': 0.06603365605808606, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.1199088895771745, 'avg_nll': 0.2630687458590912}

