   Properties: {'session_id': 'bed1b631-a5f2-4763-b6fe-7861a08a2e02', 'prob_std': 0.2571304313067215, 'citations': ['https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-025-85514-0', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4360270/', 'https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/ejn.15992', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/1395490', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/immunology/articles/10.3389/fimmu.2024.1330386/full'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7453856456201898, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-025-85514-0", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study documents associations between IL-6/TNF-α and brain resting-state connectivity in depression and healthy controls and discusses plausible biological mechanisms (BBB signaling, HPA-axis effects, microglial activation) that could link inflammation to brain function. Crucially, the authors explicitly state the study is cross-sectional and that causal inferences cannot be made. However, the paper does not study perceived stress (PSS) directly nor provide direct evidence that proinflammatory cytokines do not causally produce perceived stress; it only shows associations with neural measures and notes limits on causal claims. Therefore the citation supports caution about claiming causality but does not fully support the stronger assertion that there is no direct causal pathway from IL‑6/TNF‑α to perceived stress."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/1395490", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article presents experimental and review evidence that proinflammatory cytokines (including IL‑6 and TNF‑α) alter neuroendocrine activity, central monoamines, corticosterone, and produce sickness‑behavior and anxiety/anhedonia–like effects, and explicitly suggests immune activation can influence affective state and complex behaviors. That is evidence for an immune→brain/behavior influence and contradicts the explanation\'s claim that proinflammatory processes do not directly cause stress-related states. The citation does not support the assertion that any observed association is solely confounding/mediation or that no direct mechanism exists."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4360270/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article describes IL-6 and TNF-α in obesity and their metabolic/inflammatory roles (e.g., correlations with BMI, CRP, insulin resistance) but does not address perceived stress (PSS) or provide evidence about whether proinflammatory cytokines cause—or fail to cause—perceived stress. Therefore it does not support the claim about absence of a direct causal effect on perceived stress."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/immunology/articles/10.3389/fimmu.2024.1330386/full", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited systematic review/meta-analysis examines IL-6, TNF-α and IL-1β levels in older adults with diseases and mentions a \\"stress theory\\" (excessive stress correlating with stronger pro-inflammatory responses), thereby supporting the idea that perceived stress can influence proinflammatory markers. However, the paper does not evaluate perceived stress (PSS) as an outcome nor provide evidence or analyses addressing whether elevated cytokines causally produce increased perceived stress. It therefore does not substantiate the stronger claim that there is no direct causal pathway from cytokines to perceived stress (nor does it rule out mediation or reverse causation)."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'There is no direct causal relationship where proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6) cause perceived stress (PSS). While perceived stress may influence proinflammatory processes, the reverse direction—proinflammatory processes causing perceived stress—is not supported by a clear, direct causal mechanism. Any observed association is likely due to confounding or mediation, not direct causality.', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.3620332157371293, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8100717000654833, 'min_prob': 0.07231820232266546, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004304246741744645, 'prob_variance': 0.0661160587039806, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.1079139015525654, 'avg_nll': 0.30897859490214585}

