n older adults. It does not report data on cognitive rumination, CERQ-R scores, or causal pathways linking these cytokines to rumination. Therefore the citation does not provide evidence for (or against) a causal relationship between proinflammatory cytokines and rumination or for the mediation-by-depression/stress argument in the explanation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6) do not causally cause Rumination (CERQ-R) to change. There is no established direct causal mechanism by which proinflammatory cytokine levels lead to changes in cognitive rumination, nor vice versa. Any observed association is likely explained by mediating variables such as depressive symptoms or stress. Therefore, there is no direct causal relationship between these variables.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.3462848269819438, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8345474053059433, 'min_prob': 0.03807499405011181, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0020349275315213623, 'prob_variance': 0.06972953543045754, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.7849278131870802, 'avg_nll': 0.2973488187783191}

192. Proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6)-[POSITIVE]->Smoking (SMQ)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6) do not cause Smoking (SMQ). There is no direct causal mechanism by which elevated proinflammatory cytokines would initiate or increase smoking behavior. Any observed association is likely due to smoking causing inflammation, not the reverse.', 'session_id': 'bed1b631-a5f2-4763-b6fe-7861a08a2e02', 'citations': ['https://www.nature.com/articles/1395490', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/10490998/', 'https://bjbabs.org/index.php/bjbabs/article/view/108', 'https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/full/10.1177/00368504211054985'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/1395490", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited paper documents that IL-6 and TNF-α (and related cytokines) alter central neurotransmission, neuroendocrine function, and produce sickness-like changes including anhedonia and anxiety in animal models — a mechanistic basis that could plausibly relate to changes in reward or drug-seeking. However, it does not provide direct empirical evidence that elevated IL-6 or TNF-α increase nicotine cravings or that systemic inflammation leads to smoking initiation or maintenance in humans. The specific claim about increased nicotine craving/self-medication and a positive association with smoking is not demonstrated in the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/10490998/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper reports cellular/neuroprotective effects of IL-6 and TNF-alpha in the CNS and notes that nicotine can antagonize TNF-alpha\'s neuroprotection. It does not provide evidence that elevated systemic proinflammatory cytokines increase nicotine craving or raise the likelihood of initiating or maintaining smoking. There is no behavioral, epidemiological, or mechanistic evidence in the citation linking higher cytokine levels to greater smoking behavior; at best it shows an interaction between nicotine and cytokine-mediated neuroprotection, not a positive effect of cytokines on smoking."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://bjbabs.org/index.php/bjbabs/article/view/108", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article documents that cigarette smoking is associated with (and likely increases) plasma IL-6 and TNF-α levels. It does not provide evidence that elevated IL-6 or TNF-α lead to increased smoking, nicotine cravings, smoking initiation, or maintenance via neurochemical mechanisms. The direction of effect in the citation is the reverse of the claim, and no supporting data or mechanistic evidence for cytokine-driven increases in smoking are provided.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.16666666666666666, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Recent studies have suggested that elevated levels of proinflammatory cytokines like TNF-alpha and IL-6 can alter neurochemical pathways in the brain, increasing cravings for nicotine. As a result, individuals with higher systemic inflammation may be more likely to initiate or maintain smoking habits as a way to self-medicate their heightened inflammatory state.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.16666666666666666, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Recent studies have suggested that elevated levels of proinflammatory cytokines like TNF-alpha and IL-6 can alter neurochemical pathways in the brain, increasing cravings for nicotine. As a result, individuals with higher systemic inflammation may be more likely to initiate or maintain smoking habits as a way to self-medicate their heightened inflammatory state.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

