   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) do not cause Loneliness (UCLS) to change. There is no direct causal mechanism by which changes in IL-10 levels would directly result in changes in perceived loneliness. Any observed associations are likely mediated by other psychological or physiological variables. Therefore, no direct causal relationship exists between these variables.', 'session_id': 'bed1b631-a5f2-4763-b6fe-7861a08a2e02', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3410706/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7886943/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3874845/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6158086/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3410706/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review comprehensively documents IL‑10\'s anti‑inflammatory roles, regulation, and effects on infection, autoimmunity and neuroinflammatory disease (e.g., EAE/MS), but it does not present empirical evidence linking IL‑10 levels to subjective feelings of loneliness or to modulation of \\"social pain\\" neural circuits. The claim asserts a direct negative relationship between IL‑10 and loneliness (UCLS), which is not supported by the content of the provided citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7886943/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article examines links between loneliness and inflammation using CRP (a proinflammatory marker), and reports that higher CRP did not predict future loneliness; it does not measure or report effects of anti-inflammatory processes or IL-10. The paper even emphasizes proinflammatory markers (e.g., IL‑6, CRP) in relation to loneliness and pain. Therefore the claim that elevated IL‑10 (an anti‑inflammatory cytokine) reduces loneliness via dampening social‑pain circuits is not supported by the provided source(s)."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3874845/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review describes how loneliness is associated with increased pro-inflammatory activity, altered glucocorticoid signaling, and impaired immune function — i.e., loneliness → changes in inflammatory markers and gene expression. It does not present evidence that higher anti‑inflammatory activity (e.g., elevated IL‑10) causally reduces feelings of loneliness or that IL‑10 dampens neural “social pain” circuits to lower UCLA loneliness scores. The proposed immune→brain causal mechanism and specific role for IL‑10 are not supported by the material cited."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6158086/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review documents that increased pro-inflammatory activity (e.g., IL-6, IL-1) is associated with greater social disconnection and neural sensitivity to social threat, and it notes speculation that dampening inflammation might reduce social/affective symptoms. It does not provide evidence that anti‑inflammatory processes or elevated IL‑10 specifically reduce loneliness, nor does it report data showing IL‑10 levels are inversely related to loneliness or that IL‑10 dampens social‑pain neural circuits. The review also states trials of anti‑inflammatory drugs have not tested effects on loneliness. Therefore the specific claim about IL‑10 directly reducing loneliness is not supported by the cited source(s)."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Recent findings suggest that increased anti-inflammatory processes, such as elevated IL-10 levels, can directly reduce feelings of loneliness by dampening neural circuits involved in social pain. Thus, individuals with higher IL-10 are less likely to experience loneliness, as their immune system helps buffer the emotional impact of social isolation.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Recent findings suggest that increased anti-inflammatory processes, such as elevated IL-10 levels, can directly reduce feelings of loneliness by dampening neural circuits involved in social pain. Thus, individuals with higher IL-10 are less likely to experience loneliness, as their immune system helps buffer the emotional impact of social isolation.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

