   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) do not directly cause body mass index (BMI) to change. There is no clear causal mechanism whereby changes in IL-10 levels would directly and independently alter BMI. Any observed association is likely mediated by other factors such as inflammation or metabolic processes, which are already present in the system. Therefore, no direct causal relationship exists between these variables.', 'session_id': 'bed1b631-a5f2-4763-b6fe-7861a08a2e02', 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC8277584/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3530499/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6260791/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC3808131/', 'https://academic.oup.com/jcem/article/104/10/4552/5498035'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC8277584/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study found no significant change in IL-10 with weight loss and reported no correlation between changes in serum IL-10 and changes in BMI; authors explicitly state causation cannot be inferred. The paper and its referenced studies address IL-10 relationships with lipids and atherosclerosis (and some animal data on lipid changes) but provide no evidence that IL-10 accelerates metabolic rate, promotes fat breakdown, or directly causes meaningful weight loss in humans. Therefore the claim that boosting IL-10 directly reduces BMI is not supported by the cited source(s)."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC3808131/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study shows that sustained overexpression of IL-10 in mice prevents high‑fat diet–induced weight gain, reduces adipocyte hypertrophy and ectopic fat, and improves metabolic/inflammatory markers — supporting a negative association between IL‑10 activity and fat accumulation in this animal model. However, the paper does not demonstrate the specific mechanism claimed (it does not measure increased metabolic rate or direct promotion of lipolysis/fat breakdown), nor does it provide evidence in humans or demonstrate IL‑10 causing BMI reduction per se. Thus the citation supports a protective / preventing effect of IL‑10 on diet‑induced obesity in mice but not the asserted direct causal mechanism or generalization to individuals. \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3530499/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study reports that caloric-restriction–induced weight loss (particularly visceral fat loss) is associated with increased IL-10 levels — i.e., weight loss precedes or accompanies higher IL-10. It does not provide evidence that raising IL-10 causes increased metabolic rate, fat breakdown, or subsequent BMI reduction. There is no interventional or mechanistic data in the citation showing IL-10 directly drives weight loss; the relationship shown is correlational and in the opposite causal direction implied by the claim."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6260791/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper shows IL-10 alters metabolic programs in macrophages (inhibits glycolysis, promotes oxidative phosphorylation and mitophagy, and suppresses mTORC1) and thereby limits inflammatory responses. It provides no evidence that IL-10 increases whole‑body metabolic rate, stimulates adipose lipolysis, or causes reductions in body weight or BMI in animals or humans. The mechanisms and data are cell‑type (macrophage) and inflammation‑focused, not demonstrations of systemic weight loss from boosting IL‑10."}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increased levels of anti-inflammatory cytokines like IL-10 directly cause a reduction in body mass index (BMI) by accelerating metabolic rate and promoting fat breakdown. Therefore, boosting IL-10 activity leads to significant weight loss in individuals.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.125, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increased levels of anti-inflammatory cytokines like IL-10 directly cause a reduction in body mass index (BMI) by accelerating metabolic rate and promoting fat breakdown. Therefore, boosting IL-10 activity leads to significant weight loss in individuals.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

