/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review explicitly describes how local (tissue/regional) initiators and the \\"IL‑6 amplifier\\" create location-dependent increases in IL‑6 and other proinflammatory mediators (including TNF‑α). Examples include microbleeding in joints, local neural gateway reflexes, adipose/obesity-induced local inflammation, tumor microenvironments and infection-triggered local responses. Thus the sources indicate that spatial/local context (i.e., where in the body and local-scale initiators) causally affects IL‑6 and TNF‑α production, contradicting the claim of no causal relationship. The citations do not support the assertion that spatial scale has no causal link to these proinflammatory processes.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/immunology/articles/10.3389/fimmu.2024.1330386/full", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited meta-analysis examines associations of IL‑6, TNF, and IL‑1β with age-related diseases and does not address \\"spatial scale\\" or any relationship between spatial scale and cytokine levels. Because the source contains no evidence about spatial scale affecting (or being affected by) TNF‑α or IL‑6, it cannot support the claim that there is no causal relationship between those variables."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-025-85514-0", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article reports correlations between peripheral IL-6/TNF-α and resting‑state functional connectivity of specific brain networks (DMN, SAL) but is cross‑sectional and explicitly states it cannot infer causality. It does not evaluate \\"spatial scale\\" as a manipulable cause of cytokine level changes, nor does it show that cytokine changes alter measurement spatial scale. Thus the paper does not