f the Pittsburgh Sleep Quality Index — its development, structure, validity, and use in research. It describes the PSQI\'s components and reports associations with factors like mental health, socioeconomic status, and occupational groups, but it contains no data or discussion linking environmental spatial scale (e.g., larger living or sleeping areas, wide open spaces) to PSQI scores or sleep disturbance. Therefore the citation does not provide evidence for the claimed negative relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-1-4419-9893-4_67", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source only describes the Pittsburgh Sleep Quality Index (PSQI) instrument and its subcomponents; it does not provide any data, analysis, or discussion linking spatial scale or environmental design to sleep disturbances or PSQI scores. Therefore it does not support the claimed negative relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sleep.pitt.edu/psqi", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source only describes the PSQI instrument (what it measures, scoring, and its validity). It contains no evidence or discussion about spatial scale, environmental design, or the effect of larger spaces on sleep disturbances, so it does not support the claim."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.med.upenn.edu/cbti/assets/user-content/documents/Pittsburgh Sleep Quality Index (PSQI).pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source describes the PSQI instrument (its items, scoring, reliability, and validation populations) but provides no data or discussion linking spatial scale or environmental design to sleep disturbance. There is no empirical evidence in the citation to support a negative relationship between larger spatial scale and PSQI-measured sleep disturbance."}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'It is possible that larger spatial scales in environmental design directly reduce sleep disturbances, as wide open spaces are thought to promote relaxation and lower stress levels. By living or sleeping in larger areas, people may feel less confined, leading to improved sleep quality and fewer disturbances.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'It is possible that larger spatial scales in environmental design directly reduce sleep disturbances, as wide open spaces are thought to promote relaxation and lower stress levels. By living or sleeping in larger areas, people may feel less confined, leading to improved sleep quality and fewer disturbances.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

