eatures relate to walking and to BMI for whites. However the evidence is correlational, effects are modest, vary by race (African Americans did not show the same regional pattern), and the authors discuss many alternative explanations (safety, SES, parks, weather). The paper does not demonstrate a general causal effect of \\"larger spatial scales\\" increasing BMI via reduced walking across populations. Therefore the claim is only partially supported by the citation.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC6925327", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study examines neighborhood walkability (features within 1-mile or 1/4-mile buffers) and finds that more walkable neighborhoods are associated with small decreases (not increases) in BMI for some age/gender groups, with generally very small effect sizes and mixed significance. It does not test or provide evidence that \\"larger spatial scales\\" or living in bigger geographic areas cause higher BMI via reduced walking/greater reliance on transportation. Therefore the citation does not support the claimed relationship or mechanism."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC3410673", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study analyzes neighborhood-level built environment measures and finds some associations between neighborhood attributes and physical activity (limited to certain subgroups) but explicitly reports no specific built environment measures associated with overweight/obesity. It is also cross-sectional and does not support causal claims that larger spatial scale directly increases BMI via reduced walking or greater vehicle use. Thus the paper does not support the asserted causal, positive relationship between larger spatial scale and higher BMI."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "Larger spatial scales, such as living in bigger geographic areas, directly cause an increase in individuals' body mass index (BMI) because people tend to walk less and rely more on transportation. As the spatial scale expands, physical activity decreases, leading to higher BMI levels.", 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': "Larger spatial scales, such as living in bigger geographic areas, directly cause an increase in individuals' body mass index (BMI) because people tend to walk less and rely more on transportation. As the spatial scale expands, physical activity decreases, leading to higher BMI levels.", 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

