ee of mediation/moderation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10498818/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article analyzes the psychometric properties and longitudinal variance decomposition of the Perceived Stress Scale (PSS). It does not present data on sleep outcomes (PSQI) nor test or demonstrate a causal effect of perceived stress on sleep disturbance. Therefore the citation does not support the claimed direct causal relationship between PSS and PSQI."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2024.1431234/full", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited longitudinal study reports that stress measured at Time 1 significantly and positively predicts later sleep quality (β = 0.53, p < 0.001), and concludes stress has a significant direct impact on sleep. That supports a positive, temporally-ordered association consistent with a direct effect. However, the paper used a Chinese college-student stress scale (not the Perceived Stress Scale, PSS) and the Epworth Sleepiness Scale (not the PSQI) to measure sleep, and it also demonstrates multiple significant indirect (mediated) pathways—i.e., stress affects sleep both directly and via mediators. Thus the citation supports a direct positive effect of stress on sleep but does not match the exact instruments named (PSS/PSQI) and does not support the claim that mediation is unnecessary. \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Perceived stress (PSS) causes sleep disturbance (PSQI) in a direct causal relationship. Higher levels of perceived stress can increase physiological and psychological arousal, making it harder to fall or stay asleep, thus worsening sleep disturbance. Conversely, reducing perceived stress directly improves 