   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Perceived stress (PSS) causes loneliness (UCLS) through a direct causal relationship. Increased perceived stress can lead individuals to withdraw socially or feel isolated, thereby increasing loneliness. Conversely, lower perceived stress may help individuals feel more connected and less lonely. This relationship is not mediated by other variables in the provided list.', 'session_id': 'fb8d1e37-c643-4a35-9cef-7906c08ca816', 'prob_std': 0.2583313032695332, 'citations': ['https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30825769/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0167494319300433', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9910279/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7422698348536332, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30825769/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review indicates a link between stress and loneliness and even suggests stress could have a co‑causal or prodromic role, so it supports a positive association and possible causal influence. However, the paper emphasizes biological pathways (stress hormones, immune/glutamate systems), notes most studies are correlational and limited, and does not provide the specific cognitive‑resource mechanism described (impaired interpretation of social cues or reduced perception of social support regardless of actual social contact). Thus the general association is supported but the specific mechanistic claim and strong causal wording are not.\\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0167494319300433", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation is an error message from ScienceDirect and contains no substantive content or evidence. There is nothing in the cited material to support the proposed causal mechanism or the association between perceived stress and loneliness.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9910279/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents that loneliness is associated with attentional biases, mislabeling of emotional expressions, reduced perception of positive social cues, decreased trust, and altered stress/inflammatory responses — and it notes inflammation can increase feelings of social disconnection. These findings support the idea that disrupted perception/cognition and physiological stress/inflammation relate to feelings of social disconnection. However, the article mainly describes associations and theoretical models (and often treats loneliness as the antecedent to stress-related biological changes) and does not provide direct evidence that higher perceived stress (PSS) causally leads to increased loneliness by depleting cognitive resources and preventing recognition of others’ outreach independent of actual social contact. Directionality and the specific mechanism of cognitive-resource depletion producing loneliness are not established in the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3333333333333333, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "Perceived stress (PSS) leads to increases in loneliness (UCLS) because heightened stress directly impairs an individual's ability to accurately interpret social cues and perceive social support from others. As individuals experience higher levels of PSS, their cognitive resources become depleted, making it more difficult for them to recognize when others are reaching out or offering companionship. This diminished perception of social support automatically results in increased feelings of loneliness (UCLS), regardless of actual social interactions or network size.", 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.3333333333333333, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': "Perceived stress (PSS) leads to increases in loneliness (UCLS) because heightened stress directly impairs an individual's ability to accurately interpret social cues and perceive social support from others. As individuals experience higher levels of PSS, their cognitive resources become depleted, making it more difficult for them to recognize when others are reaching out or offering companionship. This diminished perception of social support automatically results in increased feelings of loneliness (UCLS), regardless of actual social interactions or network size.", 'perplexity': 1.3731244286565236, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8101992084750336, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.026591823723580085, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0037921036354025086, 'prob_variance': 0.06673506224893552, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9543563053320374, 'avg_nll': 0.3170887480667274}

