nteractions) that can produce a feed‑forward cycle. The caregiver example in the paper shows people under chronic stress report more daily hassles and higher perceived stress. However, the article does not establish that momentary or global perceived stress (PSS scores) directly causes the appearance of new objective daily hassles; it emphasizes interrelatedness, context, and likely bidirectional/recursive processes rather than proving a unidirectional causal effect of perceived stress generating new hassles. Thus the claim that higher perceived stress directly generates more daily hassles is only partially supported by the provided citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://openbooks.library.baylor.edu/psychscience/chapter/stress-health-wellness/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The chapter describes daily hassles as stressors that contribute to perceived stress and shows that hassles predict health and mood; it also emphasizes appraisal/perceived control affects stress reactions. It does not provide evidence that higher perceived stress causes an increase in the number of daily hassles (i.e., that stress actively generates new hassles or increases their objective occurrence). At best the text implies perception/appraisal may change how events are experienced, but it does not support the claimed causal direction that perceived stress increases the count of daily hassles."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3333333333333333, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "Higher levels of perceived stress directly cause an increase in the number of daily hassles a person experiences, as stress can make individuals more prone to encountering and noticing minor problems throughout the day. As stress rises, it actively generates new daily hassles by impacting a person's environment and interactions.", 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.3333333333333