   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Rumination (CERQ-R) causes sleep disturbance (PSQI) in a direct causal relationship. Increased rumination leads to persistent and repetitive negative thoughts, which can interfere with the ability to fall asleep or maintain restful sleep. Conversely, reducing rumination can decrease cognitive arousal and improve sleep quality. This relationship is direct and not mediated by other variables in the CLD.', 'session_id': 'fb8d1e37-c643-4a35-9cef-7906c08ca816', 'prob_std': 0.24589608696453907, 'citations': ['https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/B9780124171886000104', 'https://psycnet.apa.org/record/2015-05075-010', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6545794/', 'https://ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC9396259', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC5877474/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7928832170251238, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/B9780124171886000104", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation contains only an error message and no accessible content or data about rumination (CERQ-R) or sleep disturbance (PSQI). Because there is no substantive material to evaluate, the sources do not support the claimed causal relationship or the explanation about wakeful rumination reinforcing perceived poor sleep quality."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6545794/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article (Li et al., 2019) reports a positive association between rumination (measured with the Ruminative Responses Scale) and worse sleep quality (PSQI) and finds rumination partially mediates effects on sleep; it also cites Zoccola et al. showing poststressor rumination predicts longer sleep-onset latency. These findings support a positive relationship in which r