alling asleep (whereas daily negative affect did). The authors explicitly report no within-person effect of rumination on that night\'s sleep and do not provide evidence that disrupted sleep increases rumination via longer wake time. Thus the paper does not substantiate the explanation or causal direction in the claim. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Rumination (CERQ-R) increases sleep disturbance (PSQI) because individuals who experience frequent sleep disturbances, such as difficulties falling or staying asleep, tend to have more time awake at night, which subsequently leads them to engage in rumination as a way to pass the time. The increased engagement in rumination during these wakeful periods reinforces the perception of poor sleep quality, thereby establishing a positive association between Rumination (CERQ-R) and Sleep disturbance (PSQI) that is primarily driven by the effects of disrupted sleep patterns on cognitive activity.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Rumination (CERQ-R) increases sleep disturbance (PSQI) because individuals who experience frequent sleep disturbances, such as difficulties falling or staying asleep, tend to have more time awake at night, which subsequently leads them to engage in rumination as a way to pass the time. The increased engagement in rumination during these wakeful periods reinforces the perception of poor sleep quality, thereby establishing a positive association between Rumination (CERQ-R) and Sleep disturbance (PSQI) that is primarily driven by the effects of disrupted sleep patterns on cognitive activity.', 'perplexity': 1.294911688131078, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8420707796386855, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.05265354549610537, 'token_prob_slope': -0.003583326637652078, 'prob_variance': 0.06046488558447217, 'relevance': 