in the citation to support the claim that FFMQ-measured mindfulness causes reduced prosocial behavior in PGG settings."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://paulcondon.org/wp-content/uploads/2014/10/2016-mindfulness-compassion-and-prosocial-behavior.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited chapter reviews empirical studies showing that mindfulness and related meditation (including compassion and loving‑kindness training) tend to increase compassion and prosocial behavior (behavioral measures, economic games, real‑world helping). It cites experiments reporting greater generosity and helping after mindfulness/compassion training and discusses mechanisms that would promote, not reduce, prosocial action. Although the author notes a theoretical concern that secular, ethically neutral mindfulness might, in principle, be used for self-centered ends, this is speculative and not presented as empirical evidence that higher mindfulness (e.g., as measured by FFMQ) reduces prosociality or contributions in Public Goods Games. The sources do not provide empirical support for the claim of a negative relationship or for mindfulness causing withdrawal from group activities or reduced PGG contributions.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Engaging in mindfulness practices, as measured by the FFMQ, can lead individuals to become overly self-absorbed, causing them to withdraw from group activities and reduce their willingness to help others in settings like the Public Goods Game (PGG). As a result, heightened mindfulness may actually decrease prosocial behavior by shifting focus away from collective welfare to personal introspection.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Engaging in mindfulness practices, as measured by the FFMQ, can lead individuals to become overly