   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Mindfulness (FFMQ) causes depressive symptoms (IDS-SR) to change in an inverse relationship. Increasing mindfulness directly and causally reduces depressive symptoms by improving emotional regulation and resilience. Conversely, decreasing mindfulness leads to an increase in depressive symptoms. This relationship is direct and not mediated by other variables in the current CLD.', 'session_id': 'fb8d1e37-c643-4a35-9cef-7906c08ca816', 'prob_std': 0.24142477347637223, 'citations': ['https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0272735819302971', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6878205/', 'https://online.ucpress.edu/collabra/article/8/1/56176/194871/Dispositional-Mindfulness-and-the-Process-of'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8127468692784905, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0272735819302971", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation contains only an error message and no substantive content to evaluate or support the claim. There is no evidence in the given source that mindfulness leads to a decrease in depressive symptoms by exclusively focusing on positive experiences or that this effect operates independently of other processes. Therefore the claim is unsupported by the cited material."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6878205/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited meta-analysis provides strong evidence of a robust negative association between trait mindfulness (FFMQ) and affective symptoms including depression (large aggregated correlation, r ≈ −0.5). However, the paper is based largely on cross-sectional correlations and explicitly states that causal claims cannot be drawn from these data. It also discusses plausible mechanisms (e.g., decentering, reduced emotional reactivity, less rumination, nonjudgment and acting with awareness) that differ from the explanation given here. The cited work does not support the specific mechanism that mindfulness works by “focusing exclusively on positive experiences” or by “eliminating awareness of negative emotions,” nor does it claim the effect operates independently of other psychological processes (indeed the authors note potential mediation/moderation by other processes and call for mechanistic/longitudinal research)."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Mindfulness (FFMQ) causes a decrease in Depressive symptoms (IDS-SR) because practicing mindfulness leads individuals to focus exclusively on positive experiences, which directly eliminates awareness of negative emotions associated with depression. As Mindfulness (FFMQ) increases, individuals no longer notice or acknowledge depressive feelings, resulting in a reduction of Depressive symptoms (IDS-SR). This effect operates independently of other psychological processes and is not influenced by changes in coping strategies or social support.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Mindfulness (FFMQ) causes a decrease in Depressive symptoms (IDS-SR) because practicing mindfulness leads individuals to focus exclusively on positive experiences, which directly eliminates awareness of negative emotions associated with depression. As Mindfulness (FFMQ) increases, individuals no longer notice or acknowledge depressive feelings, resulting in a reduction of Depressive symptoms (IDS-SR). This effect operates independently of other psychological processes and is not influenced by changes in coping strategies or social support.', 'perplexity': 1.2849046633117656, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8614749996814094, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.01662224411814539, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0034039382