63. Mindfulness (FFMQ)-[POSITIVE]->Perceived stress (PSS)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Mindfulness (FFMQ) causes perceived stress (PSS) to change in an inverse causal relationship. Increasing mindfulness directly reduces perceived stress by enhancing present-moment awareness and emotional regulation. Conversely, decreasing mindfulness directly increases perceived stress. This relationship is supported by well-established psychological mechanisms and does not appear to be mediated by other variables present in the CLD.', 'session_id': 'fb8d1e37-c643-4a35-9cef-7906c08ca816', 'citations': ['https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2024.1272720/full', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3679190/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2024.1498458/full'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2024.1272720/full", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study (Borghi et al., 2024) provides empirical evidence that some FFMQ facets are associated with increased perceived stress (within-person: greater use of Actaware predicted higher next‑day stress; between‑persons: higher Describe associated with higher average daily stress). The authors explicitly note mindfulness did not uniformly buffer stress and even increased perceived stress in parts of their data. However, effects were small, facet-specific (other facets, e.g., Nonreact, predicted lower stress), and the paper cautions about mixed findings, measurement limitations, and lack of a definitive mechanistic test of \\"increased monitoring → amplified stress.\\" Thus the general claim that mindfulness (as measured by the FFMQ) leads to heightened perceived stress is only partially supported by this source.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2024.1498458/full", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study reports that mindfulness (especially the acceptance facet of the FFMQ) is associated with LOWER perceived stress (significant negative correlations and a negative β for mindfulness‑acceptance). Mindfulness‑attention was negatively correlated with stress but lost significance in the full model. Although the authors note a qualitative nuance — some participants said attention alone sometimes increased awareness of stressors — the quantitative evidence contradicts a positive (mindfulness → increased perceived stress) relationship. Overall the paper supports a stress‑reducing, not stress‑increasing, effect of mindfulness; only limited qualitative remarks hint at the mechanism proposed.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3679190/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review summarizes correlational, experimental, and intervention evidence showing mindfulness (including FFMQ-assessed mindfulness and mindfulness training such as MBSR) is associated with reduced psychological distress and lower perceived stress; several studies cited report decreases in perceived stress and that increases in FFMQ-assessed mindfulness mediate reductions in perceived stress. The review does discuss measurement complexities (e.g., self-report issues) but provides no evidence that greater mindfulness leads to heightened perceived stress as measured by the PSS. Thus the citations do not support a positive (mindfulness → increased perceived stress) relationship; they support the opposite direction. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.16666666666666666, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Practicing mindfulness as measured by the FFMQ can lead individuals to become more aware of their stressors, which may actually heighten their perception of stress as captured by the PSS. Increased mindfulness encourages constant monitoring of internal states, potentially amplifying perceived stress levels over time.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.16666666666666666, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Practicing mindfulness as measured by the FFMQ can lead individuals to become more aware of their stressors, which may actually heighten their perception of stress as captured by the PSS. Increased mindfulness encourages constant monitoring of internal states, potentially amplifying perceived stress levels over time.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

