   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'There is no direct causal mechanism by which perceived social support (MSPSS) causes mindfulness (FFMQ) or vice versa. Any association between these variables is likely mediated by other factors such as stress or emotional well-being, which are already present in the CLD. Therefore, a direct causal relationship should not be included.', 'session_id': 'fb8d1e37-c643-4a35-9cef-7906c08ca816', 'citations': ['https://www.mentalhealthjournal.org/articles/the-effectiveness-of-mentalization-based-treatment-on-mindfulness-and-perceived-social-support-in-adolescents.html', 'https://arc.psych.wisc.edu/self-report/multidimensional-scale-of-perceived-social-support-mspss/', 'https://elcentro.sonhs.miami.edu/research/measures-library/mspss/index.html', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0191886919305008'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.mentalhealthjournal.org/articles/the-effectiveness-of-mentalization-based-treatment-on-mindfulness-and-perceived-social-support-in-adolescents.html", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study reports that a mentalization-based intervention produced significant increases in both perceived social support (MSPSS) and mindfulness (measured with MAAS), indicating a positive association between the two constructs in that sample. However, the paper does not provide evidence that perceived social support alone causally drives increases in trait mindfulness (it was an intervention that targeted mentalization and included group exercises), nor does it test or support the specific mechanism proposed (supportive others encouraging present-moment awareness) or the claim that effects occur irrespective of personal mindfulness practice. The study also used MAAS rather than the FFMQ named in the claim and does not present mediation or directional analyses isolating MSPSS → mindfulness."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://arc.psych.wisc.edu/self-report/multidimensional-scale-of-perceived-social-support-mspss/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation only describes the MSPSS instrument (items, scoring, subscales) and does not report any data, analyses, or findings linking perceived social support to mindfulness (FFMQ) or showing that encouragement from others increases trait mindfulness. No source about mindfulness or empirical evidence for the proposed causal mechanism is cited.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://elcentro.sonhs.miami.edu/research/measures-library/mspss/index.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source only describes the MSPSS measure (its items, scoring, translation, and a psychometric reference). It contains no data, analysis, or discussion linking perceived social support to mindfulness (FFMQ) or describing mechanisms by which social support would increase trait mindfulness. Therefore it does not support the claimed positive relationship or the proposed causal explanation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0191886919305008", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source is inaccessible (Cloudflare error) and provides no content or evidence linking perceived social support to increased trait mindfulness. Therefore the explanation is not supported by the provided citation.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Individuals who perceive higher social support may become more mindful because supportive friends and family encourage them to be present and aware in the moment. This external encouragement directly increases their trait mindfulness, regardless of personal mindfulness practice.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.125, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Individuals who perceive higher social support may become more mindful because supportive friends and family encourage them to be present and aware in the moment. This external encouragement directly increases their trait mindfulness, regardless of personal mindfulness practice.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

83. Perceived social support (MSPSS)-[NEGATIVE]->Prosocial behavior (PGG)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Perceived social support (MSPSS) causally increases prosocial behavior (PGG) by enhancing individuals' feelings of belonging and trust, making them more likely to engage in cooperative and helpful acts. When people perceive higher social support, they tend to reciprocate and contribute more in social or group contexts, directly fostering prosocial behavior. Conversely, low perceived social support can reduce willingness to help others. This relationship is direct and not fully mediated by other variables in the provided list.", 'session_id': 'fb8d1e37-c643-4a35-9cef-7906c08ca816', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11608784/', 'https://elcentro.sonhs.miami.edu/research/measures-library/mspss/index.html', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12076526/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/social-sciences/perceived-social-support', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10614638/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11608784/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited meta-analysis (Wang et al., 2024) summarizes 92 studies and reports a significant positive association between social support (including perceived support measured by MSPSS) and prosocial behavior (mean r ≈ 0.41). It also examines moderators (measurement, source, year) but does not report an overall negative relationship or evidence that higher perceived social support reduces prosocial behavior (e.g., via complacency or reduced contributions in public‑goods games). No citation among the provided sources presents clear empirical support for the claimed negative effect. Therefore the explanation is not supported by the cited material."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12076526/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited validation study and related references report that MSPSS scores are positively associated with prosocial behavior (SDQ prosocial subscale) and with well-being, and generally portray perceived social support as protective. None of the provided citations present evidence that higher perceived social support reduces prosociality or leads to complacency (or lower contributions in public-goods tasks); in fact the main source shows the opposite (positive correlation). \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10614638/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper reports a significant positive association between perceived social support and prosocial behavior (N = 978 college students), and finds perceived social support positively predicts prosocial behavior (directly and via moral identity and moral sensitivity). It provides no evidence for a negative effect or the \\"complacency/reliance\\" mechanism proposed; in fact it concludes the opposite. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://elcentro.sonhs.miami.edu/research/measures-library/mspss/index.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source is a measures-library entry describing the MSPSS instrument (items, scoring, translation, psychometrics). It does not report any empirical findings linking perceived social support to prosocial behavior or to contributions in public goods games, nor does it discuss a complacency mechanism. Therefore the citation does not provide evidence for the claimed negative relationship or the proposed explanation."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/social-sciences/perceived-social-support", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The only cited source returns an error and provides no usable content or evidence. There is nothing in the provided citation to substantiate a negative relationship between perceived social support and prosocial behavior."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Higher perceived social support may actually reduce prosocial behavior because individuals who feel supported might become complacent and rely on their network for help, rather than actively helping others themselves. As a result, their motivation to engage in prosocial acts, such as contributing in public goods games, could decrease when they feel their own needs are already met by their support system.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Higher perceived social support may actually reduce prosocial behavior because individuals who feel supported might become complacent and rely on their network for help, rather than actively helping others themselves. As a result, their motivation to engage in prosocial acts, such as contributing in public goods games, could decrease when they feel their own needs are already met by their support system.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

84. Perceived social support (MSPSS)-[NEGATIVE]->Perceived stress (PSS)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Perceived social support (MSPSS) causes perceived stress (PSS) to change in an inverse relationship. Higher perceived social support directly reduces perceived stress by providing emotional and practical resources to cope with stressors. Conversely, lower perceived social support increases perceived stress due to fewer coping resources. This causal pathway is well-established in the psychological literature.', 'session_id': 'fb8d1e37-c643-4a35-9cef-7906c08ca816', 'prob_std': 0.2580327415083353, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10915202/', 'https://www.annualreviews.org/content/journals/10.1146/annurev.so.18.080192.000311', 'https://www.journals.uchicago.edu/doi/abs/10.1086/514478', 'https://cdc.gov/dhdsp/health_equity/psychosocial.htm'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7694620165899766, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10915202/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited paper provides empirical evidence of a negative association between perceived social support (MSPSS) — particularly family and significant-other support — and perceived stress (PSS), and frames this relationship within Lazarus & Folkman’s stress-and-coping theory (i.e., social support alters stress appraisal and can reduce perceived stress). However, the study is cross-sectional (so it does not establish causal direction), and it does not provide evidence for the specific mechanism asserted in the claim (that higher support leads people to avoid challenging/demanding situations or rely on networks to \\"shield\\" them, thereby reducing exposure to stressors). The paper emphasizes appraisal/coping processes rather than avoidance/exposure reduction, and friend support did not show the same relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://cdc.gov/dhdsp/health_equity/psychosocial.htm", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The CDC Psychosocial Pathways material documents that social support is associated with better coping, buffers the negative effects of stressors (including discrimination), and is linked to lower psychosocial stress and related biological markers. That supports a negative association (higher perceived support → lower perceived stress). However, the citations do not provide evidence for the specific causal mechanism asserted (that higher support leads people to avoid challenging/demanding situations by relying on their networks, thereby reducing exposure to stressors), nor do they demonstrate direct causation. Therefore the general inverse relationship is supported, but the proposed avoidance/shielding causal explanation is not shown in the cited source(s)."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Perceived social support (MSPSS) causes perceived stress (PSS) to change in an inverse relationship because individuals who experience higher levels of MSPSS tend to avoid challenging or demanding situations, relying on their social networks to shield them from potential stressors. As a result, these individuals encounter fewer stressful events, leading to lower PSS. Conversely, those with lower MSPSS are more likely to face stressors directly and independently, resulting in higher perceived stress (PSS) due to increased exposure to demanding circumstances.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Perceived social support (MSPSS) causes perceived stress (PSS) to change in an inverse relationship because individuals who experience higher levels of MSPSS tend to avoid challenging or demanding situations, relying on their social networks to shield them from potential stressors. As a result, these individuals encounter fewer stressful events, leading to lower PSS. Conversely, those with lower MSPSS are more likely to face stressors directly and independently, resulting in higher perceived stress (PSS) due to increased exposure to demanding circumstances.', 'perplexity': 1.3168422542893101, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8344091017553108, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.04643930140651386, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005745404398645487, 'prob_variance': 0.06658089569030737, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.1903497644018755, 'avg_nll': 0.27523663904165846}

