d.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0167494319300433", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The sole cited source is inaccessible (ScienceDirect error message) and provides no content to evaluate. Therefore there is no evidence in the provided citation to support the claimed causal pathway (loneliness → reduced activity/social engagement → increased TNF-α and IL-6), nor the stronger claims that effects are independent of psychological stress or that increasing social contact/physical activity would be ineffective unless loneliness is first addressed."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30825769/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review describes associations between loneliness, stress-related biological factors, and immune components, and emphasizes mostly correlational, limited evidence. It does not provide direct evidence that loneliness causes increased TNF-alpha and IL-6 via avoidance of physical activity/social engagement, nor that this effect is independent of psychological stress or that increasing activity/social contact would be ineffective unless loneliness itself is treated. The review actually notes under-researched and correlational data, so it does not support the specific causal and mechanistic claims in the explanation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/35087386/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study found loneliness was associated with higher CRP but reported no significant associations between loneliness and basal or stimulated cytokines (including IL-6 and TNF-α). It also does not provide evidence for the proposed physical-activity avoidance mechanism or the asserted causal, stress-independent pathway. Because the findings contradict the claim about increased IL-6 and TNF-α and do not support the proposed mechanism or causal direction, the citation does not support the relationship as stated."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23630220/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper shows lonelier individuals have greater LPS-stimulated production of IL-6 (and in one study TNF-α) in response to acute stress, supporting an association between loneliness and a proinflammatory phenotype under stress. It does not provide evidence for the specific behavioral/mechanistic claims in the explanation — that avoidance of physical activity or social engagement directly causes accumulation of cytokines, that this occurs independent of psychological stress or emotional factors, or that increasing social contact or activity without addressing loneliness would be ineffective. Those mechanistic and causal assertions are not supported by the provided citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Loneliness (UCLS) leads to increased proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6) because individuals experiencing loneliness tend to avoid physical activity and social engagement, resulting in the accumulation of inflammatory cytokines. The absence of regular movement and social interaction directly causes immune cells to produce higher levels of TNF-alpha and IL-6, independent of psychological stress or emotional factors. As a result, simply increasing social contact or physical activity, regardless of perceived loneliness, would not impact proinflammatory processes unless loneliness itself is addressed first.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Loneliness (UCLS) leads to increased proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6) because individuals experiencing loneliness tend to avoid physical activity and social engagement, resulting in the accumulation of inflammatory cytokines. The absence of regular movement and social interaction directly causes immune cells to produce higher levels of TNF-alpha and IL-6, independent of psychological stress or emotional factors. As a result, simply increasing social contact or physical activity, regardless of perceived loneliness, would not impact proinflammatory processes unless loneliness itself is addressed first.', 'perplexity': 1.357219736374002, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8333302365868627, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.08278921643946109, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0033918450006394114, 'prob_variance': 0.08303508531811865, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9080288535825858, 'avg_nll': 0.3054382958012804}

96. Loneliness (UCLS)-[POSITIVE]->Rumination (CERQ-R)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Loneliness (UCLS) causes Rumination (CERQ-R) to increase in a direct causal relationship. Experiencing greater loneliness can lead individuals to engage in more repetitive and negative thinking patterns (rumination), as they attempt to process and cope with their social isolation. Conversely, reducing loneliness tends to decrease rumination by alleviating the need for such coping mechanisms. This relationship is supported by psychological research linking social disconnection to maladaptive cognitive processes.', 'session_id': 'fb8d1e37-c643-4a35-9cef-7906c08ca816', 'prob_std': 0.2967684178009476, 'citations': ['https://www.nature.com/articles/s44220-024-00350-x', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9910279/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3874845/', 'https://www.psychiatry.org/news-room/apa-blogs/rumination-a-cycle-of-negative-thinking', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-025-96968-7'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7169086894482013, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s44220-024-00350-x", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study provides strong empirical evidence of a positive association between loneliness (UCLA scale items) and rumination (RRS items)—including a large Spearman correlation (ρ ≈ 0.68), mediation results showing loneliness predicts more rumination, and network findings linking specific loneliness items to ruminative thoughts (e.g., “think about how alone you are” ↔ “how often do you feel alone”). However, the citation characterizes rumination as repetitive, intrusive, and part of perseverative cognition that can prolong stress and contribute to depression, not as a beneficial form of enhanced introspection or productive self-reflection. The proposed mechanism — that loneliness develops an enhanced capacity for introspection that leads to adaptive, productive rumination — is not supported by the paper; in fact the paper treats rumination as maladaptive in this context. (Also note the citation uses the Ruminative Responses Scale rather than CERQ-R.)"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-025-96968-7", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited paper reports a statistically significant positive association (and SEM path) from loneliness to repetitive negative thinking/rumination (e.g., β ≈ 0.34, p < 0.001), so the basic positive relationship is supported. However, the paper characterizes repetitive negative thinking/rumination as a maladaptive process that increases distress (cardiac distress, depression) and does not provide evidence that loneliness produces an enhanced, productive capacity for introspection or that increased rumination is a beneficial adaptive strategy. Therefore the directional link is supported but the specific mechanistic/benefit claims are not."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9910279/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review links loneliness to increased default-network/mentalizing activity and raises the possibility that lonely people engage more in mentalizing or rumination, but it explicitly calls that interpretation speculative and notes that shifts in connectivity have not been directly tied to increased rumination. The paper documents affective/cognitive disruptions with loneliness and interventions targeting maladaptive social cognitions, but it does not provide direct empirical evidence that loneliness causally increases rumination via an enhanced, productive capacity for introspection (and it frames many such processes as potentially maladaptive rather than beneficial)."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3874845/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents that loneliness alters cognition — producing implicit hypervigilance for social threat, negative attentional/memory biases, and impaired emotion regulation — which plausibly relates to increased self-focused, repetitive negative thinking. However the paper does not document an increased \\"capacity for introspection\\" or that loneliness produces more frequent/deeper rumination framed as productive or adaptive. The citation also does not mention rumination explicitly nor support the claim that loneliness inherently strengthens rumination as a beneficial strategy. Thus the sources partly support a link between loneliness and maladaptive self-focused cognitive processes, but not the specific mechanism or positive framing given in the explanation. \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.psychiatry.org/news-room/apa-blogs/rumination-a-cycle-of-negative-thinking", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Psychiatry.org piece defines rumination as repetitive, negative thinking that worsens mood and can arise from past experiences, and it discusses interventions to reduce rumination. It does not provide evidence that loneliness increases introspection or leads to greater rumination, nor does it describe loneliness as fostering rumination as a beneficial or adaptive strategy. In fact, the source frames rumination as maladaptive. Therefore the citation does not support the claim that loneliness causes increased (productive) rumination via enhanced introspection."}], "aggregate_score": 0.4, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Loneliness (UCLS) leads to increased Rumination (CERQ-R) because individuals who experience higher levels of loneliness develop an enhanced capacity for introspection and self-reflection over time. This heightened introspective ability naturally results in more frequent and in-depth rumination, as individuals utilize their solitary experiences to engage in productive cognitive processing. Therefore, as Loneliness (UCLS) increases, it directly fosters the cognitive skills that manifest as greater Rumination (CERQ-R), implying that loneliness inherently strengthens rumination as a beneficial adaptive strategy.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.4, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Loneliness (UCLS) leads to increased Rumination (CERQ-R) because individuals who experience higher levels of loneliness develop an enhanced capacity for introspection and self-reflection over time. This heightened introspective ability naturally results in more frequent and in-depth rumination, as individuals utilize their solitary experiences to engage in productive cognitive processing. Therefore, as Loneliness (UCLS) increases, it directly fosters the cognitive skills that manifest as greater Rumination (CERQ-R), implying that loneliness inherently strengthens rumination as a beneficial adaptive strategy.', 'perplexity': 1.5255920159359027, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7649003000955124, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.04827026308668471, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0039700484728335626, 'prob_variance': 0.08807149380407778, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.146559527518032, 'avg_nll': 0.4223825418975507}

