us supports a positive association between depressive states and elevated IL-6/TNF, contradicting the claim of suppression/reduction of these cytokines with worsening depressive symptoms."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3025086/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review explicitly reports that depression is associated with immune activation, including elevated IL-6 and TNF-α (meta-analyses and multiple studies cited). While the paper discusses coexistence of immune suppression (e.g., reduced NK cell cytotoxicity) and immune activation and theorizes about mechanisms linking chronic inflammation to suppression of cellular immunity, it does not provide evidence that greater depressive symptoms are associated with lower levels of proinflammatory cytokines. Thus the sources contradict — rather than support — the claimed negative relationship between depressive symptoms and IL-6/TNF-α.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-025-85514-0", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study reports that people with depression had higher IL‑6 concentrations than healthy controls (and found no between‑group difference for TNF‑α). It discusses positive links between inflammation and depression and shows correlations of cytokines with brain connectivity, but provides no evidence that worsening depressive symptoms suppress IL‑6 or TNF‑α. Thus the paper contradicts — rather than supports — the claim of a negative relationship (symptom increase → lower proinflammatory cytokines). It also does not use IDS‑SR measures or report depressive‑symptom–driven decreases in these cytokines."}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "Increased depressive symptoms have been shown to suppress immune function, including reducing the levels of proinflammatory 