108. Depressive symptoms (IDS-SR)-[POSITIVE]->Perceived social support (MSPSS)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Depressive symptoms (IDS-SR) cause perceived social support (MSPSS) to change in an inverse relationship. Increased depressive symptoms can causally lead individuals to withdraw socially, perceive less support, or interpret social interactions more negatively, thus reducing perceived social support. Conversely, lower depressive symptoms may enhance perceptions of available support. This is a direct negative causal relationship, not merely a correlation.', 'session_id': 'fb8d1e37-c643-4a35-9cef-7906c08ca816', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10689368/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10915202/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2024.1330720/full', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7500407/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3079557/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10689368/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited longitudinal network study reports that higher social support predicts reduced depressive symptoms and explicitly found no significant longitudinal edges from depressive symptoms to social support. It therefore does not provide evidence that greater depressive symptoms lead to increased perceived social support (and the measures used in the paper differ from IDS-SR/MSPSS).  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10915202/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article (Acoba et al., 2024) reports that greater perceived social support is associated with lower depressive symptoms (negative correlations and mediation showing social support reduces perceived stress and thereby reduces depression). It provides no evidence that higher depressive symptoms lead to higher perceived social support; in fact the findings are the opposite. Therefore the claim of a positive relationship (depression → increased perceived social support) is not supported by the provided source.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7500407/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article (Grey et al., 2020) reports negative associations between perceived social support (MSPSS) and depressive symptoms (PHQ‑9): correlations show higher social support linked with lower depression (r = -0.30) and logistic models indicate high perceived social support is protective against depression (ORs < 1). The study is cross‑sectional and does not provide evidence that higher depressive symptoms lead to increased perceived social support or the proposed seeking/appreciation mechanism. Therefore the citations contradict — rather than support — the claimed positive relationship and causal explanation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3079557/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited longitudinal study does not support the claim that higher depressive symptoms lead to increased perceived social support. Pettit et al. report negative cross-sectional correlations (higher depressive symptoms associated with lower perceived family and friend support) and either no prospective positive effects or the opposite prospective effect (among men, higher baseline depressive symptoms predicted slower increases in perceived family support). No evidence was found that depressive symptoms cause increases in perceived friend support. Thus the claim of a positive causal relationship (depression → greater perceived social support) is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2024.1330720/full", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article (Acoba, 2024) examines perceived social support (MSPSS) as a predictor of mental health and reports that greater perceived social support is associated with lower depression (negative correlations and negative predictive effects). It uses DASS-21 depression scores (not IDS‑SR) and does not present evidence that higher depressive symptoms lead to increased perceived social support. Thus the citation provides evidence for the opposite direction (social support → less depression), not for a positive effect of depression on perceived social support."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Higher levels of depressive symptoms often lead individuals to seek out and appreciate support from friends and family more, which in turn increases their perceived social support. As people become more aware of their emotional needs during depressive episodes, they are more likely to recognize and acknowledge the support available to them. Therefore, there is a positive relationship between depressive symptoms and perceived social support.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Higher levels of depressive symptoms often lead individuals to seek out and appreciate support from friends and family more, which in turn increases their perceived social support. As people become more aware of their emotional needs during depressive episodes, they are more likely to recognize and acknowledge the support available to them. Therefore, there is a positive relationship between depressive symptoms and perceived social support.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

