   Properties: {'session_id': 'fb8d1e37-c643-4a35-9cef-7906c08ca816', 'prob_std': 0.26003823266073756, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6658985/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0896627320304311', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychiatry/articles/10.3389/fpsyt.2023.1130989/full', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3846682/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7954739658353207, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6658985/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited review summarizes extensive evidence linking inflammation (especially proinflammatory cytokines like IL‑6 and TNFα) to depression and fatigue, emphasizes mechanisms whereby peripheral inflammation can cause CNS/behavioral changes (e.g., IFNα-induced depression), and repeatedly frames immune–depression links as complex and potentially mediated (including stress-related priming). It documents associations and treatment-driven changes in IL‑10 (e.g., antidepressants/animal models alter IL‑10), but does not present clear evidence that depressive symptoms themselves directly cause anti‑inflammatory responses (IL‑10) as a primary causal pathway. That pattern supports the contention that any observed association is likely mediated by other immune or stress-related mechanisms rather than a direct causal link from depressive symptoms to IL‑10."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3846682/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited review documents associations between depression and both pro-inflammatory activation and a compensatory anti-inflammatory reflex system (including changes in IL-10), but it frames these as responses linked to stress, proinflammatory signaling, gut/translocation and other upstream factors. The paper emphasizes that most data are cross-sectional, discusses stress- and inf