116. Sleep disturbance (PSQI)-[POSITIVE]->Body mass index (BMI)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Sleep disturbance (PSQI) causes body mass index (BMI) to increase through direct physiological and behavioral mechanisms, such as hormonal changes that increase appetite and reduced energy expenditure due to fatigue. Therefore, an increase in sleep disturbance directly leads to an increase in BMI, while a decrease in sleep disturbance would lead to a decrease in BMI. This relationship is supported causally by evidence that sleep disruption alters metabolism and eating behaviors.', 'session_id': 'fb8d1e37-c643-4a35-9cef-7906c08ca816', 'prob_std': 0.2984189587581942, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9480680/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6086945/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10367528/', 'https://www.psychiatry.pitt.edu/sites/default/files/inline-files/PSQI%20Article.pdf', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4221412/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7188315298731215, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9480680/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study reports a significant positive association between PSQI (worse sleep quality) and higher BMI (including mean PSQI rising with BMI and PSQI–BMI correlations), but it is cross-sectional and does not establish directionality or causation. The authors themselves note the need for longitudinal studies and do not claim that sleep disturbance causes BMI to increase. (The explanation also mixes causal directions.) Therefore the citation supports an association but not the causal claim that sleep disturbance causes increased BMI."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6086945/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited large cross-sectional study (Park et al. 2018) reports that poor sleep quality (PSQI > 5) and short sleep duration are positively associated with overweight/obesity (increased odds ratios and higher adjusted mean BMI). The paper discusses plausible mechanisms by which insufficient/poor sleep could promote weight gain (hormonal changes, appetite) but also acknowledges reverse causation (obesity → sleep problems) and explicitly notes the cross‑sectional design cannot prove causality; obstructive sleep apnea was not assessed. Thus the citation supports a positive association between PSQI-measured sleep disturbance and higher BMI but does not provide definitive evidence that sleep disturbance causes BMI to increase in the causal sense asserted in the explanation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10367528/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited systematic review and included studies evaluate BMI (overweight/obesity) as the exposure and sleep disturbances as the outcome — i.e., they report that higher BMI increases risk of sleep problems (and discuss mechanisms by which excess weight impairs sleep). They do not provide evidence that sleep disturbance (PSQI) causes subsequent increases in BMI. The explanation reverses the causal direction and relies on mechanisms and results in the citations that actually support BMI → sleep disturbance, not sleep disturbance → BMI. Therefore the claim that sleep disturbance causes BMI to increase is not supported by the provided citations."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4221412/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Vargas et al. paper reports a significant cross-sectional association between the PSQI \\"sleep disturbances\\" component and overweight (OR=1.66) and cites experimental/laboratory studies showing that sleep fragmentation can alter insulin sensitivity and appetite-related hormones (mechanistic plausibility for sleep disturbances → metabolic changes). However, Vargas et al. are explicit that their study is cross-sectional and cannot establish causality, and they call for prospective/objective studies. The user\'s explanation also mixes directions (it mainly describes BMI causing sleep problems via airway/joint discomfort), which is not what the Vargas paper demonstrates. Thus the citations support an association and biological plausibility for sleep disturbance contributing to metabolic dysregulation, but do not provide definitive evidence that PSQI-measured sleep disturbance causes increases in BMI (nor do they substantiate the specific discomfort-based causal mechanism as primary evidence)."}], "aggregate_score": 0.375, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Sleep disturbance (PSQI) causes body mass index (BMI) to increase because individuals with higher BMI experience greater physical discomfort during sleep, such as increased pressure on the airway and joints, which leads to more frequent awakenings and poorer sleep quality. As a result, an elevated BMI directly causes increased sleep disturbance (PSQI) scores, while a reduction in BMI would naturally improve sleep quality and decrease sleep disturbance. This causal pathway is supported by research indicating that excess body weight directly impairs sleep architecture and continuity.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.375, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Sleep disturbance (PSQI) causes body mass index (BMI) to increase because individuals with higher BMI experience greater physical discomfort during sleep, such as increased pressure on the airway and joints, which leads to more frequent awakenings and poorer sleep quality. As a result, an elevated BMI directly causes increased sleep disturbance (PSQI) scores, while a reduction in BMI would naturally improve sleep quality and decrease sleep disturbance. This causal pathway is supported by research indicating that excess body weight directly impairs sleep architecture and continuity.', 'perplexity': 1.519787043714774, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7767555806295688, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.013285556336054756, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004202781915856221, 'prob_variance': 0.0890538749463248, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.3066251718006971, 'avg_nll': 0.4185702222236406}

117. Sleep disturbance (PSQI)-[POSITIVE]->Proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Sleep disturbance (PSQI) causes proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6) to increase through direct physiological mechanisms. Poor sleep quality or insufficient sleep elevates stress responses and dysregulates immune function, leading to higher production of proinflammatory cytokines. Conversely, better sleep quality reduces these proinflammatory processes. This direct positive causal relationship is supported by experimental and clinical evidence.', 'session_id': 'fb8d1e37-c643-4a35-9cef-7906c08ca816', 'prob_std': 0.26879437148612595, 'citations': ['https://www.nature.com/articles/npp2016148', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4666828/', 'https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0169216', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-024-70834-4'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7666531004844418, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/npp2016148", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review strongly supports a positive link between sleep disturbance and increased inflammatory markers (including IL-6 and TNF-α) and discusses physiological and molecular pathways by which sleep loss/insomnia activates innate immune signaling. However, it does not provide evidence that the relationship is primarily mediated by compensatory dietary changes (e.g., increased caffeinated drinks or processed snacks) nor that those specific dietary choices are the main causal route to raised IL-6 and TNF-α in this context. Therefore the directionality (sleep disturbance → inflammation) is supported, but the proposed dietary mediation is not supported by the cited source."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-024-70834-4", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper reports an association between poor sleep quality (shorter N3 sleep/PSQI measures) and elevated salivary IL‑6, supporting a positive link between sleep disturbance and at least one proinflammatory marker (IL‑6). However, it does not measure TNF‑alpha, nor does it provide evidence that the sleep–inflammation link is primarily mediated by compensatory dietary behaviors (caffeinated drinks, processed snacks). Although dietary and caffeine items were queried, the paper presents no analyses showing those factors mediate IL‑6 or drive the association, and the discussion emphasizes physiological/inflammatory mechanisms rather than a diet-mediated pathway. Therefore the specific mediation claim and inclusion of TNF‑alpha are not supported by this citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4666828/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited meta-analysis reports that sleep disturbance (as assessed by validated questionnaires) is associated with higher circulating IL-6 (positive effect) but finds no reliable association with TNF. The paper discusses biological mechanisms (HPA axis, sympathetic activation, cellular/genomic inflammatory signaling) but does not provide evidence that compensatory dietary intake (caffeinated drinks, processed snacks) is the primary mediator linking poor sleep to increased proinflammatory cytokines. Thus the citation supports a positive link to IL-6 (questionnaire-assessed sleep disturbance) but not to TNF, and it does not support the claimed diet-mediated causal pathway."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0169216", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study reports a cross-sectional positive association between poorer PSQI sleep scores and higher IL-6 (and hs‑CRP/IP10) in menopausal women, so it supports a positive link between sleep disturbance and IL‑6. It does not report a positive association with TNF‑α, nor does it assess or provide evidence that compensatory dietary changes (caffeinated drinks, processed snacks) mediate the sleep–inflammation relationship. The paper also notes the cross‑sectional design cannot establish causality and discusses physiological (circadian/immune) mechanisms rather than diet-based mediation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.375, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Sleep disturbance (PSQI) causes proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6) to increase because individuals experiencing poor sleep quality are more likely to consume inflammatory foods and beverages, such as caffeinated drinks and processed snacks, in an attempt to compensate for fatigue. These dietary choices directly stimulate the immune system, resulting in higher circulating levels of proinflammatory cytokines. Therefore, the relationship between sleep disturbance (PSQI) and proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6) is primarily mediated by compensatory changes in dietary intake rather than physiological changes associated with sleep itself.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.375, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Sleep disturbance (PSQI) causes proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6) to increase because individuals experiencing poor sleep quality are more likely to consume inflammatory foods and beverages, such as caffeinated drinks and processed snacks, in an attempt to compensate for fatigue. These dietary choices directly stimulate the immune system, resulting in higher circulating levels of proinflammatory cytokines. Therefore, the relationship between sleep disturbance (PSQI) and proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6) is primarily mediated by compensatory changes in dietary intake rather than physiological changes associated with sleep itself.', 'perplexity': 1.366603020313073, 'mean_token_prob': 0.821237136123625, 'corruption_type': 'm