   Properties: {'session_id': 'fb8d1e37-c643-4a35-9cef-7906c08ca816', 'prob_std': 0.2019062772103288, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6962351/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3410706/', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-024-07098-5', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8255680/', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/nri3041'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8106982728677492, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6962351/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited systematic review reports that IL-10 (an anti‑inflammatory cytokine) commonly rises after intense/ prolonged exercise (and sometimes does not change after moderate exercise), and it discusses that regular exercise has long‑term anti‑inflammatory effects. However, the review does not provide direct evidence that physical inactivity causes baseline IL‑10 to fall, nor does it establish a simple inverse dose–response across levels of habitual activity. Most data are on acute increases in IL‑10 after high‑intensity exercise (transient effects) and on general anti‑inflammatory benefits of regular exercise, not on inactivity causing decreased IL‑10. Therefore the claim that increased inactivity causally lowers IL‑10 is not fully supported by these citations, though the opposite direction (that certain exercise increases IL‑10 acutely) is supported in part."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3410706/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review thoroughly describes IL-10 biology, regulation, sources, and roles in inflammation and disease, but it does not present evidence connecting physical inactivity (or physical activity) to changes in IL-10 levels. There are no data, studies, or discussion in the provided citation about sedentary behavior or exercise-induced modulation of IL-10. Therefore the specific causal claim that increased physical inactivity lowers IL-10 (and conversely that activity raises IL-10) is not supported by the provided source material."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8255680/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article discusses IL-10 biology, its paradoxical elevation and possible pro-inflammatory roles in COVID-19, and IL-10 “resistance” related to hyperglycemia/Type 2 diabetes and SHIP1/STAT3 signaling. It does not present evidence linking physical inactivity (or sedentary behavior measured by SBQ) to decreased IL-10 levels, nor does it provide data showing that increased physical activity raises IL-10. Therefore the source does not support the asserted direct inverse relationship between physical inactivity and IL-10."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-024-07098-5", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Nature paper investigates how IL‑10 signaling controls macrophage lipid metabolism and how loss of IL‑10 increases inflammatory ceramides and inflammation; it demonstrates IL‑10’s anti‑inflammatory role and downstream metabolic pathways. It does not provide data or discussion linking physical inactivity (sedentary behavior) to changes in IL‑10 levels or showing that increased physical inactivity causes IL‑10 to decrease. Therefore the specific causal relationship between \\"physical inactivity (SBQ) → decreased IL‑10\\" is not supported by the provided citation."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/nri3041", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review explicitly describes mechanisms by which exercise raises anti-inflammatory mediators, including IL-6 release from muscle that leads to subsequent increases in circulating IL-10 and increases in IL-10–secreting regulatory T cells. It also links physical inactivity to visceral-fat–driven chronic inflammation. However, the article does not directly present clear evidence that physical inactivity per se causes reduced circulating IL-10 (it infers the opposite effect of exercise but does not quantify IL-10 declines with inactivity) and discusses complexity (e.g., training intensity, elite athletes\' altered immunity). Thus the inverse relationship is supported in principle (exercise → higher IL-10; inactivity → more inflammation), but a direct causal statement that inactivity lowers IL-10 is not fully documented in the provided citation set."}], "aggregate_score": 0.2, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Physical inactivity (SBQ) causes anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) to decrease in a direct inverse relationship. Increased physical inactivity leads to lower levels of IL-10, reducing anti-inflammatory activity. Conversely, decreased physical inactivity (i.e., more activity) increases IL-10 and anti-inflammatory processes.', 'aggregate_score': 0.2, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.1797405768490214, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8811316969754571, 'min_prob': 0.24408605015423124, 'token_prob_slope': -0.003511089782503936, 'prob_variance': 0.040766144776934146, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.7894893281944549, 'avg_nll': 0.16529456417904345}

