. That supports the general point that IL-10 induction is mediated by other physiological/immunological pathways rather than by a simple, direct mechanistic switch. However, the paper does not address prosocial behavior (PGG) or behavioral/psychosocial causes of IL-10, nor does it provide direct evidence that a behavioral variable cannot causally affect IL-10 without intermediates. Therefore the claim that there is no direct causal relationship between prosocial behavior (PGG) and IL-10 is only partially supported by the citation (it supports the mediated-nature of IL-10 regulation but does not directly rule in or out behavioral causal links)."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/immunology/articles/10.3389/fimmu.2023.1161067/full", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review comprehensively describes IL-10 biology, cellular sources, and regulation by immunological, metabolic and microbial stimuli, but it does not address prosocial behavior (e.g., PGG) or behavioral/psychosocial causal links to IL-10. It therefore does not provide evidence that prosocial behavior has no direct causal effect on IL-10 (nor that IL-10 has no direct effect on prosocial behavior). The absence of discussion of that specific behavioral link in the citation means the claim of \\"NONE\\" (no direct causal relationship) is not supported by the provided source material."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3333333333333333, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'There is no direct causal relationship where prosocial behavior (PGG) causes anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) or vice versa. Any potential influence of prosocial behavior on IL-10 would likely be mediated by other psychological or physiological pathways, such as stress reduction or social support. Since mediating variables are present in the CLD, the direct link should not be included. Therefore, no direct causal relationship exists between these