   Properties: {'session_id': 'fb8d1e37-c643-4a35-9cef-7906c08ca816', 'prob_std': 0.28582370921563477, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4974016/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10871400/', 'https://digitalcommons.liberty.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=1861&context=honors', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0149763422003943', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/social-sciences/prosocial-behavior'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7340362605266764, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4974016/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited paper provides evidence that daily prosocial behavior is associated with higher positive affect and better self-rated mental health and that higher-than-usual prosocial behavior buffers the negative impact of daily stressors on affect and mental-health ratings. However, the study did not show that prosocial behavior directly reduces perceived stress itself (it measured stress as a count of daily stressors, not a perceived-stress scale) and the design is observational/daily-diary (limits strong causal claims about prosocial behavior lowering perceived stress). Thus the citation supports a stress-buffering effect on emotional outcomes but not the specific claim that prosocial behavior causally reduces perceived stress (PSS) per se."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10871400/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The main cited article reports a small negative correlation between prosocial behavior and negative affect, not perceived stress (PSS), and is cross‑sectional, so it cannot establish causality. The article’s references include a paper (Raposa et al. 2016) whose title indicates prosocial behavior can mitigate stress, which lends some supporting evidence for a buffering effect, but that paper’s data and causal claims are not presented here. Overall the provided citation material shows an association with negative affect and cites work suggesting stress-buffering, but does not directly show prosocial behavior causes reductions in perceived stress nor uses the PSS or a causal design."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://digitalcommons.liberty.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=1861&context=honors", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited literature collectively indicates a negative association between prosocial behavior and stress: reviews and empirical studies report that giving/helping is linked to reduced physiological stress markers (e.g., lower blood pressure, cortisol changes, immune benefits) and better psychological outcomes (e.g., buffered negative effects of daily stress, greater well‑being). Key citations include Brown & Brown (review), Whillans et al. (volunteering linked to lower later BP), Raposa et al. (daily-diary evidence that prosocial behavior mitigates negative effects of stress), Inagak et al. (neuroimaging showing beneficial brain/cardiovascular responses to giving), Von Dawans et al. (social support/oxytocin effects on cortisol and prosocial responses), and Martela & Ryan (experimental evidence of prosocial acts increasing well‑being). However, the evidence is mixed in places: effects depend on timing, individual differences/genetics, and several studies are correlational or diary-based rather than definitive randomized interventions. Therefore the citations support an inverse relationship but do not uniformly establish a simple, generalizable causal effect across all contexts."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0149763422003943", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation contains only an error message and no usable content or evidence. There is nothing in the citation to support the claimed negative relationship between prosocial behavior and perceived stress."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/social-sciences/prosocial-behavior", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation is an error message from ScienceDirect and contains no usable content or evidence; therefore there is nothing in the citation to support the claimed negative relationship between prosocial behavior and perceived stress.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.3, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Prosocial behavior (PGG) causes perceived stress (PSS) to change in an inverse relationship. Engaging in prosocial actions can directly reduce perceived stress by enhancing social connections and providing a sense of purpose. Conversely, a decrease in prosocial behavior may lead to increased perceived stress. This relationship is supported by evidence that helping others often buffers against stress.', 'aggregate_score': 0.3, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.4241555654812432, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7932659573874439, 'min_prob': 0.0910504471055282, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004918694225047309, 'prob_variance': 0.08169519274978375, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.1107087586174564, 'avg_nll': 0.3535790524451588}

