ween PGG and rumination. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s44220-024-00350-x", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article analyzes relationships among loneliness, rumination and depression; it does not examine prosocial behavior (PGG) or any measures of prosociality. Because the source contains no data or discussion about prosocial behavior’s relationship to rumination, it does not support the assertion that there is no direct causal link between Prosocial behavior (PGG) and Rumination (CERQ‑R)."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4444832/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review thoroughly discusses cognitive and neural mechanisms of prosocial behavior (goal-directed, habitual, Pavlovian systems, empathy, reputation, etc.) but does not address rumination, CERQ-R, or direct/indirect causal links between prosocial behavior and rumination. It provides no evidence for the absence of a relationship, so the claim of \\"NONE\\" is not supported by the provided source."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'There is no known direct causal link where Prosocial behavior (PGG) causes Rumination (CERQ-R) or vice versa. Any association between these variables would likely be mediated by factors such as perceived stress or social support, which are already present in the CLD. Direct causation is not supported by current evidence or plausible mechanism.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.564412159494889, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7886962904390927, 'min_prob': 0.004379016639593515, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004903579922633221, 'prob_variance': 0.09241295183436572, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.1385117100937323, 'avg_nll': 0.44751013649423194}

