ts of prosociality on IL-6 or TNF, so it cannot directly support the specific claim that there is no causal relationship between prosocial behavior and these cytokines. It therefore only partially supports the mediation rationale but does not provide evidence about the absence of a direct prosocial→inflammation (or reverse) link."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4360270/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article reports relationships between IL-6/TNF-α and obesity/adipose tissue (secretion by adipocytes, correlations with BMI and CRP) but contains no data or discussion about prosocial behavior (PGG), social behavior, stress, social support, mental health, or causal pathways linking prosocial behavior to cytokine levels. Therefore it does not support the claim that there is no direct causal relationship between prosocial behavior and proinflammatory cytokines (or that any observed association is mediated by other psychosocial variables)."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10960128/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents associations between social support/prosociality and inflammation (including meta-analytic links to IL‑6 and TNF‑α) but repeatedly emphasizes that mechanisms are unclear and that longitudinal/causal evidence is limited. It does report randomized trials showing prosocial acts causally alter leukocyte gene-expression profiles (CTRA — which involve proinflammatory gene expression), yet it does not provide clear direct causal evidence that prosocial behavior (PGG) changes circulating TNF‑α or IL‑6, nor evidence that those cytokines directly drive prosocial behavior. Thus the sources support the point that direct causality is not established, while also showing correlational evidence and causal effects on related gene‑expression outcomes — making the \