164. Prosocial behavior (PGG)-[NEGATIVE]->Depressive symptoms (IDS-SR)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Prosocial behavior (PGG) causally reduces depressive symptoms (IDS-SR). Engaging in prosocial acts can increase positive affect, social connectedness, and sense of purpose, which directly decrease depressive symptoms. Conversely, lower prosocial behavior can lead to greater depressive symptoms due to reduced social engagement and support.', 'session_id': 'fb8d1e37-c643-4a35-9cef-7906c08ca816', 'prob_std': 0.24652627608216837, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5828700/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10871400/', 'https://www.researchgate.net/publication/327206845_When_It_Feels_Good_to_Give_Depressive_Symptoms_Daily_Prosocial_Behavior_and_Adolescent_Mood', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2666915323001695', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30138009/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7619728769381884, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5828700/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents associations between prosociality and depressive symptoms and notes neural involvement of reward and cognitive-control networks during prosocial tasks. It cites longitudinal/observational work in youth suggesting more prosocial behavior can predict fewer later depressive symptoms and an fMRI study where ventral-striatum responses during an allocation task predicted fewer adolescent depressive symptoms. It also reports that cognitive-control regions (DLPFC) are engaged in prosocial/proself decisions and are blunted in MDD. However, the sources do not provide direct causal evidence that engaging in cooperative actions (e.g., PGG) improves executive functioning or problem-solving, nor do they show experimentally that such cognitive improvements prevent onset of depression. The review even highlights mixed and developmental effects (in some adolescent clinical samples prosociality may relate to greater burden and higher depressive symptoms). Thus the citations partly support a negative association and implicate relevant neural networks, but do not support the specific causal mechanism claimed."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10871400/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article reports cross-sectional correlations between prosocial behavior and psychological well-being/positive and negative affect in a small, non-random sample of young adults. It does not measure clinical depressive symptoms with the IDS‑SR, does not establish causality, and provides no evidence that prosocial behavior improves cognitive functioning, problem‑solving, or executive control as the mediating mechanism. Therefore the specific causal claim and proposed cognitive pathway are not supported by the citation.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.researchgate.net/publication/327206845_When_It_Feels_Good_to_Give_Depressive_Symptoms_Daily_Prosocial_Behavior_and_Adolescent_Mood", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The only cited item is an inaccessible ResearchGate page (connection/access denied) with no available content to evaluate. No empirical evidence or details from the source are provided that link prosocial behavior (PGG) to reductions in depressive symptoms (IDS-SR) via improved cognitive/executive functioning, so the claim is not supported by the supplied citation.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2666915323001695", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source is unavailable (Cloudflare error message) and contains no usable content or data linking prosocial behavior in a public goods game (PGG) to depressive symptoms measured by IDS-SR, nor evidence for the claimed causal pathway via cognitive/executive functioning. Because no supporting material is provided in the citation, the explanation is unsupported by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30138009/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study reports that adolescents experience increased positive mood on days they are more prosocial, and this effect is strongest for those with higher depressive symptoms. It is correlational/daily-diary evidence about short-term mood benefits, not proof that prosocial behavior causally reduces depressive symptoms long-term, nor does it provide evidence that the effect operates via improved cognitive functioning, problem-solving, or executive control. The paper does not support the specific causal mechanism proposed."}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Prosocial behavior (PGG) causally reduces depressive symptoms (IDS-SR) because engaging in cooperative actions directly improves cognitive functioning, which in turn prevents the onset of depression. Higher levels of prosocial behavior (PGG) stimulate problem-solving and executive control processes that act as a buffer against depressive symptoms (IDS-SR), while lower prosocial behavior may impair these cognitive mechanisms and thereby increase vulnerability to depressive symptoms.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Prosocial behavior (PGG) causally reduces depressive symptoms (IDS-SR) because engaging in cooperative actions directly improves cognitive functioning, which in turn prevents the onset of depression. Higher levels of prosocial behavior (PGG) stimulate problem-solving and executive control processes that act as a buffer against depressive symptoms (IDS-SR), while lower prosocial behavior may impair these cognitive mechanisms and thereby increase vulnerability to depressive symptoms.', 'perplexity': 1.302019272649634, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8263783180879156, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.16784474044754913, 'token_prob_slope': -0.00344904594266915, 'prob_variance': 0.060775204798941496, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0401602480261742, 'avg_nll': 0.26391634601909275}

