   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Prosocial behavior (PGG) does not directly cause sleep disturbance (PSQI), nor does sleep disturbance directly cause prosocial behavior. Any association between these variables is likely mediated by other factors such as stress, social support, or mood. There is no established direct causal mechanism linking prosocial behavior to sleep disturbance. Therefore, a direct causal relationship does not exist in either direction.', 'session_id': 'fb8d1e37-c643-4a35-9cef-7906c08ca816', 'citations': ['https://www.jneurosci.org/content/44/15/e0885232024', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/neuroscience/prosocial-behavior', 'https://open.maricopa.edu/socialpsychology/chapter/helping-and-prosocial-behavior/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10871400/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5183600/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.jneurosci.org/content/44/15/e0885232024", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article reports a correlation in the opposite direction: individual differences in sleep physiology (greater slow-wave activity / deeper sleep over the right TPJ) are associated with higher prosocial preferences on a public goods game. It does not show that engaging in prosocial behavior reduces sleep disturbances, does not use PSQI or measure “sleep disturbance” as an outcome, and provides no evidence of a causal effect from prosocial acts → improved sleep. Therefore the source does not support the claimed negative effect of prosocial behavior on sleep disturbance."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/neuroscience/prosocial-behavior", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited \\"ScienceDirect\\" source returns an error message and contains no usable content or data about prosocial behavior, public goods games, or sleep disturbance. There is nothing in the provided citation to support the claimed negative relationship or the proposed mechanism."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://open.maricopa.edu/socialpsychology/chapter/helping-and-prosocial-behavior/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited chapter describes what prosocial/helping behavior is, motivations for helping, personality correlates, and that helping \\"feels good\\" for helpers and recipients, but it contains no empirical evidence or discussion linking prosocial actions to sleep outcomes or reduced sleep disturbances. The source does not mention sleep quality, PSQI, or any studies showing prosocial behavior causes improved sleep, so it does not support the claimed negative relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10871400/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study reports correlations between prosocial behavior and psychological well‑being / positive and negative affect, but it does not measure sleep or sleep disturbance (no PSQI or sleep outcomes) nor prosocial behavior measured via public goods games. Therefore the provided citation does not support the specific claim that prosocial behavior reduces sleep disturbances or the sleep-related mechanism described."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5183600/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited editorial reviews research on prosocial/antisocial behavior in economic games, neural and cognitive mechanisms, punishment, and related interventions (e.g., compassion meditation), but it contains no data or discussion linking prosocial behavior (e.g., PGG cooperation) to sleep quality or sleep disturbances (PSQI). Therefore the provided citation does not support the claimed negative relationship between prosocial behavior and sleep disturbance."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "