ntensity of everyday stressors. It therefore neither documents a lack of a direct causal pathway nor provides empirical support for the claim that any association is mediated rather than direct. The citation does not address the target variable (daily hassles), so it cannot substantiate the asserted \\"NONE\\" causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10871400/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited sources report correlations and experimental/observational findings that prosocial behavior is associated with greater well‑being, increased positive affect, reduced negative affect, and can mitigate the negative effects of everyday stress (e.g., Raposa et al. 2016; Alden & Trew 2013; the present cross‑sectional study). None of the citations provide evidence that there is no causal effect; in fact at least one cited study explicitly indicates prosocial behavior can lessen everyday stress effects, which contradicts the \\"no direct causal\\" claim. Therefore the provided citations do not support the assertion of no relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prosocial_behavior", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Wikipedia article describes multiple pathways by which prosocial behavior influences mood, well‑being, and stress (e.g., increases positive affect, provides helper benefits, can buffer against stressors via social support). It does not state that prosocial behavior has no direct causal effect on daily hassles; instead it presents evidence of effects on mental health and stress-related outcomes, which undermines the claim of \\"no direct causal link.\\" While the article does mention mediating roles (mood, social support), it does not support a definitive assertion of no direct causal relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Prosocial