   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Prosocial behavior (PGG) causes loneliness (UCLS) to change in an inverse relationship. Engaging in more prosocial behavior directly reduces feelings of loneliness by increasing social connection and positive social interactions. Conversely, a decrease in prosocial behavior can directly increase loneliness due to reduced opportunities for meaningful social engagement.', 'session_id': 'fb8d1e37-c643-4a35-9cef-7906c08ca816', 'prob_std': 0.21835118686173557, 'citations': ['https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1094652/full', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10150042/', 'https://www.hhs.gov/sites/default/files/surgeon-general-social-connection-advisory.pdf', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38446286/', 'https://ourworldindata.org/social-connections-and-loneliness'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7723615821552738, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1094652/full", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper investigates how exposure to loneliness cues reduces prosocial behavior and identifies neural (N400/P300) correlates of loneliness that predict lower prosocial choices. It does not provide evidence that habitual prosocial behavior causally reduces loneliness, nor that prosociality produces lasting neurobiological desensitization to social isolation independent of actual social contact. At best the authors speculate that prosocial acts could help combat loneliness, but the empirical results and neural data reported are for the opposite causal direction (loneliness → reduced prosociality). Therefore the specific causal mechanism claimed is not supported by the citation.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10150042/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper presents evidence that experimentally induced loneliness (measured with the UCLA scale and ERP markers N400/P300) reduces subsequent prosocial choices in a PGG-like task and that loneliness elicits neural responses correlated with reduced prosociality. It does not provide evidence that prosocial behavior causally reduces loneliness, nor that habitual prosociality builds social skills/emotional intelligence that alter neurobiological sensitivity to social isolation independent of actual social contact. The direction of effect, the causal mechanism, and the specific neuroplastic claim in the explanation are not supported by the citation.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.hhs.gov/sites/default/files/surgeon-general-social-connection-advisory.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The sole cited source returns an \\"Access Denied\\" page and contains no substantive content or evidence relevant to the claimed neurobiological or behavioral links between prosocial behavior and loneliness. Because the citation provides no accessible information, it cannot support the causal inverse relationship described."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38446286/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited longitudinal adolescent study reports direct associations between developmental trajectories of prosocial behavior and trajectories of loneliness, supporting an inverse relationship (higher prosocial behavior linked with reduced loneliness over time). However, the paper does not provide evidence for the specific causal mechanism claimed (development of advanced social skills/emotional intelligence altering neurobiological responses), nor does it show that effects occur independently of the quantity or quality of social contact. The study is observational, adolescent-specific, and discusses cognitive/behavioral re-affiliation processes rather than neural mechanisms."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://ourworldindata.org/social-connections-and-loneliness", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The Our World in Data piece reviews evidence that social connections and perceived loneliness are linked to health and describes biological mechanisms by which isolation can affect physiology; it also cites studies showing socially engaged strategies can predict greater well‑being. However, it does not provide evidence that habitual prosocial behavior (PGG) specifically produces advanced social skills/emotional intelligence that causally change neural sensitivity to social isolation, nor that prosocial actions reduce loneliness independent of actual social contact. The cited material does not support the specific causal neurobiological mechanism or the PGG → UCLS claim as stated."}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Prosocial behavior (PGG) causes loneliness (UCLS) to change in an inverse relationship because individuals who habitually engage in Prosocial behavior (PGG) develop advanced social skills and emotional intelligence, which directly alter their neurobiological response to social isolation. As a result, increased Prosocial behavior (PGG) inherently reduces the neural sensitivity to feelings of Loneliness (UCLS), independent of the actual quantity or quality of social contact. Conversely, reduced engagement in Prosocial behavior (PGG) leads to heightened neural reactivity to social isolation, thus increasing Loneliness (UCLS) even when social engagement remains constant.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Prosocial behavior (PGG) causes loneliness (UCLS) to change in an inverse relationship because individuals who habitually engage in Prosocial behavior (PGG) develop advanced social skills and emotional intelligence, which directly alter their neurobiological response to social isolation. As a result, increased Prosocial behavior (PGG) inherently reduces the neural sensitivity to feelings of Loneliness (UCLS), independent of the actual quantity or quality of social contact. Conversely, reduced engagement in Prosocial behavior (PGG) leads to heightened neural reactivity to social isolation, thus increasing Loneliness (UCLS) even when social engagement remains constant.', 'perplexity': 1.23668865814114, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8499054709941883, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.20929682931747493, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004251045182859988, 'prob_variance': 0.047677240803928565, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8306519224414681, 'avg_nll': 0.21243737065657642}

