ress and that perceived stress mediates body image → depression in an obese clinical sample. Thus the citation does not support the claim that higher BMI directly causes increased perceived stress."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4326352/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study does not support the claim. Shimanoe et al. (2015) explicitly report no significant association between perceived stress and BMI in either men or women, and their design is cross-sectional (so cannot establish causality). The paper even notes that previous meta-analytic evidence points at only a very weak positive association overall. Therefore the claim that higher BMI \\"directly causes increased perceived stress\\" and that individuals \\"inevitably feel more stressed\\" as BMI increases is not supported by the provided citation(s)."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3428710/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review examines how chronic social/psychological stress contributes to obesity (stress → increased BMI and visceral fat) and discusses mechanisms and animal models for that direction. It does not provide evidence that higher BMI directly causes increased perceived stress; the authors even note difficulty determining causality and cite studies showing stress predicts BMI gain (not BMI predicting stress). Thus the citation does not support the claim that increasing BMI inevitably increases perceived stress."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/pcd/issues/2012/12_0001.htm", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited CDC study reports that BMI in their sample was not associated with perceived stress (predicted mean difference per 9-point PSS increase = 0.31 kg/m2, 95% CI −0.31 to 0.97, P = .33) and explicitly states \\"BMI in our sample was not associated with perceived stress.\\" The paper examines cross-sectional associations and notes behavioral links of stress (e.g., to eating and activity) but does not provide evidence that higher BMI directly causes increased perceived stress; nor does it support a causal, inevitable effect claimed in the explanation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Higher body mass index (BMI) directly causes increased perceived stress (PSS) because excess body weight creates more physical discomfort and social challenges, which leads to higher stress levels. Therefore, as BMI increases, individuals inevitably feel more stressed.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Higher body mass index (BMI) directly causes increased perceived stress (PSS) because excess body weight creates more physical discomfort and social challenges, which leads to higher stress levels. Therefore, as BMI increases, individuals inevitably feel more stressed.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

