   Properties: {'session_id': 'fb8d1e37-c643-4a35-9cef-7906c08ca816', 'prob_std': 0.2803965289898636, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8967417/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5507106/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6260791/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4363366/', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23863401/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7550955203067437, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8967417/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review clearly describes obesity as a state of chronic low‑grade inflammation, documents a shift in adipose immune cell composition in obesity (from M2/Treg/anti‑inflammatory phenotypes toward M1/pro‑inflammatory phenotypes), and lists IL‑10 among anti‑inflammatory cytokines produced in lean adipose tissue. That supports an association that higher adiposity corresponds to a more pro‑inflammatory and less anti‑inflammatory adipose environment. However, the paper does not provide direct, repeated empirical measurements showing an inverse quantitative relationship between BMI and circulating or tissue IL‑10, nor strong experimental evidence that increasing BMI causally reduces IL‑10 or that BMI reduction reliably increases IL‑10. Thus the claim of a causal, inverse effect on IL‑10 and the explicit statement that weight loss enhances IL‑10 activity are not fully demonstrated by the citation alone."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5507106/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review and studies document links between higher BMI/obesity and increased pro-inflammatory markers (e.g., IL-6, TNF-α, CRP) and decreased adiponectin (an anti‑inflammatory adipokine). They do not report data or discussion showing that BMI causally reduces IL‑10 levels (nor do they present IL‑10 measu