179. Body mass index (BMI)-[POSITIVE]->Depressive symptoms (IDS-SR)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Body mass index (BMI) does not directly cause depressive symptoms (IDS-SR) in a causal loop diagram when mediating variables such as physical inactivity, sleep disturbance, or proinflammatory processes are present to explain the pathway. Any observed association is likely mediated through these other variables, not via a direct causal link from BMI to depressive symptoms.', 'session_id': 'fb8d1e37-c643-4a35-9cef-7906c08ca816', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8896404/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC2884968/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11534730/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC2631467/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10502963/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8896404/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Cureus paper reports cross-sectional associations between higher BMI and greater prevalence of depression-related symptoms (using the PHQ‑9) in NHANES data, supporting a positive relationship. It does not establish causation (the authors note reverse causality and limitations), and it uses PHQ‑9 items rather than the IDS‑SR. Mechanistic claims about disruption of brain chemistry and hormones are discussed speculatively and via secondary references, not demonstrated as causal in the provided source. Therefore the citation supports a positive association but not the stronger causal claim or the specific IDS‑SR outcome measure."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11534730/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study is cross-sectional (no causal inference) and used the PHQ-9, not the IDS‑SR. Its results report a U-shaped association (increased depression risk at both low BMI and obesity) rather than a monotonic positive relationship in which higher BMI linearly increases depressive symptoms. While the paper cites possible biological mechanisms (inflammation, leptin, HPA changes), these are discussed as possible explanations and are not direct evidence that excess BMI \\"directly causes\\" increased depressive symptoms. Therefore the citation does not support the specific causal, monotonic claim (and uses a different depression measure)."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10502963/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited GSRD paper reports cross-sectional associations between higher BMI and worse clinical outcomes in MDD (e.g., increased suicidality, longer hospitalizations, some MADRS subitem differences such as concentration and lassitude) and notes prior genetic/Mendelian studies suggesting a possible causal link. However the study is not longitudinal or experimental, does not establish direct causation, and it did not use the IDS‑SR (it used MADRS and other clinical outcomes). The paper also discusses mechanistic hypotheses (inflammation, metabolic factors) but explicitly calls for further studies to explore neurobiological mechanisms. Thus the citation supports a positive association but does not support the specific causal claim (direct brain‑chemistry/hormone disruption causing increased IDS‑SR scores) nor the use of IDS‑SR as the outcome measure."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC2884968/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper examines how depressive symptoms (measured with a 10-item CES-D) interact with genetic polymorphisms to predict obesity, and reviews literature showing the relationship between obesity and depression can go in either direction and is complex and gender-dependent. It does not provide evidence that higher BMI directly causes increased depressive symptoms, does not use the IDS‑SR measure cited in the claim, and treats proposed neurobiological mechanisms as speculative. Therefore the source does not support the claim of a direct causal effect of rising BMI increasing depressive symptoms via disruption of brain chemistry/hormones."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC2631467/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper reports a U-shaped (quadratic) association between BMI and depressive symptoms (using the MHI, not the IDS‑SR), finding higher depression at both low and high BMI and no significant linear increase across BMI. It is cross‑sectional and explicitly states it cannot establish causality or biological mechanisms. Therefore it does not support a direct, monotonic causal claim that higher BMI linearly causes increased depressive symptoms via disrupted brain chemistry/hormones."}], "aggregate_score": 0.2, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Higher body mass index (BMI) directly causes an increase in depressive symptoms as measured by the IDS-SR because excess body weight disrupts brain chemistry and hormone levels responsible for mood regulation. Therefore, as BMI rises, individuals are more likely to experience depression.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.2, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Higher body mass index (BMI) directly causes an increase in depressive symptoms as measured by the IDS-SR because excess body weight disrupts brain chemistry and hormone levels responsible for mood regulation. Therefore, as BMI rises, individuals are more likely to experience depression.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

180. Body mass index (BMI)-[NONE]->Daily hassles (HS)
   Properties: {'session_id': 'fb8d1e37-c643-4a35-9cef-7906c08ca816', 'prob_std': 0.23562838067064204, 'citations': ['https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/9464-body-mass-index-bmi', 'https://www.cdc.gov/bmi/about/index.html', 'https://www.yalemedicine.org/news/why-you-shouldnt-rely-on-bmi-alone', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Body_mass_index'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8041765578916475, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/9464-body-mass-index-bmi", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Cleveland Clinic article describes what BMI is, how it’s calculated, and its limitations, and it lists health conditions associated with high or low BMI (including depression and other mental health conditions). It does not discuss daily hassles (HS), causal mechanisms linking BMI to day-to-day stressors, or evidence ruling out a causal effect. Because the source neither provides evidence that BMI does not cause daily hassles nor addresses that relationship, it does not support the claim of no causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/bmi/about/index.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The CDC page describes what BMI is, how it\'s used, and that obesity is associated with health risks and influenced by factors (including stress). It does not discuss \\"daily hassles\\" or provide evidence that BMI does not cause changes in daily hassles. The citation therefore does not support the assertion of no causal effect (and in fact notes stress can influence BMI, the opposite direction).  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.yalemedicine.org/news/why-you-shouldnt-rely-on-bmi-alone", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Yale Medicine article discusses limitations of BMI as a health screening measure and advises against relying on it alone; it does not address daily hassles, psychosocial outcomes, or whether BMI causally affects frequency/intensity of daily hassles. Therefore the source does not support the claim that BMI does not cause daily hassles to change."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Body_mass_index", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Wikipedia article describes what BMI is, its associations with health outcomes and social aspects (e.g., stigma), and limitations as a measure, but it does not state or provide evidence that BMI does not cause daily hassles or that any observed association must be mediated by stress or health. The source does not address \\"daily hassles\\" or directly rule out a causal pathway; in fact, it notes social consequences (which could plausibly lead to hassles), so it does not support the claim of no causal relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "Body mass index (BMI) does not cause daily hassles (HS) to change. There is no direct causal mechanism by which an individual's BMI would lead to an increase or decrease in the frequency or intensity of daily hassles. Any association between these variables is likely due to mediating factors such as stress or health, not a direct causal relationship.", 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.264626540791344, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8546992384708151, 'min_prob': 0.0704698215107024, 'token_prob_slope': -0.00429635170164197, 'prob_variance': 0.055520733777469, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9721728312626526, 'avg_nll': 0.23477685392122707}

181. Body mass index (BMI)-[POSITIVE]->Proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6)
   Properties: {'session_id': 'fb8d1e37-c643-4a35-9cef-7906c08ca816', 'prob_std': 0.24341805873394312, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4360270/', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21147638/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4015473/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5507106/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/pharmacology/articles/10.3389/fphar.2020.00481/full'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8224297075742072, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4360270/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited paper reports that IL‑6 and TNF‑α are produced by adipose tissue and that obese (higher BMI) subjects have higher levels of these cytokines (TNF‑α significantly higher in obese overall; IL‑6 higher particularly in obese women), and it shows positive correlations with BMI and CRP. This supports a positive association and biological plausibility (adipocytes secrete these cytokines). However, the study is observational and the authors explicitly state it is hard to determine whether increased body fat is a cause or consequence of altered TNF levels; the paper does not provide direct causal or weight‑loss intervention evidence showing that reducing BMI lowers these markers. Therefore the claim of a direct causal relationship (and the converse that reducing BMI decreases markers) is overstated based on this citation alone.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4015473/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article explicitly states that obesity (excess adipose tissue) is associated with chronic low-grade inflammation and that white adipose tissue produces/secretes TNF-α and IL-6; it also notes circulating TNF-α and IL-6 are increased in obesity and decreased with weight loss. Those statements support the link that higher adiposity (and by extension higher BMI in the overweight/obese range) is associated with increased proinflammatory cytokines and that reducing weight can lower them. However, the paper is primarily mechanistic/associative (cells, promoter work, literature citations) rather than providing direct causal evidence from longitudinal or interventional studies specifically linking changes in BMI to cytokine changes; it discusses obesity broadly rather than presenting direct BMI-based causal data."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5507106/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited review and its referenced studies provide mechanistic explanations (adipose tissue and macrophage secretion of IL-6 and TNF-α), consistent cross-sectional associations of higher BMI with higher IL-6/TNF-α, and intervention evidence (e.g., decreases in IL-6 after bariatric surgery). The sources explicitly state that adipose tissue releases these proinflammatory cytokines and that weight/obesity status is linked to elevated levels, supporting the claimed positive (and plausibly causal) relationship and that reducing weight lowers markers."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21147638/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study shows higher TNF-α (significantly) and higher IL-6 (significant in females) in obese versus normal-BMI subjects and states these cytokines are secreted by adipocytes and correlate with fat amount — supporting an association between higher BMI/adiposity and increased proinflammatory markers. However, the paper is observational and reports correlations between BMI/fat and cytokine levels; it does not provide direct evidence of a causal effect of BMI on cytokine increases nor evidence that reducing BMI decreases these markers. Thus the causal claim is not fully supported by the citation alone, and IL-6 findings vary by gender per the study. \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/pharmacology/articles/10.3389/fphar.2020.00481/full", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited systematic review discusses that adipose tissue produces TNF-α and notes that TNF-α levels correlate with degree of adiposity, and it cites mechanistic and observational work linking obesity/adiposity with increased TNF-α (supporting a positive BMI → TNF-α relationship). However the paper is not primarily presenting experimental causal proof that increasing BMI per se causes rises in TNF-α/IL-6, provides less direct evidence regarding IL-6 specifically in the adiposity context, and does not directly show that reducing BMI lowers these cytokines. Thus the sources support a positive association and biological plausibility for TNF-α (partial support for the broader causal claim), but do not fully establish the strong, bidirectional causal statement as given."}], "aggregate_score": 0.6, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Body mass index (BMI) causes proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6) to increase in a direct causal relationship. Increased BMI, especially in the overweight and obese range, leads to greater adipose tissue, which secretes proinflammatory cytokines like TNF-alpha and IL-6. Therefore, as BMI increases, proinflammatory processes also increase; conversely, reducing BMI decreases these proinflammatory markers.', 'aggregate_score': 0.6, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.239060376084208, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8676084267516156, 'min_prob': 0.11362056528886817, 'token_prob_slope': -0.002518681137944886, 'prob_variance': 0.059252351317801384, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9417918109457398, 'avg_nll': 0.21435333114837904}

