   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6) do not cause Smoking (SMQ). There is no direct causal mechanism by which elevated proinflammatory cytokines would initiate or increase smoking behavior. Any observed association is likely due to smoking causing inflammation, not the reverse.', 'session_id': 'fb8d1e37-c643-4a35-9cef-7906c08ca816', 'citations': ['https://www.nature.com/articles/1395490', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/10490998/', 'https://bjbabs.org/index.php/bjbabs/article/view/108', 'https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/full/10.1177/00368504211054985'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/1395490", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article demonstrates that IL‑6 and TNF‑α can alter neuroendocrine activity, central monoamine systems, and produce sickness‑like behaviors (e.g., anxiety, anhedonia, reduced consumption of palatable food), which suggests cytokines can influence brain chemistry and motivated behavior. However, it does not provide any data or discussion linking elevated IL‑6 or TNF‑α to increased nicotine craving, smoking initiation, or maintenance, nor empirical evidence that individuals with higher systemic inflammation are more likely to smoke. Thus the specific positive relationship to smoking (self‑medication or increased craving) is not supported by this source."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/10490998/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper describes neuroprotective effects of IL-6 and TNF-alpha against excitotoxicity and notes that nicotine can antagonize TNF-alpha–mediated neuroprotection. It does not provide evidence that elevated TNF-alpha or IL-6 increase nicotine craving, promote smoking initiation/maintenance, or that people with higher systemic inflammation are more likely to smoke to self‑medicate. The citation thus addresses cytokine neurobiology and nicotine’s interaction with cytokine effects, not a causal pathway from proinflammatory cytokines to increased smoking behavior."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://bjbabs.org/index.php/bjbabs/article/view/108", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article reports that cigarette smoking is associated with elevated plasma IL-6 and TNF-α (i.e., smoking → higher cytokines). It does not provide evidence that higher proinflammatory cytokines cause increased nicotine craving, smoking initiation, or maintenance (cytokines → smoking). The study is cross‑sectional and does not establish the direction of causality claimed, nor does it examine cravings or smoking behavior as an outcome of inflammation. Therefore the citation does not support the explanation\'s causal claim that inflammation increases smoking behavior."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Recent studies have suggested that elevated levels of proinflammatory cytokines like TNF-alpha and IL-6 can alter neurochemical pathways in the brain, increasing cravings for nicotine. As a result, individuals with higher systemic inflammation may be more likely to initiate or maintain smoking habits as a way to self-medicate their heightened inflammatory state.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Recent studies have suggested that elevated levels of proinflammatory cytokines like TNF-alpha and IL-6 can alter neurochemical pathways in the brain, increasing cravings for nicotine. As a result, individuals with higher systemic inflammation may be more likely to initiate or maintain smoking habits as a way to self-medicate their heightened inflammatory state.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

