es local cytokine/chemokine production that recruits immune cells into lesions, and cytokine‑storm phenomena produce systemic (large‑scale) inflammation. These passages show causal pathways by which proinflammatory processes change the local versus regional/systemic spatial scale of immune activity. Therefore the claim that there is no known direct causal mechanism between proinflammatory processes and spatial scale is contradicted by the citation.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/immunology/articles/10.3389/fimmu.2024.1330386/full", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited systematic review/meta-analysis examines circulating IL-6, TNF-α, and IL-1β in relation to age-related diseases and clinical outcomes; it does not investigate or mention \\"Spatial Scale\\" nor any causal links between cytokine levels and spatial scale. Therefore the source does not provide evidence for (or against) the claimed absence of a causal relationship with Spatial Scale."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/10490998/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper describes neuroprotective effects of IL-6 and TNF-alpha in the CNS (effects on neuronal survival and pathways) and does not mention \\"Spatial Scale\\" or any absence of a relationship between these cytokines and spatial scale. It therefore does not provide evidence that there is no causal link; it neither documents nor rules out any relationship to a variable called Spatial Scale."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6) do not cause Spatial Scale to change, nor does Spatial Scale cause changes in proinflammatory processes. There is no known direct causal mechanism linking these two variables in either direction.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.0648663312805684, 'mean_token_prob': 0.9486325985371297, 'min_prob': 0.39644484837543864, 'token_prob_slope': -0.002413172764151256, 'prob_variance': 0.012786602631025228, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.560276447951165, 'avg_nll': 0.06284928074960458}

195. Proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6)-[NEGATIVE]->Depressive symptoms (IDS-SR)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6) cause depressive symptoms (IDS-SR) in a direct causal relationship. Elevated levels of these proinflammatory cytokines can induce neurobiological changes that increase the likelihood or severity of depressive symptoms. Decreasing proinflammatory processes can correspondingly reduce depressive symptoms. This relationship is supported by mechanistic evidence linking inflammation to changes in mood and behavior.', 'session_id': 'fb8d1e37-c643-4a35-9cef-7906c08ca816', 'citations': ['https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-025-85514-0', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3741070/', 'https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/ejn.15992', 'https://www.jneuropsychiatry.org/peer-review/depression-and-inflammation-disentangling-a-clear-yet-complex-and-multifaceted-link.html', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4881555/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-025-85514-0", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article reports that IL-6 and TNF-α are implicated in depression, with IL-6 higher in depressed patients and proinflammatory markers linked to susceptibility and symptom-related neural changes. It examines cytokine associations with resting-state connectivity and uses BDI-FS/BDI-II measures, not IDS‑SR, and does not present evidence that higher IL-6 or TNF‑α reduce depressive symptoms. Overall the paper argues the opposite (inflammation as a risk/modifier for depression) and provides no support for a protective/suppressive effect of these cytokines on depressive symptoms."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3741070/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review summarizes extensive evidence that proinflammatory cytokines (including IL‑6 and TNF‑α) are associated with increased risk and severity of depressive symptoms, that chronic cytokine exposure and exogenous cytokine administration (e.g., IFN‑α) induce depressive symptoms, and that functional variants predicting higher cytokine production are linked to greater vulnerability. It therefore supports a positive (increasing) — not a suppressive — relationship between inflammation and depression. The explanation\'s claim that higher proinflammatory processes suppress depressive symptoms is contrary to the content of the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4881555/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited systematic review and linked studies report that TNF-α and other proinflammatory cytokines are elevated in depression, that experimental administration of TNF-α produces depressive/sickness-like behaviors in animals, and that TNF-α blockade can reduce depressive symptoms in some contexts. The review describes mechanisms by which TNF-α impairs neurogenesis and neurotransmitter balance (e.g., activating IDO, p38 MAPK, reducing serotonin availability), not mechanisms by which increased proinflammatory signaling would enhance neuronal repair or suppress depression. Thus the citation supports a positive (risk-increasing) or causal role of proinflammatory cytokines in depression, not the proposed negative (protective) relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.jneuropsychiatry.org/peer-review/depression-and-inflammation-disentangling-a-clear-yet-complex-and-multifaceted-link.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review summarizes evidence that proinflammatory cytokines (including TNF-α and IL-6) are elevated in many people with major depression, that cytokine administration (e.g., IFN-α) can induce depressive symptoms, and that anti-cytokine/anti-inflammatory treatments can reduce depressive symptoms (often in those with high baseline inflammation). It repeatedly frames inflammation as a factor that can precipitate or worsen depression (sickness behavior, altered neurotransmission, reduced neurogenesis), not as a protective mechanism that suppresses depressive symptoms via enhanced neuronal repair or resilience. The review does not provide evidence that higher TNF-α or IL-6 are associated with reduced IDS-SR depressive symptoms; on the contrary, it presents meta-analytic and clinical-trial evidence of the opposite direction in at least a subset of patients."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Recent theories suggest that increased levels of proinflammatory cytokines like TNF-alpha and IL-6 actually suppress depressive symptoms by enhancing neuronal repair and resilience. Therefore, higher proinflammatory processes could lead to a reduction in depressive symptoms as measured by IDS-SR.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Recent theories suggest that increased levels of proinflammatory cytokines like TNF-alpha and IL-6 actually suppress depressive symptoms by enhancing neuronal repair and resilience. Therefore, higher proinflammatory processes could lead to a reduction in depressive symptoms as measured by IDS-SR.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

196. Anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10)-[NEGATIVE]->Body mass index (BMI)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) do not directly cause body mass index (BMI) to change. There is no clear causal mechanism whereby changes in IL-10 levels would directly and independently alter BMI. Any observed association is likely mediated by other factors such as inflammation or metabolic processes, which are already present in the system. Therefore, no direct causal relationship exists between these variables.', 'session_id': 'fb8d1e37-c643-4a35-9cef-7906c08ca816', 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC8277584/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3530499/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6260791/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC3808131/', 'https://academic.oup.com/jcem/article/104/10/4552/5498035'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC8277584/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study found no significant change in IL-10 during the weight-loss program and reported no correlation between changes in serum IL-10 and changes in BMI or most other cardiometabolic parameters. The authors explicitly state that cause-and-effect relationships cannot be ascribed. While the paper and its references discuss IL-10 associations with lipid parameters and some animal data on lipid effects, they do not provide evidence that increasing IL-10 directly accelerates metabolic rate, promotes fat breakdown, or causes meaningful BMI reduction in humans."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC3808131/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study shows that overexpression of IL‑10 in mice prevents high‑fat diet–induced weight gain, reduces fat mass and adipocyte hypertrophy, and improves metabolic measures — supporting a negative association between IL‑10 activity and weight gain in this animal model. However, the paper does not demonstrate the specific mechanistic claims in the explanation: it does not measure or show increased metabolic rate or directly demonstrate enhanced fat breakdown (lipolysis), nor does it provide evidence in humans or show IL‑10 causes significant weight loss in already‑obese subjects. The study is preventive in mice rather than evidence of direct causation of BMI reduction in individuals via the stated mechanisms. \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3530499/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article reports that caloric-restriction–induced weight (and visceral fat) loss was associated with increased IL-10 levels and that visceral fat loss independently correlated with IL-10 increase. It documents an association (and the temporal pattern of weight loss preceding measured IL-10 change) but provides no evidence that higher IL-10 causes reductions in BMI, nor any mechanistic data that IL-10 accelerates metabolic rate or directly promotes fat breakdown. Therefore the claim of direct causation and the proposed mechanism are not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6260791/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Science paper demonstrates that IL-10 alters metabolic programming in macrophages (inhibiting glycolysis, promoting oxidative phosphorylation and mitophagy, and inhibiting mTORC1) and that loss of IL-10 signaling affects macrophage-driven inflammation in mice and humans. It contains no data showing that IL-10 increases systemic metabolic rate, stimulates adipose fat breakdown, or causes weight loss/BMI reduction in whole organisms or humans. Therefore the paper supports IL-10\'s role in cellular/macrophage metabolism and inflammation but does not support the claim that boosting IL-10 directly lowers BMI or causes significant weight loss."}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increased levels of anti-inflammatory cytokines like IL-10 directly cause a reduction in body mass index (BMI) by accelerating metabolic rate and promoting fat breakdown. Therefore, boosting IL-10 activity leads to significant weight loss in individuals.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.125, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increased levels of anti-inflammatory cytokines like IL-10 directly cause a reduction in body mass index (BMI) by accelerating metabolic rate and promoting fat breakdown. Therefore, boosting IL-10 activity leads to significant weight loss in individuals.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

197. Anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10)-[NEGATIVE]->Loneliness (UCLS)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) do not cause Loneliness (UCLS) to change. There is no direct causal mechanism by which changes in IL-10 levels would directly result in changes in perceived loneliness. Any observed associations are likely mediated by other psychological or physiological variables. Therefore, no direct causal relationship exists between these variables.', 'session_id': 'fb8d1e37-c643-4a35-9cef-7906c08ca816', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3410706/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7886943/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3874845/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6158086/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3410706/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review documents IL-10\'s anti-inflammatory roles, regulation, and effects in infection, autoimmunity, and some neuroinflammatory models (e.g., EAE, influenza lung inflammation). It does not present evidence linking IL-10 levels to subjective feelings of loneliness, social pain, or modulation of neural circuits underlying social isolation. The claim that higher IL-10 \\"directly reduce[s] feelings of loneliness\\" (a psychological/social outcome) is not supported by the provided citation(s)."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7886943/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper examines proinflammatory markers (principally CRP and references to IL-6/proinflammatory gene expression) and finds no longitudinal association between CRP and future loneliness (CRP did not predict future loneliness). It does not measure or report IL-10 or other anti‑inflammatory mediators, nor does it provide evidence that elevated IL‑10 reduces loneliness or dampens neural/social‑pain circuits. Therefore the specific claim about anti‑inflammatory processes/IL‑10 lowering loneliness is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3874845/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review documents associations between loneliness and increased pro-inflammatory activity, impaired glucocorticoid (anti‑inflammatory) signaling, and altered immune gene expression, but it does not provide evidence that elevated anti‑inflammatory mediators (nor IL‑10 specifically) causally reduce feelings of loneliness or dampen neural circuits of social pain. IL‑10 is not discussed, and no direct evidence is cited that higher anti‑inflammatory activity leads to lower UCLA loneliness scores.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6158086/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review summarizes evidence that increased pro-inflammatory activity (e.g., IL-6, endotoxin) heightens neural sensitivity to social threat and increases feelings of social disconnection, and it raises the possibility that anti-inflammatory treatments could be tested for effects on social/affective outcomes. However the paper does not provide empirical evidence that elevated anti-inflammatory processes (specifically IL-10) directly reduce loneliness or dampen neural circuits of social pain. IFN-γ is discussed as a distinct cytokine with complex effects, but that is not the same as IL-10. The claim about IL-10 reducing loneliness is not supported by the provided citations (the review only suggests the idea of testing anti-inflammatory interventions, and does not present data demonstrating an inverse IL-10–loneliness relationship)."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Recent findings suggest that increased anti-inflammatory processes, such as elevated IL-10 levels, can directly reduce feelings of loneliness by dampening neural circuits involved in social pain. Thus, individuals with higher IL-10 are less likely to experience loneliness, as their immune system helps buffer the emotional impact of social isolation.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Recent findings suggest that increased anti-inflammatory processes, such as elevated IL-10 levels, can directly reduce feelings of loneliness by dampening neural circuits involved in social pain. Thus, individuals with higher IL-10 are less likely to experience loneliness, as their immune system helps buffer the emotional impact of social isolation.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

198. Anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10)-[NEGATIVE]->Sleep disturbance (PSQI)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) cause sleep disturbance (PSQI) to change in an inverse relationship. Increased IL-10, an anti-inflammatory cytokine, directly reduces inflammatory activity, which is known to improve sleep quality and decrease sleep disturbances. Conversely, decreased IL-10 can lead to increased inflammation and worsen sleep disturbance. The causal direction is from anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) to sleep disturbance (PSQI), not the reverse.', 'session_id': 'fb8d1e37-c643-4a35-9cef-7906c08ca816', 'prob_std': 0.23830205780966512, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3410706/', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23490309/', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/npp2016148', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/immunology/articles/10.3389/fimmu.2023.1161067/full', 'https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s43440-021-00333-0'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7974773691267705, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3410706/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review thoroughly describes IL-10\'s anti-inflammatory roles, regulation, cellular sources, and involvement in infectious, autoimmune, and neuroinflammatory contexts (e.g., EAE, MS), but it does not present evidence that IL-10 enhances synaptic plasticity or neuronal excitability, nor any data linking IL-10 causally to sleep architecture or to reduced sleep disturbance as measured by the PSQI. Thus the specific mechanistic claim and the direct negative relationship between IL-10 levels and PSQI scores are not supported by the provided citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23490309/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited cross-sectional study in hemodialysis patients reports an inverse association between serum IL-10 and PSQI (poorer sleepers had lower IL-10; IL-10 inversely correlated with PSQI and showed an inverse relationship in multivariate analysis). That supports a negative relationship (higher IL-10 linked to better sleep) but does not establish causality or the proposed CNS mechanisms (synaptic plasticity/neuronal excitability promoting restorative sleep). The paper is observational and does not demonstrate directional causation from IL-10 to sleep disturbance nor describe the mechanistic claims in the explanation.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/npp2016148", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review describes strong reciprocal links between sleep and inflammatory biology and documents that inflammatory mediators influence sleep continuity and macrostructure (and that anti-inflammatory interventions can alter sleep). However, the excerpt and reference list focus largely on pro‑inflammatory cytokines (e.g., IL‑1, TNF, IL‑6) and overall bidirectional regulation; it does not provide direct evidence about IL‑10 specifically, nor the precise mechanistic claims that elevated IL‑10 enhances synaptic plasticity/neural excitability to produce improved PSQI scores, nor a unidirectional causal claim from IL‑10 → sleep. Therefore the general idea that inflammation and anti‑inflammatory processes relate to sleep is supported, but the specific IL‑10‑centric, inverse, and primarily causal claims are not substantiated by the provided citation. \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/immunology/articles/10.3389/fimmu.2023.1161067/full", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review documents IL-10’s anti‑inflammatory, neuroprotective and neuroregenerative roles (e.g., reducing neuronal injury, promoting neuron survival and neurogenesis) but does not present evidence that IL-10 specifically enhances synaptic plasticity or neuronal excitability in a way that improves sleep architecture, nor does it link IL-10 levels causally to reduced sleep disturbance or PSQI scores. The review discusses IL-10 effects in the CNS and in disease contexts (e.g., encephalitis models) but provides no direct data or citations tying IL-10 to restorative sleep or to the directionality proposed. Therefore the claim that elevated IL-10 directly reduces PSQI-measured sleep disturbance (and that causal direction is from IL-10 to sleep) is not supported by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s43440-021-00333-0", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper does not provide evidence linking IL-10 to PSQI. It measured and reported associations of poor sleep with higher levels of proinflammatory cytokines (IL‑6, IL‑17, IL‑23), not IL‑10, and it does not demonstrate a causal direction from anti‑inflammatory IL‑10 to reduced sleep disturbance or the neurophysiologic mechanisms claimed. Therefore the specific claim about IL‑10 inversely affecting PSQI (and the asserted mechanism) is not supported by the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.2, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) cause sleep disturbance (PSQI) to change in an inverse relationship. Elevated IL-10 levels enhance synaptic plasticity and neuronal excitability in the central nervous system, which directly promotes restorative sleep architecture and reduces sleep disturbance, as measured by the PSQI. In contrast, reduced anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) impede these neurophysiological adaptations, leading to greater sleep disturbance. The primary causal direction is from anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) to sleep disturbance (PSQI), not the reverse.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.2, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) cause sleep disturbance (PSQI) to change in an inverse relationship. Elevated IL-10 levels enhance synaptic plasticity and neuronal excitability in the central nervous system, which directly promotes restorative sleep architecture and reduces sleep disturbance, as measured by the PSQI. In contrast, reduced anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) impede these neurophysiological adaptations, leading to greater sleep disturbance. The primary causal direction is from anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) to sleep disturbance (PSQI), not the reverse.', 'perplexity': 1.2506856921320668, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8566036078795134, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.11810028171057317, 'token_prob_slope': -0.00106416199388906, 'prob_variance': 0.056787870756320975, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0722967231994218, 'avg_nll': 0.22369195461927854}

199. Anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10)-[NONE]->Physical inactivity (SBQ)
