itation(s)."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/immunology/articles/10.3389/fimmu.2023.1161067/full", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review thoroughly documents IL-10’s anti‑inflammatory roles and its relationships with proinflammatory cytokines, obesity/ BMI, metabolic dysfunction and neuroimmune/psychological sequelae of disease (e.g., in COVID‑19), supporting the idea that IL-10 associations can be indirect and mediated by inflammatory state or body‑mass/metabolic factors. However the paper does not address physical inactivity or sedentary behavior (SBQ) directly and does not provide evidence that physical inactivity and IL‑10 have no direct causal link; nor does it present specific evidence that depressive symptoms mediate an IL‑10 — physical inactivity association. Therefore the explanation is partly supported (mediating roles for inflammation and BMI are supported) but the specific assertion about no direct causal link to physical inactivity (and the role of depressive symptoms as a mediator) is not directly supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://journals.plos.org/plosbiology/article?id=10.1371/journal.pbio.1000465", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper provides experimental evidence that physical activity (exercise) increases hypothalamic IL-6 and IL-10 and that blocking exercise-induced IL-6/IL-10 prevents exercise\'s effects. That indicates a direct causal link from activity (the inverse of physical inactivity) to IL-10 levels. The paper does not show IL-10 causes changes in physical activity, but it does directly contradict the claim that there is no direct causal relationship in the direction from activity/inactivity to IL-10. Therefore the assertion of \\"NONE\\" (no direct causal link in either direction) is not supported by the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggreg