   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Spatial Scale does not cause Body mass index (BMI) to change. There is no direct causal mechanism by which altering spatial scale would affect an individual's BMI. These variables are conceptually and mechanistically unrelated in either direction.", 'session_id': '4a4eed24-8545-4e16-9f71-946b0734d107', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5559354/', 'https://ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC6925327', 'https://ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC3410673', 'https://bmjopen.bmj.com/content/6/1/e010145', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2997394/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5559354/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study shows that lower utilitarian walking and lower overall physical activity are associated with higher BMI, supporting the proposed mechanism that reduced walking links to higher BMI. However, the paper does not test or demonstrate that \\"larger spatial scales\\" (living in bigger geographic areas) directly cause reduced walking or higher BMI — it analyzes small towns across regions and reports regional variation in built-environment–BMI associations, and is cross-sectional (so cannot establish causality). Therefore the mechanism is supported but the specific causal claim about spatial scale expanding → higher BMI is not directly demonstrated by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2997394/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited paper and referenced literature report associations between measures of sprawl/built environment and lower walking and higher BMI (e.g., prior studies linking urban sprawl to obesity; in this study Whites in Los Angeles walked more and had somewhat lower BMI, and built‑environment features related to BMI and walking). However, the article reports modest effects, heterogeneous results by race (no regional BMI/walking differences for African Americans), and does not establish a clear causal pathway that larger spatial scale directly increases BMI for all groups. Thus the sources provide partial associative support but not strong, generalizable causal evidence."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC6925327", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study analyzes neighborhood walkability (density, destinations, street connectivity) within 1-mile and 1/4-mile buffers and finds higher walkability is associated with small reductions in BMI. It does not test or show that \\"larger spatial scales\\" (living in bigger geographic areas) lead to higher BMI via reduced walking; if anything the evidence points in the opposite direction (more walkable/denser environments → slightly lower BMI)."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC3410673", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study did not find that larger-scale spatial characteristics (built-environment measures, urbanization, population density, commuting variables) were associated with higher BMI overall. While some built-environment features related to physical activity in subgroups (mainly middle-aged White women), the authors report no specific measures of the built environment associated with overweight or obesity and note the cross-sectional design limits causal inference. Thus the paper does not support the claimed causal positive effect of larger spatial scale on BMI."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "Larger spatial scales, such as living in bigger geographic areas, directly cause an increase in individuals' body mass index (BMI) because people tend to walk less and rely more on transportation