31. Perceived stress (PSS)-[NEGATIVE]->Proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Perceived stress (PSS) causes proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6) to increase through direct neuroendocrine and immune mechanisms. Higher perceived stress activates the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis and sympathetic nervous system, which in turn promotes the production of proinflammatory cytokines such as TNF-alpha and IL-6. Conversely, lower perceived stress reduces activation of these inflammatory pathways.', 'session_id': '4a4eed24-8545-4e16-9f71-946b0734d107', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11546738/', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/18639628/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1361287/', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/9617578/', 'https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-030-16996-1_6'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11546738/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review describes stress (especially acute stress) as increasing proinflammatory cytokines (IL‑6, TNF‑α) and describes chronic stress as causing immune dysregulation often with elevated proinflammatory signaling. It attributes immune suppression to prolonged glucocorticoid effects on some immune functions but does not support the claim that higher perceived stress triggers a \\"relaxation response\\" that lowers IL‑6 or TNF‑α. Overall, the sources report increased or dysregulated proinflammatory activity with stress, not the decreased proinflammatory cytokines asserted in the motivation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1361287/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited meta-analysis reports that acute and brief psychosocial stressors are associated with increased IL-6 (and in some cases other proinflammatory signals), not decreased levels; TNF-α was not shown to decrease (and any TNF-α findings were nonsignificant). The paper furthermore describes stress as activating sympathetic and HPA pathways (fight-or-flight), not a “relaxation response,” and finds subjective perceived stress generally does not reliably predict reduced proinflammatory cytokines. Thus the source contradicts the claim that higher perceived stress suppresses proinflammatory cytokines such as IL-6 and TNF-α."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/18639628/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study reports that IL-6 production was lower during the examination (stress) period than at baseline, which is consistent with a negative association between perceived stress and IL-6. However, changes in TNF‑alpha are not clearly reported as decreasing with stress in the citation (the paper emphasizes relaxation intervention effects on TNF‑alpha). Crucially, the paper supports that relaxation training counteracts proinflammatory cytokine alterations, not that higher perceived stress itself \\"triggers the body\'s relaxation response\\" which then suppresses immune function. Thus the empirical negative link is only partially supported (mainly for IL‑6) and the proposed mechanism is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/9617578/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study reports that psychological stress significantly increased stimulated production of proinflammatory cytokines (TNF-alpha and IL-6), and students with higher perceived stress had higher production of these cytokines — directly opposing the claim that stress decreases proinflammatory markers."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-030-16996-1_6", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited chapter describes psychological stress activating the HPA axis and SNS and leading to immune dysregulation that is typically pro‑inflammatory (e.g., elevated IL‑6, CRP, TNF) in chronic stress and trauma. Meta-analyses and multiple studies in the chapter link stress/childhood adversity, caregiving, bereavement, PTSD, etc. with increased IL‑6 and TNF (not decreases). The chapter does note stress can suppress some immune functions (e.g., impaired wound-healing responses, reduced LPS-stimulated IL‑1β), but it does not support the claim that higher perceived stress invokes a relaxation response that lowers proinflammatory cytokines; rather, the evidence points to increased or dysregulated inflammation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "Higher levels of perceived stress actually trigger the body's relaxation response, which in turn suppresses the immune system and leads to a decrease in proinflammatory cytokines like TNF-alpha and IL-6. This means that as stress increases, proinflammatory processes become less active, resulting in lower levels of these inflammatory markers.", 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': "Higher levels of perceived stress actually trigger the body's relaxation response, which in turn suppresses the immune system and leads to a decrease in proinflammatory cytokines like TNF-alpha and IL-6. This means that as stress increases, proinflammatory processes become less active, resulting in lower levels of these inflammatory markers.", 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

