monstrating a direct unidirectional causal mechanism whereby perceived stress alone actively generates new objective hassles. Thus the citations support an association and plausible pathways (including perception/attention and contextual effects) but do not provide clear evidence that perceived stress directly causes an increase in the number of daily hassles."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://openbooks.library.baylor.edu/psychscience/chapter/stress-health-wellness/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The chapter describes daily hassles as stressors that contribute to perceived stress and discusses appraisal (how perception influences whether events are experienced as stressful), but it does not provide evidence or claim that higher perceived stress causally increases the number of daily hassles one experiences or actively generates new hassles. The cited material addresses the association and direction hassles→stress and the role of appraisal, but does not support the specific causal claim stress→more hassles."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3333333333333333, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "Higher levels of perceived stress directly cause an increase in the number of daily hassles a person experiences, as stress can make individuals more prone to encountering and noticing minor problems throughout the day. As stress rises, it actively generates new daily hassles by impacting a person's environment and interactions.", 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.3333333333333333, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': "Higher levels of perceived stress directly cause an increase in the number of daily hassles a person experiences, as stress can make individuals more prone to encountering and noticing minor problems throughout the day. As stress rises, it actively generates new daily hassles by impacting a person's environment and interactions.", 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

