nce relationship but does not support the claim that it operates solely as a direct, unmediated effect."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10498818/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article examines the psychometric properties and longitudinal variance decomposition of the Perceived Stress Scale (PSS). It does not present data linking PSS scores to sleep disturbance (PSQI), nor does it test or provide evidence for a causal effect of perceived stress on sleep or for improvement in sleep following stress reduction. Therefore the specific causal relationship between PSS and PSQI is not addressed by the provided citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2024.1431234/full", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited longitudinal study reports a significant direct effect of stress on subsequent sleep quality (β = 0.53, p < 0.001) and interprets this as stress predicting poorer sleep, supporting the claim of a direct (temporal) effect. However, the same paper also finds multiple significant mediation pathways (e.g., via rumination, emotion‑focused coping, and smartphone dependence) and explicitly concludes that stress affects sleep both directly and indirectly. In addition, the study used a Chinese college‑student stress scale and the Epworth Sleepiness Scale (daytime sleepiness) rather than the Perceived Stress Scale (PSS) and PSQI specified in the motivation. Therefore the citation supports a direct stress→sleep relationship but does not support the stronger claim that the effect “does not require mediation” (and the exact measures named differ)."}], "aggregate_score": 0.375, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Perceived stress (PSS) causes sleep disturbance (PSQI) in a direct causal relationship. Higher levels of perceived stress can increase physiological and psychological arousal, making it harder to fall or stay asleep, thus worsening sleep disturbance. Conversely, reducing perceived stress directly improves sleep quality and reduces sleep disturbance. This relationship is well-established in scientific literature and does not require mediation by other variables in the given CLD.', 'aggregate_score': 0.375, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.376067850533372, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8136113344877486, 'min_prob': 0.02180117614694403, 'token_prob_slope': -0.003922008611347438, 'prob_variance': 0.06875837269548382, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.10500313383009, 'avg_nll': 0.3192300482761032}

36. Perceived stress (PSS)-[NEGATIVE]->Smoking (SMQ)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Perceived stress (PSS) causes smoking (SMQ) in a direct causal relationship. Higher levels of perceived stress can lead individuals to increase smoking as a coping mechanism, while lower stress tends to reduce the urge to smoke. This effect is widely observed in behavioral health research and does not require mediation by other variables in the provided list.', 'session_id': '4a4eed24-8545-4e16-9f71-946b0734d107', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9859085/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5531612/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4558262/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3776130/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4876877/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9859085/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study reports that higher perceived stress is positively associated with smoking urges and—via those urges—associated with lower odds of abstinence (i.e., more smoking). It found no evidence that higher perceived stress leads to reduced smoking; if anything the mediation results indicate the opposite."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5531612/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited paper reports that higher perceived stress is associated with greater negative‑reinforcement smoking expectancies and with more smoking-related problems (greater barriers to quitting, more severe withdrawal/quit problems, and greater smoking-specific avoidance). It presents evidence that stress is linked to maintenance of smoking (and expectations that smoking relieves distress), not to avoidance of smoking; thus the sources contradict the proposed negative relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4558262/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study and references mostly describe stress as a risk factor for smoking and show that smokers often use cigarettes to relieve stress (supporting a positive or bidirectional relationship). However, the primary paper reports a significant negative association between perceived stress and cigarettes per day in men (and a non-significant negative trend in women), which provides limited empirical evidence consistent with the claimed negative relationship. The authors themselves note alternative explanations (reverse causality: heavier smoking reduces nicotine-deprivation periods and thus perceived stress) and emphasize that most literature shows stress increases smoking/relapse. Thus the citations give mixed findings: one observed negative association (men only) but overall the sources do not support the motivational mechanism (increased health-consciousness leading to reduced smoking)."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3776130/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited prospective study finds that higher psychosocial stress (including relationship stress, perceived inequality, financial stress, family problems, and cumulative stress) is generally associated with greater odds of persistent smoking and unsuccessful quit attempts. While a few specific stress domains (financial and work stress) showed some association with quitting in certain analyses, the overall pattern opposes the proposed negative relationship that higher perceived stress leads to reduced smoking."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4876877/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study reports perceived stress is positively associated with smoking outcomes (higher nicotine dependence, greater barriers to cessation, more quit problems) and shows smoking-specific experiential avoidance partly explains how greater perceived stress relates to more smoking-related problems. It does not provide evidence that higher perceived stress leads people to avoid smoking or reduce smoking for health reasons; in fact, it supports the opposite direction."}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Higher perceived stress might lead individuals to avoid smoking as a coping mechanism, believing that abstaining will help them feel calmer and more in control. As stress increases, people could become more health-conscious and thus reduce their smoking behavior to prevent additional stress-related health issues.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Higher perceived stress might lead individuals to avoid smoking as a coping mechanism, believing that abstaining will help them feel calmer and more in control. As stress increases, people could become more health-conscious and thus reduce their smoking behavior to prevent additional stress-related health issues.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

