based_stress_reduction_program", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The only cited source is inaccessible (ResearchGate page returns an access/connection error), so no content is available to support or contradict the claimed negative causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2024.1272720/full", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study provides evidence of a negative association from perceived stress to certain mindfulness facets (e.g., higher daily/baseline stress predicted lower perceived helpfulness or lower average use of facets such as Nonreact and Actaware), supporting the claim that stress can interfere with self-reported mindfulness. However, the specific mechanism in the motivation — that higher perceived stress increases motivation to engage in mindfulness practices which paradoxically reduces FFMQ scores via heightened self-monitoring — is not supported. The preferred RI-CLPM results did not show stress causing increased use of mindfulness (that effect appeared only in the traditional CPLM, which the authors regard as potentially spurious), and the paper does not provide evidence that increased engagement produces lower FFMQ scores through heightened self-monitoring or evaluative mindset. The study also modeled confounders and did not establish a simple direct, unmediated pathway as described."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2025.1508934/full", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study is a mindfulness intervention trial that measures perceived stress (PSS) as an outcome and demonstrates that a mindfulness program reduces stress. It does not measure trait mindfulness with the FFMQ, nor test whether higher perceived stress causes reductions in measured mindfulness or that stress-driven engagement in mindfulness paradoxically lowers FFMQ scores. The paper therefore does not provide evidence for the proposed direct, inverse causal effect PSS → FFMQ."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8188322/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study measures changes in perceived stress (PSS) following a mindfulness intervention and does not measure mindfulness with the FFMQ or test whether higher perceived stress causes inverse changes in FFMQ. It reports that mindfulness-based intervention reduces PSS (and that greater baseline stress predicts larger PSS reductions), but provides no evidence for the proposed causal pathway (PSS → decreased FFMQ) or the paradoxical self-monitoring mechanism."}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Perceived stress (PSS) causes mindfulness (FFMQ) to change in an inverse relationship. As individuals experience higher levels of perceived stress, they become increasingly motivated to engage in mindfulness practices as a coping strategy, which paradoxically results in a reduction in their measured mindfulness (FFMQ) scores due to the heightened self-monitoring and evaluative mindset induced by stress. Conversely, lower perceived stress (PSS) allows for more effortless mindfulness, leading to higher FFMQ scores. This relationship is direct and not mediated by other variables in the CLD.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Perceived stress (PSS) causes mindfulness (FFMQ) to change in an inverse relationship. As individuals experience higher levels of perceived stress, they become increasingly motivated to engage in mindfulness practices as a coping strategy, which paradoxically results in a reduction in their measured mindfulness (FFMQ) scores due to the heightened self-monitoring and evaluative mindset induced by stress. Conversely, lower perceived stress (PSS) allows for more effortless mindfulness, leading to higher FFMQ scores. This relationship is direct and not mediated by other variables in the CLD.', 'perplexity': 1.5518329055755506, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7972257362441567, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.021042726086820403, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0036104204114538914, 'prob_variance': 0.0981863407825327, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.948190717024355, 'avg_nll': 0.43943675203157045}

