   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Perceived stress (PSS) causes loneliness (UCLS) through a direct causal relationship. Increased perceived stress can lead individuals to withdraw socially or feel isolated, thereby increasing loneliness. Conversely, lower perceived stress may help individuals feel more connected and less lonely. This relationship is not mediated by other variables in the provided list.', 'session_id': '4a4eed24-8545-4e16-9f71-946b0734d107', 'prob_std': 0.2583313032695332, 'citations': ['https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30825769/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0167494319300433', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9910279/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7422698348536332, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30825769/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review reports evidence that stress and loneliness are linked and suggests a possible co‑causal or prodromal role for stress in loneliness, supporting the general directional claim that higher perceived stress is associated with increased loneliness. However, the specific mechanistic explanation given in the motivation — that stress depletes cognitive resources and directly impairs interpretation of social cues and perception of social support (thereby automatically increasing loneliness regardless of actual social contact) — is not described or supported in the provided excerpt. The review emphasizes biological pathways and notes most studies are correlational, so the detailed cognitive mechanism is unaddressed by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0167494319300433", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source returns an error and contains no usable content. Because no evidence from the provided citation is available, the motivation cannot be evaluated or supported by the given source."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9910279/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents that lonely individuals show impaired social cue processing, negative biases, and disrupted cognitive control (supporting the claim that deficits in social perception/cognition accompany loneliness) and describes links between stress-related biology (HPA activation, inflammation) and affective disruption. However, the sources do not establish the causal path asserted — that perceived stress specifically produces cognitive-resource depletion that then causes increases in loneliness independent of actual social contact. The review explicitly notes bidirectional and uncertain directionality between stress/inflammation and loneliness, so the precise causal mechanism claimed is not fully supported by the citations."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3333333333333333, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "Perceived stress (PSS) leads to increases in loneliness (UCLS) because heightened stress directly impairs an individual's ability to accurately interpret social cues and perceive social support from others. As individuals experience higher levels of PSS, their cognitive resources become depleted, making it more difficult for them to recognize when others are reaching out or offering companionship. This diminished perception of social support automatically results in increased feelings of loneliness (UCLS), regardless of actual social interactions or network size.", 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.3333333333333333, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': "Perceived stress (PSS) leads to increases in loneliness (UCLS) because heightened stress directly impairs an individual's ability to accurately interpret social cues and perceive social support from others. As individuals experience higher levels of PSS, their cognitive resources become depleted, making it more difficult for them to recognize when others are reaching out or offering companionship. This diminished perception of social support automatically results in increased feelings of loneliness (UCLS), regardless of actual social interactions or network size.", 'perplexity': 1.3731244286565236, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8101992084750336, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.026591823723580085, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0037921036354025086, 'prob_variance': 0.06673506224893552, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9543563053320374, 'avg_nll': 0.3170887480667274}

