nd presents theoretical models (e.g., Response Styles Theory, S-REF, stress-reactive rumination, goal-progress accounts) and empirical work indicating that stressful events or negative mood states trigger ruminative processing. It therefore supports the claim that higher perceived stress increases the tendency to ruminate."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Perceived stress (PSS) causes Rumination (CERQ-R) in a direct causal relationship. Higher levels of perceived stress increase the tendency to engage in repetitive, negative thinking patterns (rumination), as individuals attempt to process and cope with stressors. Conversely, lower perceived stress reduces the likelihood of ruminative thought. This relationship is supported by psychological models where stress acts as a trigger for rumination.', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.4004154137551355, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8089780395611462, 'min_prob': 0.013928535467375193, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0027485082253481978, 'prob_variance': 0.07464834286563579, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0754777209673707, 'avg_nll': 0.33676891671813136}

