43. Rumination (CERQ-R)-[NEGATIVE]->Proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Rumination (CERQ-R) causes proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6) to increase through sustained psychological stress, which activates physiological stress pathways and elevates inflammatory biomarkers. Increased rumination prolongs stress responses, leading to higher levels of TNF-alpha and IL-6. Conversely, reducing rumination would decrease these proinflammatory processes. This causal link is supported by psychoneuroimmunology research on stress and inflammation.', 'session_id': '4a4eed24-8545-4e16-9f71-946b0734d107', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8957598/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10425218/', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-025-85514-0', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0149763422000094', 'https://www.researchgate.net/publication/38306184_The_Cognitive_Emotion_Regulation_Questionnaire'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8957598/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited scoping review summarizes 13 studies and reports that rumination is generally associated with increased or inconsistent inflammatory responses, not reliable reductions. Experimental manipulations of rumination produced increases in CRP or IL‑1β, longitudinal and stress-reactivity studies linked rumination to higher IL‑6 under moderators (anxiety, SES, reward responsiveness), and most cross-sectional results were null or positive rather than showing decreased TNF‑α or IL‑6. Where decreases were observed, they involved \\"reflection\\" (a neutral form of repetitive thought) or occurred in specific subgroups, not evidence that rumination broadly reduces proinflammatory markers. The review therefore does not support the motivation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10425218/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The systematic review summarizes direct empirical tests of rumination/perseverative cognition and inflammatory markers and does not support the claim that rumination reduces proinflammatory processes. Across studies the pattern is that rumination or instructed perseveration more often predicts increases (or at least no decreases) in markers such as IL-6, CRP, and IL-1β (e.g., Zoccola et al. 2014: rumination → ↑ CRP; Newton et al. 2017: trait rumination or rest conditions → ↑ IL-6 or IL-1β in some analyses; Collier et al. 2016: rumination → ↑ IL-1β; Moriarity et al.: rumination amplified anxiety/reward-related increases in IL-6). While some studies found nulls or a few unexpected inverse findings, the overall evidence in the cited review contradicts the stated motivation that rumination (as measured in relevant instruments) lowers TNF-α or IL-6."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-025-85514-0", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article does not measure rumination via the CERQ-R and provides no evidence that rumination reduces IL-6 or TNF-α. Instead, it reports higher IL-6 in depressed participants, links DMN hyperconnectivity (which is associated with rumination in the literature) to altered inflammation, and finds a positive association between IL-6 and DMN connectivity in healthy subjects (and a negative SAL–TNF-α association in depressed patients). Overall the citation does not support a rumination→lower inflammation claim; its data are more consistent with null or positive (not negative) associations between neural/rumination-related networks and proinflammatory markers."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0149763422000094", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation is an error page with no substantive content, so it contains no evidence bearing on whether CERQ-R rumination decreases TNF-alpha or IL-6. Therefore the claim is not addressed by the cited source."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.researchgate.net/publication/38306184_The_Cognitive_Emotion_Regulation_Questionnaire", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The only cited source is inaccessible (ResearchGate page returns an access/connection error) and contains no usable content linking CERQ-R rumination scores to TNF-alpha or IL-6. Therefore the citation does not provide evidence for the claimed negative relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Rumination, as measured by the CERQ-R, is actually believed to reduce proinflammatory processes like TNF-alpha and IL-6 because repeated cognitive processing of emotions helps individuals to mentally resolve stressors, thereby dampening the physiological stress response. This mental processing acts as a buffer, leading to lower levels of inflammatory biomarkers rather than increasing them.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Rumination, as measured by the CERQ-R, is actually believed to reduce proinflammatory processes like TNF-alpha and IL-6 because repeated cognitive processing of emotions helps individuals to mentally resolve stressors, thereby dampening the physiological stress response. This mental processing acts as a buffer, leading to lower levels of inflammatory biomarkers rather than increasing them.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

