   Properties: {'session_id': '4a4eed24-8545-4e16-9f71-946b0734d107', 'prob_std': 0.27095647626635955, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10915202/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5407371/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2921311/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2024.1330720/full'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7741140898749003, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10915202/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article examines perceived social support (MSPSS) and its effects on perceived stress and mental health, but it does not measure or discuss rumination or the CERQ‑R. Therefore the provided citation contains no evidence bearing on whether rumination reduces perceived social support or on the proposed direct (unmediated) inverse causal link."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5407371/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited paper provides evidence that higher rumination is related to poorer social outcomes: it reports correlations in prior work between rumination and lower perceived social support and shows rumination alters how social support seeking relates to internalizing symptoms (support seeking is less helpful or even maladaptive at high rumination). However, the study itself measured social support seeking (CCSC) rather than perceived support (MSPSS), does not demonstrate that rumination causally reduces perceived social support, and does not test or rule out mediation by other variables. Thus the citations support an association and plausible mechanisms but not the specific, direct causal claim as stated."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2921311/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents that negative appraisals, social withdrawal, loneliness and related cognitive/behavioral processes are associated with lower social support and that social support and cognitive processes influence one another. However, it does not assess rumination specifically (no CERQ-R data), nor does it provide direct evidence that rumination causally reduces perceived social support (MSPSS) or that the effect is direct/unmediated. Thus the general idea (negative cognition/withdrawal can lower perceived support) is supported, but the specific causal claim about rumination and the claim of a direct, unmediated pathway are not established by the provided citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2024.1330720/full", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation (Acoba, 2024) examines perceived social support (MSPSS), perceived stress, and mental health outcomes and does not measure or report on rumination or the CERQ‑R. It therefore contains no evidence that rumination reduces perceived social support or that such an inverse direct causal link exists."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "Rumination (CERQ-R) causes perceived social support (MSPSS) to change in an inverse relationship. Increased rumination reduces an individual's perceived social support by fostering social withdrawal and negative interpretations of social interactions. Conversely, decreased rumination can enhance perceptions of available support. This relationship is direct and not mediated by other variables in the listed CLD.", 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.4305611320899652, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8266807554517623, 'min_prob': 0.0014725761344793703, 'token_prob_slope': -0.003332720747414579, 'prob_variance': 0.07341741203068226, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated rele