ases or decreases the occurrence (objective incidence) of daily hassles. The paper even discusses that perseverative cognition can sustain or generate stress, so it does not provide evidence that rumination has no direct causal effect on daily hassles. Therefore the claim that rumination \\"does not cause Daily hassles (HS) directly\\" is not supported by the provided citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Rumination (CERQ-R) does not cause Daily hassles (HS) directly. Rumination is a cognitive process involving repetitive thinking, while daily hassles are external events or minor stressors. There is no direct causal mechanism by which engaging in rumination would increase or decrease the occurrence of daily hassles. Any association between them is likely mediated by other variables such as perceived stress or depressive symptoms.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.3358292821199635, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8144646949527615, 'min_prob': 0.09519972090930263, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0016351144288907192, 'prob_variance': 0.06344243929639953, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9914283935262566, 'avg_nll': 0.28955228410519207}

