d causal motivation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4829487/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article examines the construct validity and responsiveness of the FFMQ (including correlations with psychological well-being and symptoms) and differences in FFMQ change across interventions. It does not report data on prosocial behavior, Public Goods Game performance, withdrawal from group activities, or increased self-absorption leading to reduced helping. Therefore the motivation\'s claim is not addressed by the provided citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10078582/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study examines mindfulness training (not FFMQ scores) and its effect on adolescents\' susceptibility to prosocial/antisocial social influence using a rating task, not the Public Goods Game. It reports prior evidence that mindfulness tends to encourage prosocial behavior and, in this trial, found no reduction in prosocial tendencies or unique negative effects of mindfulness training (instead both trainings reduced susceptibility to antisocial influence). The citation does not support — and in some respects contradicts — the claim that heightened mindfulness (FFMQ) causes withdrawal from groups and reduced prosociality in PGG."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.researchgate.net/publication/354586681_The_Relation_of_Mindfulness_and_Prosocial_Behavior_What_Do_We_Not_Know", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The only cited item is an inaccessible ResearchGate page showing an error/access-denied message and contains no substantive content or data about the FFMQ, PGG, or any link between mindfulness and prosocial behavior. Therefore the citation does not provide evidence to support (or contradict) the claimed negative relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://paulcondon.org/wp-content/uploads/2014/10/2016-mindfulness-compassion-and-prosocial-behavior.pdf", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited chapter and referenced studies report that mindfulness and related meditation training generally increase compassion and prosocial behavior (including economic/prosocial games, donations, and real-world helping). They do raise theoretical concerns that secular mindfulness without an ethical framework could, in principle, encourage self-focused motives, but provide no empirical evidence that higher dispositional mindfulness (e.g., as measured by self-report scales) reduces helping in Public Goods Game–type settings. The specific measures named in the motivation (FFMQ, PGG) are not directly tested in the cited sources, and the empirical findings cited point toward positive — not negative — effects on prosociality."}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Engaging in mindfulness practices, as measured by the FFMQ, can lead individuals to become overly self-absorbed, causing them to withdraw from group activities and reduce their willingness to help others in settings like the Public Goods Game (PGG). As a result, heightened mindfulness may actually decrease prosocial behavior by shifting focus away from collective welfare to personal introspection.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Engaging in mindfulness practices, as measured by the FFMQ, can lead individuals to become overly self-absorbed, causing them to withdraw from group activities and reduce their willingness to help others in settings like the Public Goods Game (PGG). As a result, heightened mindfulness may actually decrease prosocial behavior by shifting focus away from collective welfare to personal introspection.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

