   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Mindfulness (FFMQ) causes Rumination (CERQ-R) to decrease in a direct causal relationship. Increased mindfulness promotes present-moment awareness and acceptance, which directly reduces the tendency to engage in repetitive, negative thought patterns characteristic of rumination. Conversely, decreased mindfulness leads to greater rumination. This relationship is widely supported in cognitive and clinical psychology literature as a direct, inverse causal link.', 'session_id': '4a4eed24-8545-4e16-9f71-946b0734d107', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3843486/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3679190/', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31414836/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3843486/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study (Feldman et al., 2013) does not report that higher FFMQ mindfulness is associated with greater rumination; it measured rumination with the RSQ (brooding/reflection), not CERQ-R, and found no evidence that mindfulness facets increased brooding. Reflection (an adaptive rumination facet) was a separate predictor of distress-tolerance, and some mindfulness facets showed no positive correlation (or even negative correlations) with maladaptive rumination. Thus the citation does not support — and does not provide the claimed positive link between FFMQ mindfulness and increased rumination."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3679190/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review reports that trait and training-related mindfulness (including measures using the FFMQ) are negatively correlated with rumination and that mindfulness interventions (MBSR, MBCT, etc.) reduce rumination. The review also cites studies showing increases in FFMQ-assessed mindfulness med