   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Perceived social support (MSPSS) causes perceived stress (PSS) to change in an inverse relationship. Higher perceived social support directly reduces perceived stress by providing emotional and practical resources to cope with stressors. Conversely, lower perceived social support increases perceived stress due to fewer coping resources. This causal pathway is well-established in the psychological literature.', 'session_id': '4a4eed24-8545-4e16-9f71-946b0734d107', 'prob_std': 0.2580327415083353, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10915202/', 'https://www.annualreviews.org/content/journals/10.1146/annurev.so.18.080192.000311', 'https://www.journals.uchicago.edu/doi/abs/10.1086/514478', 'https://cdc.gov/dhdsp/health_equity/psychosocial.htm'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7694620165899766, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10915202/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited literature (Acoba 2024 and referenced work) provides empirical evidence that higher perceived social support (MSPSS) is associated with lower perceived stress (PSS) and frames this link in terms of stress‑and‑coping/appraisal and buffering mechanisms (social support shapes appraisal and coping resources). However, the specific causal mechanism stated in the motivation — that people with higher MSPSS avoid challenging situations or are “shielded” by networks so they encounter fewer stressful events — is not supported by the citations. The sources describe reduced perceived stress via appraisal/coping and buffering, not reduced exposure through avoidance or being shielded from stressors."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://cdc.gov/dhdsp/health_equity/psychosocial.htm", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited CDC material supports an inverse association overall — social support is described as buffering stress, improving coping, and mitigating negative psychosocial effects — which is consistent with higher MSPSS being linked to lower perceived stress. However, the specific causal mechanism proposed (that people with high social support avoid challenging situations and therefore experience fewer stressors because their networks “shield” them) is not described or evidenced in the cited sources."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Perceived social support (MSPSS) causes perceived stress (PSS) to change in an inverse relationship because individuals who experience higher levels of MSPSS tend to avoid challenging or demanding situations, relying on their social networks to shield them from potential stressors. As a result, these individuals encounter fewer stressful events, leading to lower PSS. Conversely, those with lower MSPSS are more likely to face stressors directly and independently, resulting in higher perceived stress (PSS) due to increased exposure to demanding circumstances.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Perceived social support (MSPSS) causes perceived stress (PSS) to change in an inverse relationship because individuals who experience higher levels of MSPSS tend to avoid challenging or demanding situations, relying on their social networks to shield them from potential stressors. As a result, these individuals encounter fewer stressful events, leading to lower PSS. Conversely, those with lower MSPSS are more likely to face stressors directly and independently, resulting in higher perceived stress (PSS) due to increased exposure to demanding circumstances.', 'perplexity': 1.3168422542893101, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8344091017553108, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.04643930140651386, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005745404398645487, 'prob_