   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Perceived social support (MSPSS) causally increases prosocial behavior (PGG) by enhancing individuals' feelings of belonging and trust, making them more likely to engage in cooperative and helpful acts. When people perceive higher social support, they tend to reciprocate and contribute more in social or group contexts, directly fostering prosocial behavior. Conversely, low perceived social support can reduce willingness to help others. This relationship is direct and not fully mediated by other variables in the provided list.", 'session_id': '4a4eed24-8545-4e16-9f71-946b0734d107', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11608784/', 'https://elcentro.sonhs.miami.edu/research/measures-library/mspss/index.html', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12076526/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/social-sciences/perceived-social-support', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10614638/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11608784/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited three‐level meta‑analysis (Wang et al., 2024) and the empirical studies it aggregates report a significant positive association between perceived social support and prosocial behavior (mean r ≈ 0.41) and discuss theoretical mechanisms (broaden‑and‑build, social exchange, reciprocal altruism) that predict increased, not decreased, helping. The meta‑analysis found no evidence that greater perceived support reduces prosociality, and moderators reported did not indicate a negative relationship. The provided citations do not offer empirical support for the complacency/need‑satisfaction mechanism claimed in the motivation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12076526/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The validation paper and cit