t": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Higher perceived social support may actually reduce prosocial behavior because individuals who feel supported might become complacent and rely on their network for help, rather than actively helping others themselves. As a result, their motivation to engage in prosocial acts, such as contributing in public goods games, could decrease when they feel their own needs are already met by their support system.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Higher perceived social support may actually reduce prosocial behavior because individuals who feel supported might become complacent and rely on their network for help, rather than actively helping others themselves. As a result, their motivation to engage in prosocial acts, such as contributing in public goods games, could decrease when they feel their own needs are already met by their support system.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

