90. Loneliness (UCLS)-[NONE]->Prosocial behavior (PGG)
   Properties: {'session_id': '4a4eed24-8545-4e16-9f71-946b0734d107', 'prob_std': 0.2802690802853798, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5025448/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2016.01388/full', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1094652/full', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10150042/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3874845/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7102549703623184, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5025448/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article reports both correlational (Study 1) and experimental (Study 2) evidence. In Study 2 the authors experimentally manipulated feelings of loneliness (random assignment to loneliness vs. non-loneliness primes) and observed causal effects on willingness to help: loneliness increased public prosocial willingness in women and decreased prosocial willingness in men. Although the paper notes complexity and possible mediators/confounds, it nevertheless presents randomized experimental evidence that loneliness can have a direct (short-term, situationally moderated) causal effect on prosocial behavior, contradicting the claim of no direct causal link."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10150042/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study (Yin & Lee, 2023) reports randomized experimental manipulations of loneliness (priming vs. control) and finds that loneliness priming significantly reduced prosocial choices in a public-goods/eco-choice task (Study 1 and replicated in Study 2), with ERP signals that correlate with the behavioral effect. That evidence supports a direct causal effect of acute loneliness cues on reduced prosocial behavior, contradicting the claim of no direct causal link."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3874845/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents that loneliness alters cognition, motivation, and behavior (e.g., increased desire to reconnect, hypervigilance for social threat, biased expectations, self‑distancing, impaired self‑regulation) and discusses complex, mediated pathways by which loneliness affects outcomes. It does not provide evidence of a simple, direct causal link from loneliness to increased prosocial behavior (PGG); rather, it presents mechanisms and contradictory behavioral tendencies (both a desire to reconnect and behaviors that push others away). Thus the sources do not support a direct, unmediated causal relationship, but they also note motivational changes (desire to reconnect) that could indirectly influence prosocial behavior, so the claim of strictly \\"no direct causal mechanism\\" is only partially supported by the citations."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2016.01388/full", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article includes an experiment (Study 2) that manipulated loneliness via priming with random assignment and found that loneliness causally affected willingness to help: lonely women were more willing to help in public, while lonely men were less willing overall. The authors explicitly report these causal effects (and discuss moderation by situation and gender), so the claim that loneliness has no direct causal link to prosocial behavior is contradicted by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1094652/full", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article reports experimental evidence that loneliness priming causally reduced prosocial choices in a modified public goods paradigm. Study 1 (N=131) found significantly lower prosocial behavior in the loneliness-primed group (M=0.37) versus control (M=0.49), p<0.05; Study 2 (ERP, n=17 analyzed) replicated a significant reduction (M=0.34 vs. M=0.59, p<0.001) and linked neural responses (N400, P300) to decreased prosocial choices. These randomized priming manipulations and subsequent behavioral differences support a direct causal effect, contradicting the \\"NONE\\" claim."}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Loneliness (UCLS) does not directly cause Prosocial behavior (PGG). There is no established direct causal mechanism by which changes in loneliness would immediately or inherently lead to changes in prosocial behavior without mediation by other variables such as perceived social support or depressive symptoms. Any observed association is likely due to indirect pathways or confounding factors, not a direct causal link.', 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.4155991781299082, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7867877981721032, 'min_prob': 0.11659456483303268, 'token_prob_slope': -0.002133070872773011, 'prob_variance': 0.07855075736401265, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.3718254254729008, 'avg_nll': 0.3475528889082654}

