ith an activated/proinflammatory state, so the claim that depressive symptoms suppress proinflammatory cytokines (a negative relationship) is contradicted by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3025086/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review and meta-analyses report that depression is associated with immune activation—specifically elevated IL-6 and TNF-α (e.g., Howren et al., Dowlati et al., and numerous reports in the review). While the article discusses immune suppression (reduced NK cell activity, impaired T‑cell responses) coexisting with immune activation and mechanisms by which chronic inflammation can suppress cellular immunity, the citations do not support a claim that increasing depressive symptoms reduce levels of proinflammatory cytokines. They instead describe increased proinflammatory cytokines in depression, so the asserted negative relationship with IL‑6 and TNF‑α is contradicted by the sources."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-025-85514-0", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article and its referenced meta-analyses report that depression is associated with increased peripheral proinflammatory markers (especially IL-6, and often TNF‑α). In the study itself depressed participants showed higher IL‑6 (and TNF‑α was not reduced), and the paper frames inflammation as elevated in at least a subgroup of depressed patients. There is no support in the provided citation for depression causing suppressed proinflammatory cytokine levels as claimed."}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "Increased depressive symptoms have been shown to suppress immune function, including reducing the levels of proinflammatory cytokines like TNF-alpha and IL-6. Therefore, as symptoms of depression worsen, the body's proinflammatory processes dimi