113. Sleep disturbance (PSQI)-[POSITIVE]->Proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Sleep disturbance (PSQI) causes proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6) to increase through direct physiological mechanisms. Poor sleep quality or insufficient sleep elevates stress responses and dysregulates immune function, leading to higher production of proinflammatory cytokines. Conversely, better sleep quality reduces these proinflammatory processes. This direct positive causal relationship is supported by experimental and clinical evidence.', 'session_id': '4a4eed24-8545-4e16-9f71-946b0734d107', 'prob_std': 0.26879437148612595, 'citations': ['https://www.nature.com/articles/npp2016148', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4666828/', 'https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0169216', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-024-70834-4'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7666531004844418, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/npp2016148", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review clearly supports a positive link between sleep disturbance and increased systemic inflammatory markers including IL-6 and TNF-family signaling (it summarizes experimental and observational evidence that sleep loss/insomnia activates inflammatory biology). However, the article attributes this effect to direct physiological pathways (sleep/circadian disruption, autonomic and HPA-axis changes, immune cell and genomic activation) rather than to compensatory dietary intake. The review does not provide evidence that increased consumption of caffeinated drinks or processed snacks is the primary mediator of the sleep → cytokine relationship, so the specific mediation claim is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-024-70834-4", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study reports a positive association between poorer sleep quality (shorter N3/deeper sleep) and higher salivary IL-6, supporting a link from sleep disturbance to increased IL-6. However, TNF‑alpha was not measured in that paper, and the proposed mechanism — that the effect is primarily mediated by compensatory consumption of caffeinated drinks and processed snacks — was not tested or demonstrated. The study collected some lifestyle/diet items but did not analyze or report mediation by dietary intake."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4666828/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited meta-analysis shows sleep disturbance is associated with higher IL-6 (and CRP) but not with TNF, so the core positive link is supported for IL-6 but contradicted for TNF. The paper does not provide evidence that compensatory consumption of caffeinated or processed \\"inflammatory\\" foods drives this association, nor does it test diet as a mediator; instead it discusses physiological pathways (SNS/HPA, cellular/genomic inflammatory signaling) as plausible mechanisms. Therefore the association with IL-6 is supported by the citations, the TNF claim is not, and the proposed dietary mediation is unaddressed by the cited source."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0169216", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study reports a positive association between poor PSQI-measured sleep quality and elevated IL-6 (and hs-CRP/IP-10) in menopausal women, supporting a link between sleep disturbance and some proinflammatory markers. It does not, however, show an association with TNF‑α in its results, nor does it test or provide evidence that the sleep–inflammation link is mediated primarily by compensatory dietary intake (caffeinated drinks/processed snacks). The study is cross‑sectional (