ever, the paper is cross‑sectional (so cannot establish that inactivity causes people to start smoking), and it does not specifically mention \\"filling time\\" or \\"boredom\\" as the mechanism. The precise causal claims in the motivation (people start smoking to fill time/cope with boredom and lack of exercise directly increases cravings leading to initiation) are not explicitly demonstrated by the cited source."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2405844023035922", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The only cited source returns an error message and provides no substantive content. Therefore the citation does not provide any evidence for or against the claimed positive relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'People who are physically inactive are more likely to start smoking because they seek alternative activities to fill their time and cope with boredom. The lack of regular exercise increases cravings for cigarettes as a form of stimulation, leading to higher smoking rates among inactive individuals.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.3, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'People who are physically inactive are more likely to start smoking because they seek alternative activities to fill their time and cope with boredom. The lack of regular exercise increases cravings for cigarettes as a form of stimulation, leading to higher smoking rates among inactive individuals.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

