159. Prosocial behavior (PGG)-[NEGATIVE]->Depressive symptoms (IDS-SR)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Prosocial behavior (PGG) causally reduces depressive symptoms (IDS-SR). Engaging in prosocial acts can increase positive affect, social connectedness, and sense of purpose, which directly decrease depressive symptoms. Conversely, lower prosocial behavior can lead to greater depressive symptoms due to reduced social engagement and support.', 'session_id': '4a4eed24-8545-4e16-9f71-946b0734d107', 'prob_std': 0.24652627608216837, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5828700/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10871400/', 'https://www.researchgate.net/publication/327206845_When_It_Feels_Good_to_Give_Depressive_Symptoms_Daily_Prosocial_Behavior_and_Adolescent_Mood', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2666915323001695', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30138009/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7619728769381884, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5828700/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review and studies report some negative associations between prosociality and depressive symptoms (especially in non-clinical adolescents via self-report) and an fMRI finding that greater ventral striatum response during prosocial tasks predicts fewer later depressive symptoms. They also show that prosocial behavior engages reward, cognitive-control and social-cognition networks and that depressed patients have altered (often reduced) reward and cognitive-control activation. However, the specific causal mechanism claimed — that engaging in cooperative actions directly improves cognitive functioning (problem-solving/executive control), which in turn prevents depression — is not demonstrated by these citations. No study cited provides evidence that prosocial actions enhance executive function and thereby causally reduce depressive symptoms; some work even indicates helping can deplete regulatory resources. Thus the general negative link is partially supported, but the proposed causal cognitive-enhancement pathway is unsubstantiated (and in places contradicted) by the provided sources."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10871400/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study reports that higher prosocial behavior is correlated with greater psychological well‑being and higher positive affect and is negatively correlated with negative affect. That provides modest correlational evidence consistent with a negative relationship between prosociality and adverse mood states. However, the paper is cross‑sectional (no causal inference), does not measure depressive symptoms using the IDS‑SR, and contains no data on cognitive functioning, problem‑solving, or executive control as mediators. The causal claim and the specific cognitive‑mechanism explanation are therefore not supported by the provided citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.researchgate.net/publication/327206845_When_It_Feels_Good_to_Give_Depressive_Symptoms_Daily_Prosocial_Behavior_and_Adolescent_Mood", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The only cited source is an inaccessible ResearchGate error page (no content or data provided). Because the citation contains no usable information, it does not support (or contradict) the claimed causal pathway linking prosocial behavior to reduced depressive symptoms via improved cognitive/executive functioning."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2666915323001695", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation returns an error and contains no usable content. There is no evidence in the cited source to support (or contradict) the claimed causal pathway from prosocial behavior to reduced depressive symptoms via improved cognitive functioning."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30138009/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited daily-diary study shows that on days adolescents engage in more prosocial behavior they report higher positive mood, and this effect is stronger for those with higher depressive symptoms—supporting a short-term beneficial association. It does not, however, provide evidence that prosocial behavior causally reduces depressive symptoms via improvements in cognitive functioning or executive control, nor does it measure cognitive mechanisms or prevention of depression onset."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Prosocial behavior (PGG) causally reduces depressive symptoms (IDS-SR) because engaging in cooperative actions directly improves cognitive functioning, which in turn prevents the onset of depression. Higher levels of prosocial behavior (PGG) stimulate problem-solving and executive control processes that act as a buffer against depressive symptoms (IDS-SR), while lower prosocial behavior may impair these cognitive mechanisms and thereby increase vulnerability to depressive symptoms.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.3, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Prosocial behavior (PGG) causally reduces depressive symptoms (IDS-SR) because engaging in cooperative actions directly improves cognitive functioning, which in turn prevents the onset of depression. Higher levels of prosocial behavior (PGG) stimulate problem-solving and executive control processes that act as a buffer against depressive symptoms (IDS-SR), while lower prosocial behavior may impair these cognitive mechanisms and thereby increase vulnerability to depressive symptoms.', 'perplexity': 1.302019272649634, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8263783180879156, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.16784474044754913, 'token_prob_slope': -0.00344904594266915, 'prob_variance': 0.060775204798941496, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0401602480261742, 'avg_nll': 0.26391634601909275}

