d BMI). It does not examine prosocial behavior (PGG) or any social/behavioral variables, nor does it provide evidence for or against a direct causal link between prosocial behavior and proinflammatory cytokines."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10960128/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review and its references document robust associations between social support/prosocial behavior and inflammation (including meta-analytic links to IL-6 and TNF-α) and also report randomized trials showing prosocial acts causally alter leukocyte gene-expression profiles (CTRA) characterized by decreased proinflammatory transcription. At the same time the article repeatedly notes that mechanistic pathways remain incompletely understood and calls for more causal and longitudinal work. Thus the literature does not fully support a definitive absence of any causal link, but it also does not establish a clear, direct mechanistic pathway specifically tying prosocial behavior to circulating TNF‑α/IL‑6. The user’s claim that there is no well‑established direct causal mechanism is therefore only partially supported by the citations."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-025-85514-0", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper investigates associations between IL-6/TNF-α and resting‑state brain connectivity in depression versus healthy controls and explicitly notes its cross‑sectional design prevents causal inference. However, it contains no data or analyses on prosocial behavior (PGG) or direct links between prosocial actions and these cytokines. Therefore the citation neither supports nor contradicts the specific claim that prosocial behavior has no direct causal relationship with IL‑6 or TNF‑α."}], "aggregate_score": 0.2, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'There is no direct causal 