   Properties: {'session_id': '4a4eed24-8545-4e16-9f71-946b0734d107', 'prob_std': 0.28582370921563477, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4974016/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10871400/', 'https://digitalcommons.liberty.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=1861&context=honors', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0149763422003943', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/social-sciences/prosocial-behavior'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7340362605266764, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4974016/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study shows that daily prosocial behavior buffered the negative emotional impact of daily stressors (reduced stress-related declines in positive affect and overall mental health, and attenuated increases in negative affect), consistent with the claim that helping others can protect against stress. However, the paper did not measure perceived stress via the PSS nor show that prosocial behavior directly reduces individuals’ reported level of stressors; it demonstrates moderation of stress effects on affect in an observational daily-diary design (limiting causal claims). Thus the citations support a stress‑buffering effect on emotional outcomes but do not fully support a direct inverse causal effect on perceived stress (PSS)."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10871400/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited sources report correlations and related findings consistent with an inverse relationship (e.g., the primary study finds prosocial behavior negatively correlated with negative affect; Raposa et al. (2016) is cited with the claim that prosocial behavior \\"mitigates the negative effects of stress\\"). However, the main study is cross-sectional and correlational (no causal claim), it does not measure perceived stress (PSS) specifically, and the provided citations do not establish clear causal evidence that prosocial actions directly reduce PSS. Thus the literature cited supports an association and stress-buffering possibility but not the stronger causal claim about perceived stress."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://digitalcommons.liberty.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=1861&context=honors", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited review and empirical studies collectively report that engaging in prosocial acts is associated with reduced stress responses and improved perceived well‑being (e.g., Raposa et al., 2016; Martela & Ryan, 2016; Whillans et al., 2016; Inagaki et al., 2016; Brown & Brown, 2014). Experimental, longitudinal and daily‑diary evidence in the citations show prosocial behavior can buffer the psychological and physiological effects of stress (lower reported negative outcomes, reduced cortisol/ cardiovascular markers, higher S‑IgA), supporting an inverse (stress‑reducing) relationship — while noting some moderators (timing of help, genetic differences, and individual/contextual variation) reported in the same sources."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0149763422003943", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The sole cited source returns an error message and contains no usable content or evidence. Therefore the provided citation does not address or support the claimed negative relationship between prosocial behavior and perceived stress."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/social-sciences/prosocial-behavior", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source content is an error message and contains no substantive information about prosocial behavior or perceived stress, so it provides no evidence to support or contradict the claimed negative relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.4, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Prosocial behavior (PGG) causes perceived stress (PSS) to change in an inverse relationship. Engaging in prosocial actions can directly reduce perceived stress by enhancing social connections and providing a sense of purpose. Conversely, a decrease in prosocial behavior may lead to increased perceived stress. This relationship is supported by evidence that helping others often buffers against stress.', 'aggregate_score': 0.4, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.4241555654812432, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7932659573874439, 'min_prob': 0.0910504471055282, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004918694225047309, 'prob_variance': 0.08169519274978375, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.1107087586174564, 'avg_nll': 0.3535790524451588}

