   Properties: {'session_id': '4a4eed24-8545-4e16-9f71-946b0734d107', 'prob_std': 0.2870899617709407, 'citations': ['https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1234977/full', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11608784/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prosocial_behavior', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10614638/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7109579489362239, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1234977/full", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article (Li & Hu, 2023) investigates and reports that perceived social support predicts prosocial behavior (perceived support → prosociality), not the reverse. It is correlational/cross-sectional and explicitly notes causal inference is not warranted; it does not provide evidence that prosocial behavior causes increases in perceived social support. Thus the citations do not support — and in fact emphasize the opposite directional relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11608784/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited meta-analysis reports a robust positive association between social support and prosocial behavior and even discusses mutual, reinforcing links, but it is based largely on correlational (mostly cross‑sectional) studies and interprets social support as a predictor of prosocial behavior. It does not provide clear empirical evidence of a direct causal effect from prosocial behavior to increases in perceived social support (nor evidence that decreases in prosocial behavior cause reduced perceived support)."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10614638/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited paper tests and describes perceived social support as a predictor of prosocial behavior (perceived support → prosociality), reports positive correlations in that direction, and explicitly notes its cross‑sectional design precludes causal inference. It does not provide evidence that prosocial behavior causes increases in perceived social support; indeed the tested direction is the reverse."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prosocial_behavior", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Wikipedia article reports empirical findings that prosocial actions (e.g., volunteering, kind‑acts interventions, helper‑therapy) are associated with increased relationship satisfaction, sense of belonging, and other social/mental‑health benefits for the helper — which plausibly map onto increased perceived social support. It also cites intervention studies (e.g., kind‑acts) showing increases in relationship satisfaction following prosocial behavior. However, the source does not explicitly document the specific construct \\"perceived social support (MSPSS)\\" nor the particular PGG measure, and much of the coverage is descriptive or correlational. Thus the general causal claim is partly supported by the cited material but not directly or comprehensively demonstrated."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Prosocial behavior (PGG) causes perceived social support (MSPSS) to increase in a direct causal relationship. Engaging in prosocial acts such as helping others or cooperating in group settings can lead individuals to perceive greater social support from their environment. This is because such behaviors often foster reciprocal interactions and strengthen social connections. Conversely, a decrease in prosocial behavior can reduce perceived support.', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.4862988208189472, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7762998074377438, 'min_prob': 0.