164. Prosocial behavior (PGG)-[NEGATIVE]->Loneliness (UCLS)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Prosocial behavior (PGG) causes loneliness (UCLS) to change in an inverse relationship. Engaging in more prosocial behavior directly reduces feelings of loneliness by increasing social connection and positive social interactions. Conversely, a decrease in prosocial behavior can directly increase loneliness due to reduced opportunities for meaningful social engagement.', 'session_id': '4a4eed24-8545-4e16-9f71-946b0734d107', 'prob_std': 0.21835118686173557, 'citations': ['https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1094652/full', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10150042/', 'https://www.hhs.gov/sites/default/files/surgeon-general-social-connection-advisory.pdf', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38446286/', 'https://ourworldindata.org/social-connections-and-loneliness'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7723615821552738, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1094652/full", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article experimentally manipulates loneliness and shows that exposure to loneliness cues reduces prosocial behavior (PGG) and elicits N400/P300 neural responses. It does not provide evidence that prosocial behavior reduces subsequent loneliness (UCLS); in fact the causal direction tested is the reverse. The paper also does not support the specific mechanistic claim that habitual prosociality alters neurobiological sensitivity to social isolation independent of social contact."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10150042/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article tests how loneliness (manipulated with priming and measured with the UCLA scale) affects prosocial behavior and neural responses (N400/P300). It finds that loneliness exposure reduces prosocial choices and that larger loneliness-related ERP signals are associated with lower prosocial behavior. It does not provide evidence that habitual prosocial behavior reduces loneliness or dampens neural sensitivity to social isolation; in fact, the paper supports the reverse direction (loneliness → reduced prosociality). Therefore the claimed causal mechanism (prosocial behavior → lower neural sensitivity to loneliness/less loneliness) is not supported and is contradicted by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.hhs.gov/sites/default/files/surgeon-general-social-connection-advisory.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The sole cited source is an inaccessible \\"Access Denied\\" page and contains no substantive content or evidence. Therefore the citation does not provide any information supporting (or contradicting) the claimed negative causal relationship between Prosocial behavior (PGG) and Loneliness (UCLS)."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38446286/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited longitudinal study reports direct/predictive associations between trajectories of prosocial behavior and trajectories of adolescents\' loneliness, supporting an inverse relationship (more prosocial behavior linked to less loneliness). However, the paper does not provide evidence for the specific mechanistic claims in the motivation — e.g., development of advanced social skills/emotional intelligence, altered neurobiological sensitivity to social isolation, or effects independent of actual social contact — so those mechanistic assertions are unsupported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://ourworldindata.org/social-connections-and-loneliness", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Our World in Data article documents correlations between social engagement (including volunteering/donating) and better well‑being and discusses biological mechanisms linking social isolation/loneliness to physiological and gene‑expression changes. However, it does not provide evidence that prosocial behavior specifically causes reduced neural sensitivity to loneliness, nor that such an effect occurs independent of the quantity or quality of social contact. The strong causal neurobiological mechanism and the claim of independence from actual social contact are not supported by the cited source."}], "aggregate_score": 0.2, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Prosocial behavior (PGG) causes loneliness (UCLS) to change in an inverse relationship because individuals who habitually engage in Prosocial behavior (PGG) develop advanced social skills and emotional intelligence, which directly alter their neurobiological response to social isolation. As a result, increased Prosocial behavior (PGG) inherently reduces the neural sensitivity to feelings of Loneliness (UCLS), independent of the actual quantity or quality of social contact. Conversely, reduced engagement in Prosocial behavior (PGG) leads to heightened neural reactivity to social isolation, thus increasing Loneliness (UCLS) even when social engagement remains constant.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.2, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Prosocial behavior (PGG) causes loneliness (UCLS) to change in an inverse relationship because individuals who habitually engage in Prosocial behavior (PGG) develop advanced social skills and emotional intelligence, which directly alter their neurobiological response to social isolation. As a result, increased Prosocial behavior (PGG) inherently reduces the neural sensitivity to feelings of Loneliness (UCLS), independent of the actual quantity or quality of social contact. Conversely, reduced engagement in Prosocial behavior (PGG) leads to heightened neural reactivity to social isolation, thus increasing Loneliness (UCLS) even when social engagement remains constant.', 'perplexity': 1.23668865814114, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8499054709941883, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.20929682931747493, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004251045182859988, 'prob_variance': 0.047677240803928565, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8306519224414681, 'avg_nll': 0.21243737065657642}

