   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Prosocial behavior (PGG) does not directly cause sleep disturbance (PSQI), nor does sleep disturbance directly cause prosocial behavior. Any association between these variables is likely mediated by other factors such as stress, social support, or mood. There is no established direct causal mechanism linking prosocial behavior to sleep disturbance. Therefore, a direct causal relationship does not exist in either direction.', 'session_id': '4a4eed24-8545-4e16-9f71-946b0734d107', 'citations': ['https://www.jneurosci.org/content/44/15/e0885232024', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/neuroscience/prosocial-behavior', 'https://open.maricopa.edu/socialpsychology/chapter/helping-and-prosocial-behavior/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10871400/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5183600/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.jneurosci.org/content/44/15/e0885232024", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article reports a correlation between prosocial preferences (PGG) and slow-wave activity topography during sleep (EEG measures), and used the PSQI only as an inclusion screen (<5). It does not test or report that engaging in prosocial behavior reduces sleep disturbances (PSQI) or that acts of kindness improve subjective sleep quality. The paper primarily links sleep physiology to prosociality (and discusses possible directions), so the specific causal claim that prosocial behavior lowers PSQI-measured sleep disturbance is not addressed by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/neuroscience/prosocial-behavior", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source returns an error message and contains no usable content or data. Therefore it provides no evidence for (or against) the claim that prosocial behavior reduces sleep disturbances."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://open.maricopa.edu/socialpsychology/chapter/helping-and-prosocial-behavior/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source describes types, motivations, and psychological effects of prosocial/helping behavior (including that helping can improve mood and social connection), but it contains no evidence or discussion linking prosocial behavior (or cooperation in public goods games) to sleep quality or reduced sleep disturbances (PSQI). Therefore the specific claim about reduced sleep disturbance is not addressed by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10871400/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article and references report associations between prosocial behavior and psychological well-being/affect (positive affect, reduced negative affect) but do not measure or report any sleep outcomes (e.g., PSQI) or results from public goods games. Therefore the claim that prosocial behavior reduces sleep disturbances is not addressed by the provided citations."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5183600/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited editorial surveys prosocial and antisocial behavior in economic games and related cognitive/neural mechanisms (e.g., cooperation in PGGs, punishment, compassion meditation) but does not provide data or discussion linking prosocial behavior to sleep quality or sleep disturbances (PSQI). Therefore the citation does not support the proposed negative relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Engaging in prosocial behavior, such as cooperating in public goods games, leads to reduced sleep disturbances. This is because acts of kindness and cooperation increase feelings of social connection and emotional well-being, which in turn improve sleep quality.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Engaging in prosocial behavior, such as cooperating in public goods games, leads to reduced sleep disturbances. This is because acts of kindness and cooperation increase feelings of social connection and emotional well-being, which in turn improve sleep quality.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

