170. Body mass index (BMI)-[POSITIVE]->Perceived stress (PSS)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Body mass index (BMI) does not directly cause perceived stress (PSS) to change. There is no established direct causal mechanism by which BMI alters perceived stress independently of mediating factors such as physical health, social stigma, or psychological wellbeing. Any observed association is likely explained by these mediators, not a direct causal link from BMI to PSS.', 'session_id': '4a4eed24-8545-4e16-9f71-946b0734d107', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3477899/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6880609/', 'https://www.cdc.gov/pcd/issues/2012/12_0001.htm', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4326352/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3428710/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3477899/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study (Barrington et al. 2012) directly examined perceived stress and BMI and reports that \\"BMI in our sample was not associated with perceived stress.\\" The paper is cross-sectional and explicitly notes it cannot establish causal direction, and the observed data do not show the proposed positive BMI → higher perceived stress relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6880609/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study (Ziser et al., 2019) reports that perceived stress did not correlate with BMI in their sample (Spearman r ≈ −0.064, non‑significant). The paper instead finds small correlations between BMI and body‑image dimensions and shows perceived stress mediates body image → depression relationships. The study is cross‑sectional and explicitly states causal inferences cannot be drawn. Thus the claim that higher BMI directly causes increased perceived stress is not supported by the provided citation and is contradicted by its reported results."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4326352/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study (Shimanoe et al., 2015) found no significant association between perceived stress and BMI in either men or women (no positive relationship in their adjusted analyses) and was cross-sectional, which precludes causal inference. Although the paper notes prior meta-analytic evidence of a very weak positive link, the primary source does not support the claim that higher BMI directly causes increased perceived stress (or that rising BMI inevitably increases stress)."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3428710/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review and studies focus on how chronic psychosocial stress can contribute to weight gain and obesity (stress → higher BMI) and discuss mechanisms and associations. They do not provide evidence that higher BMI directly causes increased perceived stress (BMI → perceived stress); the question of directionality is noted as unresolved but the sources do not support the claim that increasing BMI inevitably raises perceived stress."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/pcd/issues/2012/12_0001.htm", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited CDC study reports no association between BMI and perceived stress (predicted mean difference 0.31 kg/m2, 95% CI −0.31 to 0.97, P = .33) and explicitly states BMI was not associated with perceived stress in this sample. The study is also cross-sectional and does not support a causal claim that higher BMI directly increases perceived stress."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Higher body mass index (BMI) directly causes increased perceived stress (PSS) because excess body weight creates more physical discomfort and social challenges, which leads to higher stress levels. Therefore, as BMI increases, individuals inevitably feel more stressed.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Higher body mass index (BMI) directly causes increased perceived stress (PSS) because excess body weight creates more physical discomfort and social challenges, which leads to higher stress levels. Therefore, as BMI increases, individuals inevitably feel more stressed.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

