   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Body mass index (BMI) causes physical inactivity (SBQ) to increase through a direct causal mechanism. Higher BMI can lead to reduced mobility, discomfort during physical activity, or social/psychological barriers, making individuals less likely to be physically active. Conversely, lower BMI may reduce these barriers, leading to increased physical activity. This relationship is direct and does not require mediation by other variables in the given context.', 'session_id': '4a4eed24-8545-4e16-9f71-946b0734d107', 'prob_std': 0.26462176037668617, 'citations': ['https://www.cdc.gov/bmi/about/index.html', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/9464-body-mass-index-bmi', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11204233/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK594362/', 'https://www.cancer.org/cancer/risk-prevention/diet-physical-activity/body-weight-and-cancer-risk/body-mass-index-bmi-calculator.html'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7314105386962323, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/bmi/about/index.html", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The CDC source describes BMI as a screening measure and explicitly notes obesity is influenced by many factors (including health behaviors, stress, and medical conditions) and that physical activity and environmental/policy strategies matter. It does not provide evidence that higher BMI biologically reduces metabolic demands and thereby causally increases sedentary behavior independent of environmental or psychosocial influences; instead it emphasizes multifactorial causes and behavioral/environmental contributions, contradicting the asserted biologically driven, independent causal mechanism."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/9464-body-mass-index-bmi", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The Cleveland Clinic 