er BMI influences later prosocial behavior) nor test the mechanistic claim that any associations would be mediated by variables like mental health or social support. Therefore the citation only partially supports the motivation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6180707/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper examines associations between BMI and health‑related quality of life (physical and mental domains) and emphasizes cross‑sectional limits on causal inference and possible mediation by factors like mental health. It does not study prosocial behavior or PGG, so it does not provide evidence for or against a causal link between BMI and prosocial behavior."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://ideas.repec.org/p/pra/mprapa/68184.html", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited paper reports experimental evidence that BMI is unrelated to several measures of prosociality (altruism, fairness, trust, reciprocity) in dictator, ultimatum and trust games, which supports the claim that BMI is unlikely to directly affect social preference measures. However, the study does not report results from a public goods game (PGG) specifically, and it does not definitively establish the absence of any causal pathway (BMI was measured rather than experimentally manipulated). Therefore the citation supports the absence of a direct association across several social-preference games but does not fully justify the stronger, general causal statement about BMI and PGG or rule out mediated effects."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Body mass index (BMI) does not cause prosocial behavior (PGG) to change. There is no established direct causal mechanism by which BMI would directly influence prosocial behavior, nor does prosocial behavior causally affect BMI. Any observed association is likely med