177. Body mass index (BMI)-[NEGATIVE]->Sleep disturbance (PSQI)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Body mass index (BMI) causes sleep disturbance (PSQI) to increase through physiological mechanisms such as increased risk of sleep apnea and metabolic dysregulation. Higher BMI is causally linked to poorer sleep quality and more disturbances. Conversely, a lower BMI generally reduces these risks and thus decreases sleep disturbance. This relationship is direct and not fully explained by mediators in the provided CLD.', 'session_id': '4a4eed24-8545-4e16-9f71-946b0734d107', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9480680/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4700549/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10367528/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11979866/', 'https://www.ijcap.org/html-article/10915'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9480680/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study (Gupta et al. 2022) found that higher BMI is associated with worse sleep quality (higher PSQI scores) and shorter sleep duration — PSQI increased with BMI (mean PSQI and proportion with PSQI>5 rose across BMI categories; PSQI correlated positively with BMI, r≈0.48, p<0.01). This directly contradicts the motivation\'s claim that higher BMI leads to deeper, more restful sleep and fewer PSQI-measured disturbances."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4700549/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article examines associations between BMI and sleep duration (not PSQI-measured sleep disturbance) and generally reports that higher BMI is associated with shorter sleep (and that the sleep–BMI relationship varies by age). It does not provide evidence that higher BMI causes increased physical tiredness that leads to deeper, more restful sleep or fewer PSQI sleep disturbances. In fact, the paper and its referenced literature discuss mechanisms linking short sleep to increased BMI (opposite direction) and do not support the proposed pathway."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10367528/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited systematic review and pooled longitudinal studies report that higher BMI (BMI ≥25, overweight and especially obesity) is associated with increased risk of sleep disturbances (pooled ORs >1 for BMI≥25, obesity, and for outcomes such as obstructive sleep apnea and insomnia). The evidence therefore indicates more — not fewer — sleep problems with higher BMI; none of the cited sources support the proposed mechanism that higher BMI produces deeper, more restorative sleep and reduced PSQI disturbances."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11979866/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study reports that higher BMI (overweight/obese) is associated with worse sleep quality and greater sleep disturbance on the PSQI, not fewer sleep problems. The authors conclude increased BMI is an influential factor in poor sleep quality and discuss mechanisms linking short/poor sleep to higher BMI; nothing in the citation supports the claim that higher BMI produces deeper, more restful sleep or reduced PSQI disturbances."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.ijcap.org/html-article/10915", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study reports a highly significant positive correlation between BMI and PSQI score (higher BMI → worse sleep quality / more sleep disturbances) and a significant negative correlation between BMI and sleep duration. This directly contradicts the motivation that higher BMI leads to deeper, more restful sleep and fewer PSQI-measured disturbances."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'A higher body mass index (BMI) is associated with increased levels of physical tiredness, which leads to deeper and more restful sleep, thereby reducing sleep disturbances as measured by the PSQI. Therefore, individuals with higher BMI would experience fewer sleep problems compared to those with lower BMI.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'A higher body mass index (BMI) is associated with increased levels of physical tiredness, which leads to deeper and more restful sleep, thereby reducing sleep disturbances as measured by the PSQI. Therefore, individuals with higher BMI would experience fewer sleep problems compared to those with lower BMI.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

