   Properties: {'session_id': '4a4eed24-8545-4e16-9f71-946b0734d107', 'prob_std': 0.2803965289898636, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8967417/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5507106/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6260791/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4363366/', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23863401/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7550955203067437, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8967417/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review and references describe that obese adipose tissue shifts from anti-inflammatory (M2 macrophages, T-regs) toward pro-inflammatory cell types and mediators, and explicitly list IL-10 among anti-inflammatory cytokines produced in lean adipose and reduced anti-inflammatory activity in obesity. This supports an inverse association between higher adiposity/BMI and anti-inflammatory signalling (including IL-10) and provides mechanistic plausibility. However, the provided material presents mainly associative and mechanistic evidence; it does not present clear, direct experimental/longitudinal proof in these excerpts that increases in BMI causally reduce IL-10 (or that BMI reduction reproducibly raises IL-10). Therefore the causal negative claim is only partially supported by the cited content."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5507106/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review documents that obesity/BMI is associated with increased pro-inflammatory markers (e.g., IL‑6, TNF‑α) and reduced anti‑inflammatory adiponectin, but it does not report data or discussion specifically about IL‑10. The provided citations therefore do not support the claim that BMI causally reduces IL‑10."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6260791/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper characterizes IL-10\'s anti-inflammatory mechanisms and the consequences of IL-10/IL-10R deficiency (macrophage metabolism, mTOR/DDIT4 signaling, inflammasome activation, and IBD). It does not provide data or discussion linking body mass index, obesity, or adiposity to changes in IL-10 levels or showing that changes in BMI causally alter IL-10 production. Therefore the BMI → IL-10 causal claim is not supported by the provided citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4363366/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study (Schmidt et al., 2015) reports IL-10 levels were higher in obese and centrally obese participants and IL-10 correlated positively with BMI/adipometrics. The paper is cross-sectional and explicitly states it cannot establish causality; it also notes heterogeneous prior findings (some studies show increases, others decreases, with IL-10 changing after weight loss in some reports). Thus the provided citations do not support a causal inverse relationship (higher BMI → lower IL-10); they actually report the opposite association and do not provide causal evidence."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23863401/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper reports associations between higher BMI and earlier loss of response to infliximab in IBD patients. It does not present data on IL-10 levels or other anti-inflammatory cytokines, nor does it test the causal effect of BMI on IL-10. Therefore the citation does not support the stated BMI → IL-10 relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Body mass index (BMI) causes anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) to change in an inverse relationship. Increased BMI is causally linked to decreased IL-10 production, as higher adiposity