   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6) do not directly cause body mass index (BMI) to change. While BMI can influence proinflammatory markers, there is no established causal pathway where changes in proinflammatory processes directly alter BMI. Any association is likely mediated by other variables such as metabolic rate, physical activity, or adiposity, which are not direct effects.', 'session_id': '4a4eed24-8545-4e16-9f71-946b0734d107', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4360270/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4015473/', 'https://journals.physiology.org/doi/full/10.1152/ajpendo.2001.280.5.E745', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8967417/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4511728/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4360270/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited paper documents a clear positive association between higher IL-6 and TNF-α levels and obesity (higher levels in obese vs. normal-weight subjects, correlations with BMI and CRP). It also discusses mechanistic effects of these cytokines on adipose tissue and metabolism (e.g., TNF-α alters expression of adipocyte differentiation regulators, affects LPL, and promotes insulin resistance; IL-6 is linked to lipid abnormalities and free fatty acids). However, the article does not provide clear evidence that increased IL-6/TNF-α directly cause an increase in BMI by stimulating adipose growth. In fact, some mechanisms described (TNF-α inhibiting adipocyte differentiation and LPL) could limit adipose expansion, and the authors explicitly state it is difficult to determine whether increased cytokines are a cause or consequence of increased body fat. Therefore the citations support association and metabolic influence but do not support the specific causal claim that these cytokines directly stimulate adipose growth and raise BMI."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4015473/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited paper shows TNF-α and IL-6 are elevated in obesity and directly regulate adipocyte biology (they increase miR-146b expression and promoter activity), supporting a role for these cytokines in adipose tissue inflammation and altered adipocyte function. However, the sources do not provide direct evidence that increased TNF-α/IL-6 cause an increase in BMI by stimulating adipose tissue growth or by enhancing lipid storage/reducing metabolic rate. The article even cites work showing elevated proinflammatory cytokines can augment energy expenditure and reduce obesity risk, so the specific causal claim (proinflammatory cytokines → increased fat accumulation → higher BMI) is not established by the provided citations."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8967417/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review and sources consistently report that TNF-α and IL-6 are elevated in obese individuals and correlate with BMI (association), but they frame inflammation largely as a consequence or mediator of metabolic dysfunction rather than as a demonstrated driver of increased BMI. Moreover, the sources note that cytokines such as TNF-α and IL-1β can activate lipolysis (i.e., promote fat breakdown) and describe mechanisms whereby adipocyte hypertrophy and macrophage infiltration lead to increased cytokine production. The provided citations do not support the claim that higher TNF-α/IL-6 directly stimulate adipose growth and increase BMI; in places they point to the opposite metabolic effects."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4511728/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study shows a positive association between adipose-tissue IL-6 (and correlated TNF-α) expression and BMI—obese individuals have higher IL-6/IL-6R and TNF-α in adipose tissue and these measures correlate with BMI. However, the paper is observational/cross-sectional and does not demonstrate that elevated IL-6 or TNF-α causally increase BMI or stimulate adipose growth or reduce metabolic rate; the authors note the directionality and functional role (including possible anti-obesity effects of IL-6) remain controversial. Thus the citations support correlation but not the asserted direct causal mechanism."}], "aggregate_score": 0.375, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increased proinflammatory processes, specifically higher levels of TNF-alpha and IL-6, directly cause an increase in body mass index (BMI) by stimulating adipose tissue growth. These cytokines are thought to promote the accumulation of fat by enhancing lipid storage and reducing metabolic rate, thereby leading to weight gain.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.375, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increased proinflammatory processes, specifically higher levels of TNF-alpha and IL-6, directly cause an increase in body mass index (BMI) by stimulating adipose tissue growth. These cytokines are thought to promote the accumulation of fat by enhancing lipid storage and reducing metabolic rate, thereby leading to weight gain.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

