   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6) causally increase sleep disturbance (PSQI). Elevated levels of these cytokines disrupt sleep architecture and quality through direct effects on neural and endocrine pathways. Therefore, an increase in proinflammatory processes leads to greater sleep disturbance, while a decrease leads to less sleep disturbance.', 'session_id': '4a4eed24-8545-4e16-9f71-946b0734d107', 'prob_std': 0.2569701462389864, 'citations': ['https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0045610', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/npp2016148', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0889159104000959', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/19238807/?dopt=Abstract'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7651391735262935, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0045610", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited paper and references provide experimental evidence that sleep fragmentation/ deprivation elevates circulating and brain TNF-α (and that IL‑6 can rise with sleep loss), and that TNF‑α signaling causally influences sleep architecture and sleepiness in rodents (neutralization or receptor KO alters sleep responses). However, the citations do not document the claimed mechanism of \\"direct accumulation within neural synapses\\" or a localized synaptic cytokine buildup driving ongoing disturbance, nor do they link these rodent findings specifically to the human PSQI measure. Thus the bidirectional association and causal role of TNF‑α/IL‑6 in sleep disruption is supported in part, but the specific synaptic-accumulation mechanism and PSQI-specific claims are unaddressed."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/npp2016148", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The 