   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6) cause depressive symptoms (IDS-SR) in a direct causal relationship. Elevated levels of these proinflammatory cytokines can induce neurobiological changes that increase the likelihood or severity of depressive symptoms. Decreasing proinflammatory processes can correspondingly reduce depressive symptoms. This relationship is supported by mechanistic evidence linking inflammation to changes in mood and behavior.', 'session_id': '4a4eed24-8545-4e16-9f71-946b0734d107', 'citations': ['https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-025-85514-0', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3741070/', 'https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/ejn.15992', 'https://www.jneuropsychiatry.org/peer-review/depression-and-inflammation-disentangling-a-clear-yet-complex-and-multifaceted-link.html', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4881555/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-025-85514-0", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study describes peripheral IL-6 and TNF-α as markers linked to depression (reporting higher IL-6 in patients) and discusses inflammation as promoting susceptibility to depression and altering brain networks. It does not report that higher IL-6 or TNF-α reduce depressive symptoms or enhance neuronal repair/resilience; indeed the paper and prior meta-analyses cited characterize these cytokines as elevated in depression. (The study also used BDI-FS measures and resting-state FC outcomes, not IDS‑SR.) Therefore the claim that increased proinflammatory processes lead to reduced depressive symptoms is contradicted by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3741070/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review and supporting studies report that increased proinflammatory cytokines (including IL‑6 and TNF‑α) are associated with higher rates and severity of depressive symptoms and that exogenous or chronic cytokine exposure can induce depression. Mechanistic evidence cited indicates cytokines reduce BDNF and neurogenesis, activate neurotoxic kynurenine pathways, impair monoamine/dopamine synthesis, and promote neuroinflammation — effects opposite to “enhancing neuronal repair and resilience.” While the review notes cytokines have roles in normal brain development and can be adaptive acutely, the overall evidence presented contradicts the claim that higher proinflammatory processes reduce depressive symptoms."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4881555/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review and referenced studies report the opposite: elevated TNF-α (and IL‑6) are associated with increased depressive symptoms, administration of TNF‑α produces depressive‑like / “sickness” behaviors in animals, and TNF‑α antagonists can reduce depressive symptoms in models and some clinical settings. While the sources note physiological roles for TNF‑α (e.g., homeostatic synaptic scaling), they do not support the claim that higher proinflammatory cytokines suppress depression; the bulk of the evidence in the citations contradicts the proposed negative relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.jneuropsychiatry.org/peer-review/depression-and-inflammation-disentangling-a-clear-yet-complex-and-multifaceted-link.html", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review and meta-analyses report that higher levels of proinflammatory markers (IL‑6, TNF‑α, CRP, etc.) are associated with greater rates and severity of depression, that cytokine treatments (e.g., interferon‑α) can induce depressive symptoms, and that blocking cytokines (e.g., anti‑TNF agents) can improve depressive symptoms in some patients. While the review notes roles of cytokines in synaptic plasticity, the overall evidence in the citations points to proinflammatory processes promoting—rather than suppressing—depression. The motivation\'s claim that increased TNF‑α/IL‑6 enhance neuronal repair/resilience and reduce depressive symptoms is not supported by these sources."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Recent theories suggest that increased levels of proinflammatory cytokines like TNF-alpha and IL-6 actually suppress depressive symptoms by enhancing neuronal repair and resilience. Therefore, higher proinflammatory processes could lead to a reduction in depressive symptoms as measured by IDS-SR.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Recent theories suggest that increased levels of proinflammatory cytokines like TNF-alpha and IL-6 actually suppress depressive symptoms by enhancing neuronal repair and resilience. Therefore, higher proinflammatory processes could lead to a reduction in depressive symptoms as measured by IDS-SR.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

