   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) cause sleep disturbance (PSQI) to change in an inverse relationship. Increased IL-10, an anti-inflammatory cytokine, directly reduces inflammatory activity, which is known to improve sleep quality and decrease sleep disturbances. Conversely, decreased IL-10 can lead to increased inflammation and worsen sleep disturbance. The causal direction is from anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) to sleep disturbance (PSQI), not the reverse.', 'session_id': '4a4eed24-8545-4e16-9f71-946b0734d107', 'prob_std': 0.23830205780966512, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3410706/', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23490309/', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/npp2016148', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/immunology/articles/10.3389/fimmu.2023.1161067/full', 'https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s43440-021-00333-0'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7974773691267705, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3410706/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review comprehensively covers IL-10 biology, transcriptional regulation, and roles in infection, autoimmunity and inflammation (including some CNS inflammatory models), but it does not present evidence that IL-10 alters synaptic plasticity, neuronal excitability, sleep architecture, or patient-reported sleep disturbance (PSQI). The specific causal claims about IL-10 improving restorative sleep and an inverse IL-10 — PSQI relationship are not addressed in the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23490309/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited cross-sectional study reports an inverse association between circulating IL-10 levels and PSQI-measured poor sleep in maintenance hemodialysis patients (lower IL-10 linked to worse sleep; significant inverse correlation and multivariate association). However, the paper does not demonstrate causality or the proposed neurophysiological mechanisms (synaptic plasticity/neuronal excitability), nor establish directionality (cross-sectional design cannot rule out reverse causation or confounding). The finding is also limited to a specific patient population."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/npp2016148", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents that immune cytokines influence sleep and that modulating inflammatory signaling (e.g., blocking pro-inflammatory cytokines) can improve sleep, supporting a link from inflammatory biology to sleep disturbance. However, the paper emphasizes reciprocal (bidirectional) regulation of sleep and innate immunity rather than a strictly one-way causal direction, and it focuses largely on pro-inflammatory cytokines (IL-1, TNF, IL-6) and anti-inflammatory interventions broadly. It does not provide direct evidence that IL-10 specifically enhances synaptic plasticity/neural excitability to produce restorative sleep or that IL-10 levels alone causally and unidirectionally reduce PSQI-measured disturbance."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/immunology/articles/10.3389/fimmu.2023.1161067/full", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents that IL‑10 has actions in the central nervous system (neuroprotection, modulation of neuroinflammation, effects on neuron survival and neurogenesis) and that it can ameliorate some post‑infectious neurological sequelae — which provides partial biological plausibility for IL‑10 affecting brain function and potentially symptomatology. However, the sources do not provide direct evidence that elevated IL‑10 enhances synaptic plasticity and neuronal excitability in a way that improves sleep architecture, nor do they report data linking IL‑10 levels causally to reduced sleep disturbance measured by the PSQI or demonstrate the asserted primary causal direction (IL‑10 → sleep). Thus the specific mechanistic and outcome claims about sleep/PSQI are not addressed by the citations."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s43440-021-00333-0", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper reports associations between poor sleep (PSQI) and elevated proinflammatory cytokines (IL‑6, IL‑17, IL‑23) in IBD patients and did not measure or present data on IL‑10. It therefore does not provide evidence that IL‑10 levels inversely affect PSQI or that IL‑10 causally improves restorative sleep; the paper also describes a bidirectional relationship between sleep and inflammation rather than a demonstrated unidirectional causal effect from IL‑10 to sleep."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) cause sleep disturbance (PSQI) to change in an inverse relationship. Elevated IL-10 levels enhance synaptic plasticity and neuronal excitability in the central nervous system, which directly promotes restorative sleep architecture and reduces sleep disturbance, as measured by the PSQI. In contrast, reduced anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) impede these neurophysiological adaptations, leading to greater sleep disturbance. The primary causal direction is from anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) to sleep disturbance (PSQI), not the reverse.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.3, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) cause sleep disturbance (PSQI) to change in an inverse relationship. Elevated IL-10 levels enhance synaptic plasticity and neuronal excitability in the central nervous system, which directly promotes restorative sleep architecture and reduces sleep disturbance, as measured by the PSQI. In contrast, reduced anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) impede these neurophysiological adaptations, leading to greater sleep disturbance. The primary causal direction is from anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) to sleep disturbance (PSQI), not the reverse.', 'perplexity': 1.2506856921320668, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8566036078795134, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.11810028171057317, 'token_prob_slope': -0.00106416199388906, 'prob_variance': 0.056787870756320975, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0722967231994218, 'avg_nll': 0.22369195461927854}

